science/article/pii/S2667325826004255)\n  57. NASA probe brings back samples from asteroid Bennu, discovers diverse amino acids: International research group confirmed the discovery of \"Materials responsible for life\" (https://sj.jst.go.jp/news/202503/n0324-01k.html)\n  58. Phase Transformations of Glutamic Acid and Its Decomposition Products (https://pubs.acs.org/doi/abs/10.1021/cg901303a)\n  59. Thermal decomposition of the amino acids glycine, cysteine, aspartic acid, asparagine, glutamic acid, glutamine, arginine and histidine - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5807855/)\n  60. Phase Transformations of Glutamic Acid and Its Decomposition Products (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/231233025_Phase_Transformations_of_Glutamic_Acid_and_Its_Decomposition_Products)\n  61. The effects of temperature, pH and redox state on the stability of glutamic acid in hydrothermal fluids - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0016703714001537)\n  62. Enantiomeric Excesses of Acid Labile Amino Acid Precursors of the Murchison Meteorite - NASA Technical Reports Server (NTRS) (https://ntrs.nasa.gov/search.jsp?R=19990021044&hterms=pyroglutamic+acid&qs=N%3D0%26Ntk%3DAll%26Ntx%3Dmode%2Bmatchall%26Ntt%3Dpyroglutamic%2Bacid)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Glutamate\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Construct a connected, anoxic network containing 33 chemical-species nodes, 23 reaction nodes and 10 distinct synthesis pathways. Eight pathways combine two alternative reductions supplying alpha-ketoglutarate with four nitrogen-installation strategies; two additional pathways use aldehyde/nitrile chemistry. These are distinct reaction sequences, not ten independently demonstrated end-to-end syntheses. Experimentally supported transformations, proposed environmental adaptations and unresolved mechanistic closures are distinguished in the reaction reasoning. Initial inventories supply water, pyruvate, glyoxylate, ammonia, hydrogen, cyanide and the branch-specific small-molecule or mineral reagents. Their continuous geological supply is not established here; no unsupported direct CO2-to-glutamate reaction is assumed. Glycine can be generated within the network as a nitrogen-transfer reagent. Pathway sequences are ordered synthesis schedules and may include a separately prepared co-reactant. Organic acids are represented by neutral-parent structures for reaction accounting, followed by explicit conversion of glutamic acid to the glutamate monoanion. No enantioselection is assumed. Where a species occurs in several environments, environment_formed identifies one represented formation setting. The schema's intermediate role also covers coproducts and reservoirs. Reaction nodes represent net transformations rather than necessarily elementary steps. Competing hydroxyglutarate formation, reversible pyroglutamate storage and a qualified thermal-degradation branch are included. Productive chemistry avoids hot vent interiors and requires staged changes in pH, reductant availability or irradiation; integrated one-pot glutamate production must not be inferred.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Oxopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Pyruvic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Oxoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glyoxylic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6O6\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-Hydroxy-2-oxopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydroxyketoglutaric acid; maloylformate neutral parent\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H4O5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(3E)-2-Oxopent-3-enedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Fumaroylformate neutral parent\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6O5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Oxopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Alpha-ketoglutaric acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H6O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Ethanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Oxalic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2O4/c3-1(4)2(5)6/h(H,3,4)(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Aminopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Racemic glutamic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO4/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h3H,1-2,6H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeS\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Iron(II) sulfide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Fresh iron monosulfide; mackinawite-like material\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Sulfane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeS2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Iron disulfide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Pyrite\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Hydroxylamine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H7NO5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(Hydroxyimino)pentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Alpha-ketoglutarate oxime\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Iron\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Native iron\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"H+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Hydrogen(1+)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen ion; aqueous proton accounting species\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/p+1\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe2+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Iron(2+)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ferrous ion\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe/q+2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3HN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Prop-2-ynenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Cyanoacetylene\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3HN/c1-2-3-4/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H2N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2Z)-But-2-enedinitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Maleonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H4N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Butanedinitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Succinonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H4N2/c5-3-1-2-4-6/h1-2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_024\",\n      \"formula\": \"S8\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Cyclooctasulfur\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Elemental sulfur\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_025\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H5NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-Oxobutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"3-Cyanopropanal; beta-cyanopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_026\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Hydroxypentanedinitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"3-Cyanopropanal cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_027\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H7N3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Aminopentanedinitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glutamate/glutamine aminodinitrile precursor\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_028\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Prop-2-enal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Acrolein\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O/c1-2-3-4/h2-3H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_029\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8O5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Hydroxypentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Racemic 2-hydroxyglutaric acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_030\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H7NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-Oxopyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Racemic pyroglutamic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_031\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8NO4-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Azaniumylpentanedioate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Racemic glutamate monoanion\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_032\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H6O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Butanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Succinic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H6O4/c5-3(6)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,5,6)(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_033\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldol construction of the C5 hydroxyketoacid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved inorganic phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Phosphate general acid/base catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic concentrated pool; pH approximately 7; 0.5 M phosphate is the laboratory network benchmark, not an assumed ocean concentration; limit irradiation of accumulated ketoacids.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The pyruvate enol/enolate adds to the aldehyde carbon of glyoxylate, forming the C5 carbon skeleton without carbon loss.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The phosphate-mediated network contains maloylformate, fumaroylformate and alpha-ketoglutarate (Stubbs et al., 2020). This chemically required aldol step makes the skeleton from C2 and C3 feedstocks. The reported product distribution is a network result, not an isolated yield for this edge. Concentrated phosphate and sustained glyoxylate availability are substantial geological assumptions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydroxyketoacid dehydration to fumaroylformate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved inorganic phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Phosphate general acid/base catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic; pH approximately 7; same aqueous feeder module as rxn_001; competing reactions remain possible.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Elimination of water produces the conjugated unsaturated keto-dicarboxylate, represented by its fumaroylformate geometry.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Fumaroylformate is an identified intermediate in the metal-free alpha-ketoacid network (Stubbs et al., 2020). Its formation creates the activated double bond required by the two alternative downstream reductions. The approximately 6% fumaroylformate reported in the supplied research is a mixture composition, not this step's conversion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate-driven reduction supplying alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved inorganic phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Phosphate-mediated reaction network\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic; pH approximately 7; 0.5 M phosphate benchmark; approximately 21 h for the reported overall network; glyoxylate is consumed as reductant as well as carbon feedstock.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net reduction of the activated carbon-carbon double bond preserves the keto group. Oxidation of glyoxylate to oxalate supplies a formally balanced two-electron donor half-reaction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Alpha-ketoglutarate formation at approximately 5% in a glyoxylate-driven, metal-free network is supported by Stubbs et al. (2020). The supplied report does not establish quantitative oxalate recovery or an elementary hydride-transfer mechanism: the oxalate-producing closure is therefore a speculative redox-accounting model, not a verified exclusive mechanism. Low alpha-ketoglutarate accumulation is an explicit supply bottleneck.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-driven reduction supplying alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Supported metallic nickel\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2 laboratory benchmark\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni supported on silica-alumina\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, H2-retaining mineral-interface compartment; near-neutral to mildly alkaline solution; natural catalyst accessibility and hydrogen retention require local justification.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nickel activates molecular hydrogen and hydrogenates the activated alkene of fumaroylformate while retaining the alpha-keto group in the selected productive branch.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"H2/Ni promotes downstream reductive aldol-network chemistry, whereas the relevant reductions do not proceed without catalyst (Kaur et al., 2024). This node resolves that reported network into its chemically necessary alkene-reduction transformation; an isolated yield is not supplied. Supported laboratory nickel is not automatically equivalent to any natural nickel-bearing mineral, and competing carbonyl reduction limits selectivity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Nickel-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Supported metallic nickel\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni supported on silica-alumina\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic; pH approximately 8; benchmark uses 30 mM ketoacid and 150 mM ammonium bicarbonate for 72 h; ammonia node accounts for incorporated nitrogen; bicarbonate is a buffer rather than a carbon source here.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia forms an imine/iminium intermediate with the alpha-keto group; surface-activated hydrogen reduces it to the amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Conversion of separately supplied alpha-ketoglutarate to glutamate under H2 with nickel is experimentally supported (Kaur et al., 2024). Their reported yield ranges cover several substrates and amino/hydroxy-acid mixtures, not a glutamate-specific yield. Crucially, glutamate was not detectable in their integrated ketoacid-forming network. Slow alpha-ketoglutarate amination and rxn_018 therefore remain key bottlenecks (Mayer and Moran, 2022).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyrite-coupled iron-sulfide reductive amination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fresh FeS surface, consumed during pyrite-forming redox chemistry\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-348 K proposed warm-interface window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, sulfide-bearing sediment or geothermal-pool interface; mildly alkaline ammonia-bearing water; exact optimum for the selected pyrite-coupled glutamate branch is not established by the supplied report.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Oxidation of monosulfide sulfur during FeS2 formation supplies reducing equivalents for an alpha-ketoglutarate-derived imine. FeS and H2S are stoichiometric reactants, not an inexhaustible catalyst system.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pyrite-coupled reductive amination is supported by Hafenbradl et al. (1995); related fresh-FeS amino-acid synthesis is reported by Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003). The supplied report does not verify a specific approximately 50% glutamate yield for this exact formulation, so none is assigned. Fresh mineral reactivity, ammonia concentration, passivation and sulfide-mediated product loss require testing.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Alpha-ketoglutarate oxime formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic; mildly acidic to near-neutral screening conditions; hydroxylamine addition occurs after ketoacid formation, rather than being assumed compatible with the feeder reactions.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine condenses with the keto group to form an oxime, providing a more accessible nitrogen-bearing reduction substrate than an ammonia-derived imine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Adding hydroxylamine and Fe0 to an iron-promoted ketoacid network produced biological amino acids including glutamate (Muchowska et al., 2019). Resolving the nitrogen-installation sequence through an oxime is chemically justified, although the supplied report does not give a separate oxime yield. A sustained natural hydroxylamine source is uncertain, and its use cannot be treated as equivalent to abundant ammonia.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Native-iron reduction of the alpha-ketoglutarate oxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Native iron as a stoichiometric electron donor\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic; sufficient proton activity for corrosion and reduction; native iron may be locally meteoritic or produced in strongly reducing rocks; passivation competes.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Iron oxidation to Fe2+ supplies the four-electron reduction needed to convert the oxime to an amine with loss of its oxygen as water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a mechanistic resolution of the hydroxylamine/Fe0 amino-acid-producing treatment in Muchowska et al. (2019), rather than a separately quantified isolated reaction. Iron is explicitly consumed. The treatment must be staged because hydroxylamine and Fe0 can disrupt upstream Fe2+-promoted ketoacid chemistry, as discussed by Kaur et al. (2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycine-to-alpha-ketoglutarate transamination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-343 K proposed screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic; near-neutral to mildly alkaline solution; glycine-rich feed prepared separately; reducing glyoxylate activity would favor the forward direction; no enzyme, B6 cofactor or Al3+ catalyst is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Intermolecular imine formation, proton transfer and hydrolysis transfer the glycine amino group to alpha-ketoglutarate, regenerating glyoxylate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycine-mediated transamination of alpha-ketoacid analogues is discussed in Stubbs et al. (2020). A useful glutamate-specific flux under these proposed conditions is not established by the supplied evidence; this implementation is speculative. Reverse nitrogen transfer from glutamate is often thermodynamically favored (Mayer and Moran, 2022). The edge tests a nitrogen-transfer alternative but requires donor excess or product management and must not be described as a validated high-yield route.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Preparation of the glycine nitrogen donor\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Supported metallic nickel\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2 laboratory benchmark\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni supported on silica-alumina\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic; pH approximately 8; ammonium-bearing solution; perform in a separate aliquot before the proposed transamination stage.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reductive amination of glyoxylate generates glycine, which can subsequently donate nitrogen in rxn_009.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glyoxylate-to-glycine conversion is included in the H2/metal amino-acid chemistry of Kaur et al. (2024). Glyoxylate is intrinsically easier to reductively aminate than alpha-ketoglutarate (Mayer and Moran, 2022), providing a chemical motivation for testing a nitrogen-transfer relay. The full relay to glutamate, however, is not experimentally established and inherits the equilibrium limitation of rxn_009.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanoacetylene hydrocyanation to maleonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K proposed surface window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic; locally concentrated cyanide-bearing water; cyanide/HCN speciation must permit addition while limiting volatile HCN loss; the selected Z-isomer branch is represented.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net addition of HCN across the activated cyanoacetylene triple bond generates an unsaturated C4 dinitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) connect cyanoacetylene-derived maleonitrile to the glutamate-precursor branch. The supplied report does not specify a standalone yield, catalyst requirement or E/Z selectivity for this entry reaction; the uncatalyzed environmental implementation shown here is therefore conditional. This edge does not establish a continuous geological cyanoacetylene supply.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide-driven photoreduction of maleonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_024\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic UV-exposed shallow solution; 254 nm is the experimental irradiation benchmark; near-neutral sulfide-bearing water; H2S/HS- is a consumable photoreductant.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photogenerated reducing equivalents from sulfide saturate the dinitrile double bond. Elemental sulfur represents an idealized terminal sulfur-oxidation balance.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Maleonitrile-to-succinonitrile photoreduction is explicitly reported by Patel et al. (2015). Copper can accelerate related photoredox chemistry but is not imposed as a universally available catalyst here. S8 is a formal terminal sulfur product, not a claim that the experiment exclusively produced S8 rather than polysulfide mixtures. Local sulfide availability and realistic solar spectral efficiency remain constraints (Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Selective nitrile photoreduction to the C4 semialdehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_024\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic; continued UV exposure with available sulfide; stage irradiation to limit reduction of both nitrile groups and destruction of the aldehyde.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reduction of one nitrile gives an imine-level intermediate that hydrolyzes to 4-oxobutanenitrile and ammonia; the other nitrile remains intact.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Selective formation of the semialdehyde from succinonitrile is explicitly described by Patel et al. (2015). It addresses the crucial C4 aldehyde-supply bottleneck, but per-step yields were not verified in the supplied research. Sequential irradiation and reagent delivery are required; the S8 coproduct is again an idealized redox closure rather than an exclusive measured sulfur species.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin formation from 3-cyanopropanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_021\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_026\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic; staged HCN addition after photoreduction; near-neutral to mildly alkaline solution; limit further irradiation of the aldehyde inventory.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide addition to the aldehyde carbonyl followed by proton transfer generates the C5 cyanohydrin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) explicitly describe HCN addition to semialdehyde 52 to produce cyanohydrin 53. This addition installs the carbon that becomes the alpha-carboxyl group of glutamate. Competition from HCN volatilization, cyanide side chemistry and aldehyde degradation makes local concentration and staging necessary.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-dependent aminodinitrile formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_026\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_027\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic; ammonia-bearing concentrated solution; mildly alkaline conditions; separated from the preceding sulfide photoreduction stage where necessary.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible cyanohydrin dissociation, aldehyde-ammonia condensation and cyanide capture yield the alpha-aminodinitrile. This is not represented as direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Formation of aminonitrile 54 after ammonia addition is reported by Patel et al. (2015). It is a shared precursor of glutamine and glutamate, not evidence that either amino acid accumulates quantitatively in the same pot. Both nitrile groups must ultimately be hydrolyzed to obtain glutamate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Exhaustive aminodinitrile hydrolysis to glutamic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_027\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydrogen ions for acid-catalyzed hydrolysis\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-370 K proposed environmental window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic acidic geothermal pool; approximately pH 1-2 as a screening condition; prolonged residence may be required; subsequent cooling and neutralization are needed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Both nitrile groups hydrolyze through amide stages to carboxylic acids, releasing their nitrogen as ammonia/ammonium while retaining the alpha-amino group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The aminodinitrile is identified as a glutamate/glutamine precursor by Patel et al. (2015), and nitrile hydrolysis is established chemistry. Efficient exhaustive hydrolysis within this relatively mild natural window is not demonstrated by the supplied report and is explicitly speculative. Strong laboratory acid hydrolysis cannot be assumed to occur naturally; incomplete hydrolysis and pyroglutamate formation compete.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Proposed conjugate cyanide addition to acrolein\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_028\", \"mol_021\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_025\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K proposed screening range\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic; locally supplied acrolein and cyanide; near-neutral to mildly alkaline water; explicitly screen conjugate addition against carbonyl addition and polymerization.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proposed 1,4-addition of cyanide to acrolein extends the carbon skeleton while retaining an aldehyde group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Van Trump and Miller (1972) proposed acrolein as a precursor to glutamic acid, and Parker et al. (2011) discuss the underlying scenario. A prebiotic acrolein-to-glutamate yield was not established in the supplied research. This edge is speculative: competing 1,2-cyanohydrin formation and acrolein polymerization may prevent useful semialdehyde production.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing hydrogenation to 2-hydroxyglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_029\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Supported metallic nickel\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni supported on silica-alumina\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic; competes with rxn_005 in the same pH approximately 8 reaction mixture; ammonia does not eliminate carbonyl reduction.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrogenation of the keto group produces the alpha-hydroxy acid instead of installing nitrogen.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) report amino-acid/hydroxy-acid mixtures from ketoacids under H2 with nickel or meteoritic catalysts. Approximate panel-wide product ratios must not be assigned as an exact glutamate-specific branching ratio. This separate competing reaction prevents treating all consumed alpha-ketoglutarate as glutamate yield.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Glutamic acid cyclization to a pyroglutamate reservoir\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_030\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Acidic medium\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-370 K surface-reservoir window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic; acidic concentrated solutions, especially around pH 2-3.5; evaporation can promote net cyclization; no molecular oxygen is required.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Intramolecular attack of the amino group on the side-chain carboxyl group forms a five-membered lactam with loss of water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pyroglutamate formation is a documented glutamate sink and storage form (Lee et al., 2014; Wu et al., 2010; Weiss et al., 2018). Those studies include substantially hotter conditions and do not establish a rate throughout this surface window. The peptide-bound glutamate rate bound quoted in the research is not applied to free glutamate, and the uncertain food-chemistry rate is not used as a validated parameter.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_020\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic hydrolysis of the pyroglutamate reservoir\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_030\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydrogen ions\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-370 K proposed environmental window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic; strongly acidic, water-rich conditions; long residence may be necessary; cool and neutralize recovered glutamate to reduce renewed cyclization.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water addition and lactam C-N bond cleavage reopen the pyroglutamate ring to glutamic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Acid-labile meteoritic pyroglutamate can release glutamate during hydrolysis (Pizzarello and Cooper, 2001). This supports reservoir chemistry but not rapid recovery under natural surface conditions. The proposed environmental rate and conversion are speculative; harsh analytical hydrolysis must not be equated with an efficient prebiotic recycling process.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_021\",\n      \"name\": \"Formation of the aqueous glutamate monoanion\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_031\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic; buffered receiving solution at approximately pH 6-8; glutamate is predominantly the net-minus-one species, with two carboxylates and a protonated amino group.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-base equilibration converts the neutral glutamic-acid formula unit to the glutamate monoanion without changing its carbon skeleton or stereochemistry.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is established acid-base speciation, not a new synthetic claim. Explicit bookkeeping distinguishes the requested glutamate ion from the neutral-parent structures used elsewhere. The pH approximately 8 amino-acid-forming conditions in Kaur et al. (2024) are consistent with glutamate being predominantly anionic. No prebiotic enantiomeric enrichment is inferred.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_022\",\n      \"name\": \"Glutamate protonation in an acidic receiving compartment\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_031\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic; approximately pH 3-4; protonation makes the neutral-formula glutamic-acid pool accessible to the represented cyclization branch.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Protonation of a carboxylate shifts the acid-base distribution toward glutamic acid, predominantly its zwitterionic form in this pH interval.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This reverse speciation edge follows established acid-base chemistry and connects accumulated glutamate to the acid-sensitive reservoir chemistry. It is included for chemical bookkeeping rather than counted as an additional synthesis route. The relevance of pH to glutamate stability is supported by Lee et al. (2014).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_023\",\n      \"name\": \"Lumped irreversible high-temperature degradation branch\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_032\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_033\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"423-523 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"100-250 bar, maintaining liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic hot-fluid excursion; pH 6-10 experimental comparison range; minute-scale residence can matter; dissolved H2 suppresses some decomposition.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formal net closure of a succinate-producing degradation branch involving nitrogen loss and oxidative carbon-skeleton degradation; it is not assigned an elementary mechanism.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Lee et al. (2014) observed glutamate cyclization and decomposition yielding succinate, formate, CO2 and ammonia under hydrothermal conditions, with H2 suppressing degradation. This node is a speculative mass-balanced closure for only the succinate/CO2 fraction; its inferred hydrogen evolution and exact product stoichiometry are not a separately established experimental reaction. Formate-producing and other degradation channels remain unresolved. It is excluded from all productive pathways.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate-driven C5 assembly followed by nickel reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Assemble the C5 ketoacid through phosphate-mediated aldol chemistry and glyoxylate-driven reduction, then transfer the ketoacid fraction to an H2/Ni amination compartment. The feeder network and isolated-ketoacid amination are supported separately; the specific redox closure in rxn_003 and successful integrated glutamate accumulation remain unverified.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate-driven C5 assembly followed by pyrite-coupled amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Use the metal-free carbon-assembly module, then expose its alpha-ketoglutarate fraction to fresh FeS, sulfide and ammonia. This substitutes consumable sulfur/mineral redox chemistry for H2/Ni during nitrogen installation. Transfer compatibility, mineral passivation and glutamate survival are unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate-driven C5 assembly followed by oxime reduction\",\n      \"description\": \"After phosphate-mediated alpha-ketoglutarate formation, install nitrogen as an oxime and reduce it with native iron. This sequence avoids relying on direct ammonia-imine reduction but requires an uncertain hydroxylamine supply and consumes Fe0. The combination is a staged proposal, not a demonstrated one-pot process.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate-driven C5 assembly with a glycine nitrogen-transfer relay\",\n      \"description\": \"Prepare glycine in a separate H2/Ni aliquot, assemble alpha-ketoglutarate through the phosphate/glyoxylate branch, then test nitrogen transfer from glycine. The sequence includes both required feeder preparations. Forward glutamate-producing transamination is speculative and may require high glycine activity or removal of regenerated glyoxylate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction and nickel reductive amination\",\n      \"description\": \"After C5 aldol condensation and dehydration, use H2/Ni for alkene reduction and subsequently for reductive amination. Sharing a catalyst does not establish one-pot compatibility: Kaur et al. (2024) did not detect glutamate in their integrated network. Alpha-ketoglutarate isolation or enrichment and control of hydroxyglutarate formation remain necessary tests.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction followed by iron-sulfide amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Generate alpha-ketoglutarate using catalytic hydrogenation, then change to an ammonia-bearing FeS/sulfide interface for nitrogen installation. Sulfide exposure must be staged because it can poison upstream nickel surfaces. The pathway joins supported reaction classes without claiming demonstrated end-to-end operation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction followed by hydroxylamine and native iron\",\n      \"description\": \"Supply alpha-ketoglutarate through H2-driven alkene reduction, then form and reduce its oxime in a separate stage. This is chemically distinct from direct ammonia reductive amination but is contingent on hydroxylamine availability, accessible Fe0 and compatibility after the catalyst change.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction with a glycine nitrogen-transfer relay\",\n      \"description\": \"Prepare glycine separately, form the C5 ketoacid using H2/Ni reduction, then transfer nitrogen from glycine to alpha-ketoglutarate. This tests whether the greater reductive-amination reactivity of glyoxylate can be exploited indirectly. The final transamination and the full relay remain speculative.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanoacetylene-derived cyanosulfidic nitrile pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Build the C4 semialdehyde through maleonitrile and succinonitrile photochemistry, add cyanide to construct the C5 skeleton, install the amino group and hydrolyze both nitriles. Patel et al. (2015) support the explicit precursor sequence. Natural feedstock delivery, irradiation staging and exhaustive hydrolysis to free glutamate remain important gaps.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_023\", \"mol_025\", \"mol_026\", \"mol_027\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Proposed acrolein-entry Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Supply the same C4 semialdehyde through proposed conjugate cyanide addition to acrolein, then follow cyanohydrin formation, aminodinitrile formation and exhaustive hydrolysis. This route is explicitly hypothetical at its acrolein-entry step and additionally inherits the natural-hydrolysis limitation; it is not presented as a demonstrated Miller-type glutamate mechanism.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_017\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_026\", \"mol_027\", \"mol_010\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_010\",\n    \"mol_012\",\n    \"mol_017\",\n    \"mol_021\",\n    \"mol_025\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_018\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_025\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_020\", \"rxn_022\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_019\", \"rxn_021\", \"rxn_023\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_023\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_015\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Glutamate", "overall_strategy": "Construct a connected, anoxic network containing 33 chemical-species nodes, 23 reaction nodes and 10 distinct synthesis pathways. Eight pathways combine two alternative reductions supplying alpha-ketoglutarate with four nitrogen-installation strategies; two additional pathways use aldehyde/nitrile chemistry. These are distinct reaction sequences, not ten independently demonstrated end-to-end syntheses. Experimentally supported transformations, proposed environmental adaptations and unresolved mechanistic closures are distinguished in the reaction reasoning. Initial inventories supply water, pyruvate, glyoxylate, ammonia, hydrogen, cyanide and the branch-specific small-molecule or mineral reagents. Their continuous geological supply is not established here; no unsupported direct CO2-to-glutamate reaction is assumed. Glycine can be generated within the network as a nitrogen-transfer reagent. Pathway sequences are ordered synthesis schedules and may include a separately prepared co-reactant. Organic acids are represented by neutral-parent structures for reaction accounting, followed by explicit conversion of glutamic acid to the glutamate monoanion. No enantioselection is assumed. Where a species occurs in several environments, environment_formed identifies one represented formation setting. The schema's intermediate role also covers coproducts and reservoirs. Reaction nodes represent net transformations rather than necessarily elementary steps. Competing hydroxyglutarate formation, reversible pyroglutamate storage and a qualified thermal-degradation branch are included. Productive chemistry avoids hot vent interiors and requires staged changes in pH, reductant availability or irradiation; integrated one-pot glutamate production must not be inferred.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Oxidane", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-Oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "Pyruvic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "C2H2O3", "iupac_name": "2-Oxoacetic acid", "common_name": "Glyoxylic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "C5H6O6", "iupac_name": "4-Hydroxy-2-oxopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "Hydroxyketoglutaric acid; maloylformate neutral parent", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C5H4O5", "iupac_name": "(3E)-2-Oxopent-3-enedioic acid", "common_name": "Fumaroylformate neutral parent", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C5H6O5", "iupac_name": "2-Oxopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "Alpha-ketoglutaric acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H6O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C2H2O4", "iupac_name": "Ethanedioic acid", "common_name": "Oxalic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O4/c3-1(4)2(5)6/h(H,3,4)(H,5,6)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "Dihydrogen", "common_name": "Hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "Azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C5H9NO4", "iupac_name": "2-Aminopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "Racemic glutamic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO4/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h3H,1-2,6H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "FeS", "iupac_name": "Iron(II) sulfide", "common_name": "Fresh iron monosulfide; mackinawite-like material", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "Sulfane", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "FeS2", "iupac_name": "Iron disulfide", "common_name": "Pyrite", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "H3NO", "iupac_name": "Hydroxylamine", "common_name": "Hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H7NO5", "iupac_name": "2-(Hydroxyimino)pentanedioic acid", "common_name": "Alpha-ketoglutarate oxime", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "Iron", "common_name": "Native iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen(1+)", "common_name": "Hydrogen ion; aqueous proton accounting species", "inchi": "InChI=1S/p+1", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "Fe2+", "iupac_name": "Iron(2+)", "common_name": "Ferrous ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2-Aminoacetic acid", "common_name": "Glycine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C3HN", "iupac_name": "Prop-2-ynenitrile", "common_name": "Cyanoacetylene", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3HN/c1-2-3-4/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "C4H2N2", "iupac_name": "(2Z)-But-2-enedinitrile", "common_name": "Maleonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "C4H4N2", "iupac_name": "Butanedinitrile", "common_name": "Succinonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H4N2/c5-3-1-2-4-6/h1-2H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_024", "formula": "S8", "iupac_name": "Cyclooctasulfur", "common_name": "Elemental sulfur", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_025", "formula": "C4H5NO", "iupac_name": "4-Oxobutanenitrile", "common_name": "3-Cyanopropanal; beta-cyanopropionaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_026", "formula": "C5H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-Hydroxypentanedinitrile", "common_name": "3-Cyanopropanal cyanohydrin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_027", "formula": "C5H7N3", "iupac_name": "2-Aminopentanedinitrile", "common_name": "Glutamate/glutamine aminodinitrile precursor", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_028", "formula": "C3H4O", "iupac_name": "Prop-2-enal", "common_name": "Acrolein", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O/c1-2-3-4/h2-3H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_029", "formula": "C5H8O5", "iupac_name": "2-Hydroxypentanedioic acid", "common_name": "Racemic 2-hydroxyglutaric acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_030", "formula": "C5H7NO3", "iupac_name": "5-Oxopyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "Racemic pyroglutamic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_031", "formula": "C5H8NO4-", "iupac_name": "2-Azaniumylpentanedioate", "common_name": "Racemic glutamate monoanion", "inchi": null, "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_032", "formula": "C4H6O4", "iupac_name": "Butanedioic acid", "common_name": "Succinic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H6O4/c5-3(6)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,5,6)(H,7,8)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_033", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "Carbon dioxide", "common_name": "Carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Aldol construction of the C5 hydroxyketoacid", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved inorganic phosphate"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Phosphate general acid/base catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic concentrated pool; pH approximately 7; 0.5 M phosphate is the laboratory network benchmark, not an assumed ocean concentration; limit irradiation of accumulated ketoacids."}, "mechanism": "The pyruvate enol/enolate adds to the aldehyde carbon of glyoxylate, forming the C5 carbon skeleton without carbon loss.", "reasoning": "The phosphate-mediated network contains maloylformate, fumaroylformate and alpha-ketoglutarate (Stubbs et al., 2020). This chemically required aldol step makes the skeleton from C2 and C3 feedstocks. The reported product distribution is a network result, not an isolated yield for this edge. Concentrated phosphate and sustained glyoxylate availability are substantial geological assumptions."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Hydroxyketoacid dehydration to fumaroylformate", "inputs": ["mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_005", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved inorganic phosphate"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Phosphate general acid/base catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic; pH approximately 7; same aqueous feeder module as rxn_001; competing reactions remain possible."}, "mechanism": "Elimination of water produces the conjugated unsaturated keto-dicarboxylate, represented by its fumaroylformate geometry.", "reasoning": "Fumaroylformate is an identified intermediate in the metal-free alpha-ketoacid network (Stubbs et al., 2020). Its formation creates the activated double bond required by the two alternative downstream reductions. The approximately 6% fumaroylformate reported in the supplied research is a mixture composition, not this step's conversion."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Glyoxylate-driven reduction supplying alpha-ketoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_003", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved inorganic phosphate"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Phosphate-mediated reaction network", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic; pH approximately 7; 0.5 M phosphate benchmark; approximately 21 h for the reported overall network; glyoxylate is consumed as reductant as well as carbon feedstock."}, "mechanism": "Net reduction of the activated carbon-carbon double bond preserves the keto group. Oxidation of glyoxylate to oxalate supplies a formally balanced two-electron donor half-reaction.", "reasoning": "Alpha-ketoglutarate formation at approximately 5% in a glyoxylate-driven, metal-free network is supported by Stubbs et al. (2020). The supplied report does not establish quantitative oxalate recovery or an elementary hydride-transfer mechanism: the oxalate-producing closure is therefore a speculative redox-accounting model, not a verified exclusive mechanism. Low alpha-ketoglutarate accumulation is an explicit supply bottleneck."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Hydrogen-driven reduction supplying alpha-ketoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Supported metallic nickel"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2 laboratory benchmark", "catalyst": "Ni supported on silica-alumina", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, H2-retaining mineral-interface compartment; near-neutral to mildly alkaline solution; natural catalyst accessibility and hydrogen retention require local justification."}, "mechanism": "Nickel activates molecular hydrogen and hydrogenates the activated alkene of fumaroylformate while retaining the alpha-keto group in the selected productive branch.", "reasoning": "H2/Ni promotes downstream reductive aldol-network chemistry, whereas the relevant reductions do not proceed without catalyst (Kaur et al., 2024). This node resolves that reported network into its chemically necessary alkene-reduction transformation; an isolated yield is not supplied. Supported laboratory nickel is not automatically equivalent to any natural nickel-bearing mineral, and competing carbonyl reduction limits selectivity."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Nickel-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_009", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Supported metallic nickel"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni supported on silica-alumina", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic; pH approximately 8; benchmark uses 30 mM ketoacid and 150 mM ammonium bicarbonate for 72 h; ammonia node accounts for incorporated nitrogen; bicarbonate is a buffer rather than a carbon source here."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia forms an imine/iminium intermediate with the alpha-keto group; surface-activated hydrogen reduces it to the amino acid.", "reasoning": "Conversion of separately supplied alpha-ketoglutarate to glutamate under H2 with nickel is experimentally supported (Kaur et al., 2024). Their reported yield ranges cover several substrates and amino/hydroxy-acid mixtures, not a glutamate-specific yield. Crucially, glutamate was not detectable in their integrated ketoacid-forming network. Slow alpha-ketoglutarate amination and rxn_018 therefore remain key bottlenecks (Mayer and Moran, 2022)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Pyrite-coupled iron-sulfide reductive amination", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_009", "mol_011", "mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001", "mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Fresh FeS surface, consumed during pyrite-forming redox chemistry"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-348 K proposed warm-interface window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, sulfide-bearing sediment or geothermal-pool interface; mildly alkaline ammonia-bearing water; exact optimum for the selected pyrite-coupled glutamate branch is not established by the supplied report."}, "mechanism": "Oxidation of monosulfide sulfur during FeS2 formation supplies reducing equivalents for an alpha-ketoglutarate-derived imine. FeS and H2S are stoichiometric reactants, not an inexhaustible catalyst system.", "reasoning": "Pyrite-coupled reductive amination is supported by Hafenbradl et al. (1995); related fresh-FeS amino-acid synthesis is reported by Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003). The supplied report does not verify a specific approximately 50% glutamate yield for this exact formulation, so none is assigned. Fresh mineral reactivity, ammonia concentration, passivation and sulfide-mediated product loss require testing."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Alpha-ketoglutarate oxime formation", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "323-343 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic; mildly acidic to near-neutral screening conditions; hydroxylamine addition occurs after ketoacid formation, rather than being assumed compatible with the feeder reactions."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxylamine condenses with the keto group to form an oxime, providing a more accessible nitrogen-bearing reduction substrate than an ammonia-derived imine.", "reasoning": "Adding hydroxylamine and Fe0 to an iron-promoted ketoacid network produced biological amino acids including glutamate (Muchowska et al., 2019). Resolving the nitrogen-installation sequence through an oxime is chemically justified, although the supplied report does not give a separate oxime yield. A sustained natural hydroxylamine source is uncertain, and its use cannot be treated as equivalent to abundant ammonia."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Native-iron reduction of the alpha-ketoglutarate oxime", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_016", "mol_017"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_018", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Native iron as a stoichiometric electron donor"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-343 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic; sufficient proton activity for corrosion and reduction; native iron may be locally meteoritic or produced in strongly reducing rocks; passivation competes."}, "mechanism": "Iron oxidation to Fe2+ supplies the four-electron reduction needed to convert the oxime to an amine with loss of its oxygen as water.", "reasoning": "This is a mechanistic resolution of the hydroxylamine/Fe0 amino-acid-producing treatment in Muchowska et al. (2019), rather than a separately quantified isolated reaction. Iron is explicitly consumed. The treatment must be staged because hydroxylamine and Fe0 can disrupt upstream Fe2+-promoted ketoacid chemistry, as discussed by Kaur et al. (2024)."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Glycine-to-alpha-ketoglutarate transamination", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_019"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "323-343 K proposed screening range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic; near-neutral to mildly alkaline solution; glycine-rich feed prepared separately; reducing glyoxylate activity would favor the forward direction; no enzyme, B6 cofactor or Al3+ catalyst is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Intermolecular imine formation, proton transfer and hydrolysis transfer the glycine amino group to alpha-ketoglutarate, regenerating glyoxylate.", "reasoning": "Glycine-mediated transamination of alpha-ketoacid analogues is discussed in Stubbs et al. (2020). A useful glutamate-specific flux under these proposed conditions is not established by the supplied evidence; this implementation is speculative. Reverse nitrogen transfer from glutamate is often thermodynamically favored (Mayer and Moran, 2022). The edge tests a nitrogen-transfer alternative but requires donor excess or product management and must not be described as a validated high-yield route."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Preparation of the glycine nitrogen donor", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_009", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_019", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Supported metallic nickel"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2 laboratory benchmark", "catalyst": "Ni supported on silica-alumina", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic; pH approximately 8; ammonium-bearing solution; perform in a separate aliquot before the proposed transamination stage."}, "mechanism": "Reductive amination of glyoxylate generates glycine, which can subsequently donate nitrogen in rxn_009.", "reasoning": "Glyoxylate-to-glycine conversion is included in the H2/metal amino-acid chemistry of Kaur et al. (2024). Glyoxylate is intrinsically easier to reductively aminate than alpha-ketoglutarate (Mayer and Moran, 2022), providing a chemical motivation for testing a nitrogen-transfer relay. The full relay to glutamate, however, is not experimentally established and inherits the equilibrium limitation of rxn_009."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Cyanoacetylene hydrocyanation to maleonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_021"], "outputs": ["mol_022"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K proposed surface window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic; locally concentrated cyanide-bearing water; cyanide/HCN speciation must permit addition while limiting volatile HCN loss; the selected Z-isomer branch is represented."}, "mechanism": "Net addition of HCN across the activated cyanoacetylene triple bond generates an unsaturated C4 dinitrile.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) connect cyanoacetylene-derived maleonitrile to the glutamate-precursor branch. The supplied report does not specify a standalone yield, catalyst requirement or E/Z selectivity for this entry reaction; the uncatalyzed environmental implementation shown here is therefore conditional. This edge does not establish a continuous geological cyanoacetylene supply."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Sulfide-driven photoreduction of maleonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_022", "mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_023", "mol_024"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic UV-exposed shallow solution; 254 nm is the experimental irradiation benchmark; near-neutral sulfide-bearing water; H2S/HS- is a consumable photoreductant."}, "mechanism": "Photogenerated reducing equivalents from sulfide saturate the dinitrile double bond. Elemental sulfur represents an idealized terminal sulfur-oxidation balance.", "reasoning": "Maleonitrile-to-succinonitrile photoreduction is explicitly reported by Patel et al. (2015). Copper can accelerate related photoredox chemistry but is not imposed as a universally available catalyst here. S8 is a formal terminal sulfur product, not a claim that the experiment exclusively produced S8 rather than polysulfide mixtures. Local sulfide availability and realistic solar spectral efficiency remain constraints (Ranjan et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Selective nitrile photoreduction to the C4 semialdehyde", "inputs": ["mol_023", "mol_012", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_025", "mol_009", "mol_024"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic; continued UV exposure with available sulfide; stage irradiation to limit reduction of both nitrile groups and destruction of the aldehyde."}, "mechanism": "Reduction of one nitrile gives an imine-level intermediate that hydrolyzes to 4-oxobutanenitrile and ammonia; the other nitrile remains intact.", "reasoning": "Selective formation of the semialdehyde from succinonitrile is explicitly described by Patel et al. (2015). It addresses the crucial C4 aldehyde-supply bottleneck, but per-step yields were not verified in the supplied research. Sequential irradiation and reagent delivery are required; the S8 coproduct is again an idealized redox closure rather than an exclusive measured sulfur species."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Cyanohydrin formation from 3-cyanopropanal", "inputs": ["mol_025", "mol_021"], "outputs": ["mol_026"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic; staged HCN addition after photoreduction; near-neutral to mildly alkaline solution; limit further irradiation of the aldehyde inventory."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide addition to the aldehyde carbonyl followed by proton transfer generates the C5 cyanohydrin.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) explicitly describe HCN addition to semialdehyde 52 to produce cyanohydrin 53. This addition installs the carbon that becomes the alpha-carboxyl group of glutamate. Competition from HCN volatilization, cyanide side chemistry and aldehyde degradation makes local concentration and staging necessary."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Ammonia-dependent aminodinitrile formation", "inputs": ["mol_026", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_027", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic; ammonia-bearing concentrated solution; mildly alkaline conditions; separated from the preceding sulfide photoreduction stage where necessary."}, "mechanism": "Reversible cyanohydrin dissociation, aldehyde-ammonia condensation and cyanide capture yield the alpha-aminodinitrile. This is not represented as direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group.", "reasoning": "Formation of aminonitrile 54 after ammonia addition is reported by Patel et al. (2015). It is a shared precursor of glutamine and glutamate, not evidence that either amino acid accumulates quantitatively in the same pot. Both nitrile groups must ultimately be hydrolyzed to obtain glutamate."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Exhaustive aminodinitrile hydrolysis to glutamic acid", "inputs": ["mol_027", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydrogen ions for acid-catalyzed hydrolysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-370 K proposed environmental window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic acidic geothermal pool; approximately pH 1-2 as a screening condition; prolonged residence may be required; subsequent cooling and neutralization are needed."}, "mechanism": "Both nitrile groups hydrolyze through amide stages to carboxylic acids, releasing their nitrogen as ammonia/ammonium while retaining the alpha-amino group.", "reasoning": "The aminodinitrile is identified as a glutamate/glutamine precursor by Patel et al. (2015), and nitrile hydrolysis is established chemistry. Efficient exhaustive hydrolysis within this relatively mild natural window is not demonstrated by the supplied report and is explicitly speculative. Strong laboratory acid hydrolysis cannot be assumed to occur naturally; incomplete hydrolysis and pyroglutamate formation compete."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Proposed conjugate cyanide addition to acrolein", "inputs": ["mol_028", "mol_021"], "outputs": ["mol_025"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K proposed screening range", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic; locally supplied acrolein and cyanide; near-neutral to mildly alkaline water; explicitly screen conjugate addition against carbonyl addition and polymerization."}, "mechanism": "Proposed 1,4-addition of cyanide to acrolein extends the carbon skeleton while retaining an aldehyde group.", "reasoning": "Van Trump and Miller (1972) proposed acrolein as a precursor to glutamic acid, and Parker et al. (2011) discuss the underlying scenario. A prebiotic acrolein-to-glutamate yield was not established in the supplied research. This edge is speculative: competing 1,2-cyanohydrin formation and acrolein polymerization may prevent useful semialdehyde production."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Competing hydrogenation to 2-hydroxyglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_029"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Supported metallic nickel"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni supported on silica-alumina", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic; competes with rxn_005 in the same pH approximately 8 reaction mixture; ammonia does not eliminate carbonyl reduction."}, "mechanism": "Hydrogenation of the keto group produces the alpha-hydroxy acid instead of installing nitrogen.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) report amino-acid/hydroxy-acid mixtures from ketoacids under H2 with nickel or meteoritic catalysts. Approximate panel-wide product ratios must not be assigned as an exact glutamate-specific branching ratio. This separate competing reaction prevents treating all consumed alpha-ketoglutarate as glutamate yield."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "Glutamic acid cyclization to a pyroglutamate reservoir", "inputs": ["mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_030", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Acidic medium"], "conditions": {"temperature": "333-370 K surface-reservoir window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic; acidic concentrated solutions, especially around pH 2-3.5; evaporation can promote net cyclization; no molecular oxygen is required."}, "mechanism": "Intramolecular attack of the amino group on the side-chain carboxyl group forms a five-membered lactam with loss of water.", "reasoning": "Pyroglutamate formation is a documented glutamate sink and storage form (Lee et al., 2014; Wu et al., 2010; Weiss et al., 2018). Those studies include substantially hotter conditions and do not establish a rate throughout this surface window. The peptide-bound glutamate rate bound quoted in the research is not applied to free glutamate, and the uncertain food-chemistry rate is not used as a validated parameter."}, {"id": "rxn_020", "name": "Acidic hydrolysis of the pyroglutamate reservoir", "inputs": ["mol_030", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydrogen ions"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-370 K proposed environmental window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic; strongly acidic, water-rich conditions; long residence may be necessary; cool and neutralize recovered glutamate to reduce renewed cyclization."}, "mechanism": "Water addition and lactam C-N bond cleavage reopen the pyroglutamate ring to glutamic acid.", "reasoning": "Acid-labile meteoritic pyroglutamate can release glutamate during hydrolysis (Pizzarello and Cooper, 2001). This supports reservoir chemistry but not rapid recovery under natural surface conditions. The proposed environmental rate and conversion are speculative; harsh analytical hydrolysis must not be equated with an efficient prebiotic recycling process."}, {"id": "rxn_021", "name": "Formation of the aqueous glutamate monoanion", "inputs": ["mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_031", "mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic; buffered receiving solution at approximately pH 6-8; glutamate is predominantly the net-minus-one species, with two carboxylates and a protonated amino group."}, "mechanism": "Acid-base equilibration converts the neutral glutamic-acid formula unit to the glutamate monoanion without changing its carbon skeleton or stereochemistry.", "reasoning": "This is established acid-base speciation, not a new synthetic claim. Explicit bookkeeping distinguishes the requested glutamate ion from the neutral-parent structures used elsewhere. The pH approximately 8 amino-acid-forming conditions in Kaur et al. (2024) are consistent with glutamate being predominantly anionic. No prebiotic enantiomeric enrichment is inferred."}, {"id": "rxn_022", "name": "Glutamate protonation in an acidic receiving compartment", "inputs": ["mol_031", "mol_017"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic; approximately pH 3-4; protonation makes the neutral-formula glutamic-acid pool accessible to the represented cyclization branch."}, "mechanism": "Protonation of a carboxylate shifts the acid-base distribution toward glutamic acid, predominantly its zwitterionic form in this pH interval.", "reasoning": "This reverse speciation edge follows established acid-base chemistry and connects accumulated glutamate to the acid-sensitive reservoir chemistry. It is included for chemical bookkeeping rather than counted as an additional synthesis route. The relevance of pH to glutamate stability is supported by Lee et al. (2014)."}, {"id": "rxn_023", "name": "Lumped irreversible high-temperature degradation branch", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_032", "mol_009", "mol_033", "mol_008"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "423-523 K", "pressure": "100-250 bar, maintaining liquid water", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic hot-fluid excursion; pH 6-10 experimental comparison range; minute-scale residence can matter; dissolved H2 suppresses some decomposition."}, "mechanism": "Formal net closure of a succinate-producing degradation branch involving nitrogen loss and oxidative carbon-skeleton degradation; it is not assigned an elementary mechanism.", "reasoning": "Lee et al. (2014) observed glutamate cyclization and decomposition yielding succinate, formate, CO2 and ammonia under hydrothermal conditions, with H2 suppressing degradation. This node is a speculative mass-balanced closure for only the succinate/CO2 fraction; its inferred hydrogen evolution and exact product stoichiometry are not a separately established experimental reaction. Formate-producing and other degradation channels remain unresolved. It is excluded from all productive pathways."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Glyoxylate-driven C5 assembly followed by nickel reductive amination", "description": "Assemble the C5 ketoacid through phosphate-mediated aldol chemistry and glyoxylate-driven reduction, then transfer the ketoacid fraction to an H2/Ni amination compartment. The feeder network and isolated-ketoacid amination are supported separately; the specific redox closure in rxn_003 and successful integrated glutamate accumulation remain unverified.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_004", "mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Glyoxylate-driven C5 assembly followed by pyrite-coupled amination", "description": "Use the metal-free carbon-assembly module, then expose its alpha-ketoglutarate fraction to fresh FeS, sulfide and ammonia. This substitutes consumable sulfur/mineral redox chemistry for H2/Ni during nitrogen installation. Transfer compatibility, mineral passivation and glutamate survival are unresolved.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_006", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Glyoxylate-driven C5 assembly followed by oxime reduction", "description": "After phosphate-mediated alpha-ketoglutarate formation, install nitrogen as an oxime and reduce it with native iron. This sequence avoids relying on direct ammonia-imine reduction but requires an uncertain hydroxylamine supply and consumes Fe0. The combination is a staged proposal, not a demonstrated one-pot process.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_015", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Glyoxylate-driven C5 assembly with a glycine nitrogen-transfer relay", "description": "Prepare glycine in a separate H2/Ni aliquot, assemble alpha-ketoglutarate through the phosphate/glyoxylate branch, then test nitrogen transfer from glycine. The sequence includes both required feeder preparations. Forward glutamate-producing transamination is speculative and may require high glycine activity or removal of regenerated glyoxylate.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_010", "rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_009", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_019", "mol_006", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction and nickel reductive amination", "description": "After C5 aldol condensation and dehydration, use H2/Ni for alkene reduction and subsequently for reductive amination. Sharing a catalyst does not establish one-pot compatibility: Kaur et al. (2024) did not detect glutamate in their integrated network. Alpha-ketoglutarate isolation or enrichment and control of hydroxyglutarate formation remain necessary tests.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction followed by iron-sulfide amination", "description": "Generate alpha-ketoglutarate using catalytic hydrogenation, then change to an ammonia-bearing FeS/sulfide interface for nitrogen installation. Sulfide exposure must be staged because it can poison upstream nickel surfaces. The pathway joins supported reaction classes without claiming demonstrated end-to-end operation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction followed by hydroxylamine and native iron", "description": "Supply alpha-ketoglutarate through H2-driven alkene reduction, then form and reduce its oxime in a separate stage. This is chemically distinct from direct ammonia reductive amination but is contingent on hydroxylamine availability, accessible Fe0 and compatibility after the catalyst change.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_015", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction with a glycine nitrogen-transfer relay", "description": "Prepare glycine separately, form the C5 ketoacid using H2/Ni reduction, then transfer nitrogen from glycine to alpha-ketoglutarate. This tests whether the greater reductive-amination reactivity of glyoxylate can be exploited indirectly. The final transamination and the full relay remain speculative.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_010", "rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_009", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_019", "mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Cyanoacetylene-derived cyanosulfidic nitrile pathway", "description": "Build the C4 semialdehyde through maleonitrile and succinonitrile photochemistry, add cyanide to construct the C5 skeleton, install the amino group and hydrolyze both nitriles. Patel et al. (2015) support the explicit precursor sequence. Natural feedstock delivery, irradiation staging and exhaustive hydrolysis to free glutamate remain important gaps.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_013", "rxn_014", "rxn_015", "rxn_016", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_022", "mol_023", "mol_025", "mol_026", "mol_027", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Proposed acrolein-entry Strecker pathway", "description": "Supply the same C4 semialdehyde through proposed conjugate cyanide addition to acrolein, then follow cyanohydrin formation, aminodinitrile formation and exhaustive hydrolysis. This route is explicitly hypothetical at its acrolein-entry step and additionally inherits the natural-hydrolysis limitation; it is not presented as a demonstrated Miller-type glutamate mechanism.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_017", "rxn_014", "rxn_015", "rxn_016", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_025", "mol_026", "mol_027", "mol_010"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_001", "mol_003", "mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010", "mol_012", "mol_017", "mol_021", "mol_025"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_006", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_018"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_025", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_013", "rxn_017"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_014"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_010", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_016", "rxn_020", "rxn_022"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_019", "rxn_021", "rxn_023"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_009", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_013", "rxn_016", "rxn_023"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_010", "rxn_015"]}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 479270.22099494934, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-18T20:17:30.202507", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Glutamate\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct a connected, anoxic network containing 33 chemical-species nodes, 23 reaction nodes and 10 distinct synthesis pathways. Eight pathways combine two alternative reductions supplying alpha-ketoglutarate with four nitrogen-installation strategies; two additional pathways use aldehyde/nitrile chemistry. These are distinct reaction sequences, not ten independently demonstrated end-to-end syntheses. Experimentally supported transformations, proposed environmental adaptations and unresolved mechanistic closures are distinguished in the reaction reasoning. Initial inventories supply water, pyruvate, glyoxylate, ammonia, hydrogen, cyanide and the branch-specific small-molecule or mineral reagents. Their continuous geological supply is not established here; no unsupported direct CO2-to-glutamate reaction is assumed. Glycine can be generated within the network as a nitrogen-transfer reagent. Pathway sequences are ordered synthesis schedules and may include a separately prepared co-reactant. Organic acids are represented by neutral-parent structures for reaction accounting, followed by explicit conversion of glutamic acid to the glutamate monoanion. No enantioselection is assumed. Where a species occurs in several environments, environment_formed identifies one represented formation setting. The schema's intermediate role also covers coproducts and reservoirs. Reaction nodes represent net transformations rather than necessarily elementary steps. Competing hydroxyglutarate formation, reversible pyroglutamate storage and a qualified thermal-degradation branch are included. Productive chemistry avoids hot vent interiors and requires staged changes in pH, reductant availability or irradiation; integrated one-pot glutamate production must not be inferred.\n\nMOLECULES (33 nodes):\n  - mol_001: H2O (Water) [starting]\n  - mol_002: C3H4O3 (Pyruvic acid) [starting]\n  - mol_003: C2H2O3 (Glyoxylic acid) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_004: C5H6O6 (Hydroxyketoglutaric acid; maloylformate neutral parent) [intermediate]\n  - mol_005: C5H4O5 (Fumaroylformate neutral parent) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_006: C5H6O5 (Alpha-ketoglutaric acid) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_007: C2H2O4 (Oxalic acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_008: H2 (Hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_009: H3N (Ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_010: C5H9NO4 (Racemic glutamic acid) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_011: FeS (Fresh iron monosulfide; mackinawite-like material) [starting]\n  - mol_012: H2S (Hydrogen sulfide) [starting]\n  - mol_013: FeS2 (Pyrite) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: H3NO (Hydroxylamine) [starting]\n  - mol_015: C5H7NO5 (Alpha-ketoglutarate oxime) [intermediate]\n  - mol_016: Fe (Native iron) [starting]\n  - mol_017: H+ (Hydrogen ion; aqueous proton accounting species) [starting]\n  - mol_018: Fe2+ (Ferrous ion) [intermediate]\n  - mol_019: C2H5NO2 (Glycine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_020: C3HN (Cyanoacetylene) [starting]\n  - mol_021: CHN (Hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_022: C4H2N2 (Maleonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_023: C4H4N2 (Succinonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_024: S8 (Elemental sulfur) [intermediate]\n  - mol_025: C4H5NO (3-Cyanopropanal; beta-cyanopropionaldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_026: C5H6N2O (3-Cyanopropanal cyanohydrin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_027: C5H7N3 (Glutamate/glutamine aminodinitrile precursor) [intermediate]\n  - mol_028: C3H4O (Acrolein) [starting]\n  - mol_029: C5H8O5 (Racemic 2-hydroxyglutaric acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_030: C5H7NO3 (Racemic pyroglutamic acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_031: C5H8NO4- (Racemic glutamate monoanion) [target]\n  - mol_032: C4H6O4 (Succinic acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_033: CO2 (Carbon dioxide) [intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (23 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_002, mol_003 -> mol_004 (Aldol construction of the C5 hydroxyketoacid) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_004 -> mol_005, mol_001 (Hydroxyketoacid dehydration to fumaroylformate) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_005, mol_003, mol_001 -> mol_006, mol_007 (Glyoxylate-driven reduction supplying alpha-ketoglutarate) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_005, mol_008 -> mol_006 (Hydrogen-driven reduction supplying alpha-ketoglutarate) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_005: mol_006, mol_009, mol_008 -> mol_010, mol_001 (Nickel-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_006: mol_006, mol_009, mol_011, mol_012 -> mol_010, mol_001, mol_013 (Pyrite-coupled iron-sulfide reductive amination) [Surface]\n  - rxn_007: mol_006, mol_014 -> mol_015, mol_001 (Alpha-ketoglutarate oxime formation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol_015, mol_016, mol_017 -> mol_010, mol_018, mol_001 (Native-iron reduction of the alpha-ketoglutarate oxime) [Surface]\n  - rxn_009: mol_006, mol_019 -> mol_010, mol_003 (Glycine-to-alpha-ketoglutarate transamination) [Surface]\n  - rxn_010: mol_003, mol_009, mol_008 -> mol_019, mol_001 (Preparation of the glycine nitrogen donor) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_011: mol_020, mol_021 -> mol_022 (Cyanoacetylene hydrocyanation to maleonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_012: mol_022, mol_012 -> mol_023, mol_024 (Sulfide-driven photoreduction of maleonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_013: mol_023, mol_012, mol_001 -> mol_025, mol_009, mol_024 (Selective nitrile photoreduction to the C4 semialdehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_014: mol_025, mol_021 -> mol_026 (Cyanohydrin formation from 3-cyanopropanal) [Surface]\n  - rxn_015: mol_026, mol_009 -> mol_027, mol_001 (Ammonia-dependent aminodinitrile formation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_016: mol_027, mol_001 -> mol_010, mol_009 (Exhaustive aminodinitrile hydrolysis to glutamic acid) [Surface]\n  - rxn_017: mol_028, mol_021 -> mol_025 (Proposed conjugate cyanide addition to acrolein) [Surface]\n  - rxn_018: mol_006, mol_008 -> mol_029 (Competing hydrogenation to 2-hydroxyglutarate) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_019: mol_010 -> mol_030, mol_001 (Glutamic acid cyclization to a pyroglutamate reservoir) [Surface]\n  - rxn_020: mol_030, mol_001 -> mol_010 (Acidic hydrolysis of the pyroglutamate reservoir) [Surface]\n  - rxn_021: mol_010 -> mol_031, mol_017 (Formation of the aqueous glutamate monoanion) [Surface]\n  - rxn_022: mol_031, mol_017 -> mol_010 (Glutamate protonation in an acidic receiving compartment) [Surface]\n  - rxn_023: mol_010, mol_001 -> mol_032, mol_009, mol_033, mol_008 (Lumped irreversible high-temperature degradation branch) [Hydrothermal]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Glyoxylate-driven C5 assembly followed by nickel reductive amination (5 steps)\n  - P2: Glyoxylate-driven C5 assembly followed by pyrite-coupled amination (5 steps)\n  - P3: Glyoxylate-driven C5 assembly followed by oxime reduction (6 steps)\n  - P4: Glyoxylate-driven C5 assembly with a glycine nitrogen-transfer relay (6 steps)\n  - P5: Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction and nickel reductive amination (5 steps)\n  - P6: Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction followed by iron-sulfide amination (5 steps)\n  - P7: Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction followed by hydroxylamine and native iron (6 steps)\n  - P8: Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction with a glycine nitrogen-transfer relay (6 steps)\n  - P9: Cyanoacetylene-derived cyanosulfidic nitrile pathway (7 steps)\n  - P10: Proposed acrolein-entry Strecker pathway (5 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 23 reactions | 18 balanced as written | 5 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_008 balanceable: C5H7NO5 + 2 Fe + 4 H+ -> C5H9NO4 + 2 Fe2+ + H2O\n  - rxn_012 balanceable: 8 C4H2N2 + 8 H2S -> 8 C4H4N2 + S8\n  - rxn_013 balanceable: 8 C4H4N2 + 8 H2S + 8 H2O -> 8 C4H5NO + 8 H3N + S8\n  - rxn_016 balanceable: C5H7N3 + 4 H2O -> C5H9NO4 + 2 H3N\n  - rxn_023 balanceable: C5H9NO4 + 2 H2O -> C4H6O4 + H3N + CO2 + 2 H2\nFormula/structure inconsistencies:\n  - mol_018 (Ferrous ion): stated formula Fe2+ disagrees with its InChI structure (stated minus InChI: Fe+1, charge 1 vs 2)\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH ON TARGET MOLECULE ===\nThe following research was conducted on the target molecule before network generation. Use it to assess whether the network aligns with known prebiotic chemistry literature.\n\n=== DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of Glutamate (Glutamic Acid): Routes, Evidence, Constraints and Open Problems\n\nNo single prebiotic route to glutamate is both experimentally complete and uncontested. The best-documented chemistry splits into two disconnected families. One is aldehyde/nitrile (Strecker-type) chemistry, which makes glutamate as a minor product of atmospheric, cyanide and meteoritic chemistry. The other is \u03b1-ketoglutarate (\u03b1-KG) chemistry, which mirrors biology: abiotic \u03b1-KG formation and nonenzymatic reductive amination or transamination have each been shown. Coupling the two in one pot has so far failed for glutamate specifically. Glutamate is consistently a low-abundance \"early\" amino acid, and it is unusually prone to cyclizing to pyroglutamate. Both points should be built into any network design.\n\n## TL;DR\n- **Two families of routes, neither closed end-to-end.** Strecker/nitrile chemistry needs a C4 aldehyde, 3-cyanopropanal (\u03b2-cyanopropionaldehyde). This has been reached by an explicit UV/H\u2082S (and Cu-photoredox) sequence from cyanoacetylene (Patel et al. 2015, *Nature Chemistry*), and it is proposed (not demonstrated) from spark-derived acrolein (Van Trump & Miller 1972, *Science*). \u03b1-KG routes have shown each half separately:\n  - \u03b1-KG from glyoxylate + pyruvate (Fe\u00b2\u207a: Muchowska et al. 2019, *Nature*; metal-free: Stubbs et al. 2020, *Nature Chemistry*).\n  - \u03b1-KG \u2192 glutamate with Fe/FeS, Ni/H\u2082, cyanide/Bucherer\u2013Bergs or transamination.\n  - But in the best one-pot H\u2082/Ni network, glutamate itself was not detectable (Kaur et al. 2024, *Chem*).\n- **Glutamate is real but minor in abiotic inventories.** It shows up in reanalysed Miller discharge extracts (Johnson et al. 2008, *Science*), in neutral-atmosphere sparks only when nitrite oxidation is suppressed (Cleaves et al. 2008, *OLEB*), and in HCN-polymer hydrolysates. It is present in carbonaceous chondrites and racemic in pristine Bennu samples (Glavin et al. 2025, *Nature Astronomy*). It falls in Higgs & Pudritz's (2009, *Astrobiology*) thermodynamically ranked \"early ten\", but always well below Gly/Ala.\n- **The main bottlenecks are:**\n  - supply of the C4 precursor (3-cyanopropanal or \u03b1-KG) at useful concentration;\n  - incompatibility between \u03b1-KG-forming and aminating conditions;\n  - \u03b1-KG's intrinsically slow reductive amination (Mayer & Moran 2022, *Angew. Chem.*);\n  - cyclization of glutamate to pyroglutamate and thermal loss above ~150\u2013200 \u00b0C.\n\n  The Strecker routes are contested mainly on precursor availability and on the cyanide-vs-metabolism \"congruence\" argument. The \u03b1-KG routes are contested on reagent choice (hydroxylamine, stoichiometric Fe\u2070, noble metals), on ammonia concentration and on Orgel-type objections to self-organizing cycles.\n\n## Key Findings\n\n| Route / framework | Feedstocks | Key glutamate-relevant intermediate | Best quantitative evidence | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Electric discharge, strongly reducing (CH\u2084/NH\u2083/H\u2082/H\u2082O) | CH\u2084, NH\u2083, H\u2082, H\u2082O | HCN + aldehydes \u2192 aminonitriles (Strecker); acrolein proposed for Glu | Glu identified in Miller's 1950s extracts (Johnson et al. 2008); glycine yields 2.1% / 1.8% / 0.4% of CH\u2084-carbon in classic / volcanic / silent apparatus; Glu is a trace product | Contested: early-atmosphere redox |\n| Discharge, H\u2082S-rich | CH\u2084, H\u2082S, NH\u2083, CO\u2082 | Same, plus thio-chemistry | Glu detected (Parker et al. 2011, *PNAS*); L-enrichment of Asp/Glu/Ser flagged as possible trace contamination | Contested: atmosphere; Glu losses via H\u2082S |\n| Discharge, neutral (CO\u2082/N\u2082) | CO\u2082, N\u2082, H\u2082O | Strecker | Glu among products only when Fe\u00b2\u207a/ascorbate suppress nitrite/nitrate oxidation during workup (Cleaves et al. 2008); Plankensteiner et al. 2006 did not detect Asp/Glu | Contested: yields, workup |\n| Proton/cosmic-ray irradiation | CO/CO\u2082, N\u2082, H\u2082O \u00b1 CH\u2084 | Macromolecular \"amino acid precursors\" (hydrolysis needed) | Amino acids appear at \u22650.5% CH\u2084 (Kobayashi et al. 2023, *Life*); Gly dominant; Glu not reported as a major product | Plausible energy source; Glu-specific data thin |\n| HCN oligomer/polymer hydrolysis | HCN, NH\u2083 | Polymer-bound nitrile/amide units | Glu among 12 protein amino acids released on acid hydrolysis (Ruiz-Bermejo et al. 2013, *Life*); Gly always dominant | Contested: needs \u22650.1 M HCN and acid hydrolysis |\n| Cyanosulfidic photochemistry | HCN, cyanoacetylene, H\u2082S, Cu, UV | Maleonitrile \u2192 succinonitrile \u2192 3-cyanopropanal \u2192 cyanohydrin \u2192 \u03b1-aminonitrile 54 | Explicit Glu/Gln aminonitrile route (Patel et al. 2015); step yields only in SI | Contested: sulfide, Cu, UV, sequential additions |\n| \u03b1-KG via glyoxylate + pyruvate | Glyoxylate, pyruvate (ultimately CO\u2082) | Hydroxyketoglutarate \u2192 oxopentenedioate \u2192 \u03b1-KG | 5% \u03b1-KG (0.5 M phosphate, pH 7, 50 \u00b0C, 21 h; Stubbs et al. 2020); Fe\u00b2\u207a 70 \u00b0C network (Muchowska et al. 2019) | Contested: glyoxylate supply, cycle plausibility |\n| \u03b1-KG reductive amination (Fe, FeS, Ni/H\u2082, electrochemical) | \u03b1-KG, NH\u2083/NH\u2084\u207a, reductant | Imine of \u03b1-KG | Up to ~50% Glu with fresh FeS at 50\u201375 \u00b0C (Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user 2003); >50% in electrochemically reduced FeS (Kitadai et al. 2019); Ni/Pd/meteorite + H\u2082 at 22 \u00b0C, pH 8 (Kaur et al. 2024) | Strongest experimentally; contested on reagents, NH\u2083 levels and coupling |\n| \u03b1-KG via Bucherer\u2013Bergs (cyanide) | \u03b1-KG, HCN, NH\u2083 source (DAP) | 5-substituted hydantoin | Hydantoin 96%; glutamate 19% after hydrolysis with 72% hydantoin left (Pulletikurti et al. 2022, *Nat. Chem.*) | Contested: cyanide \"incongruent\" with metabolism |\n| Hydrothermal one-step thermodynamics | CO\u2082, NH\u2084\u207a, H\u2082 | n/a | \u0394Gr(Glu\u207b) = \u22121.43 kJ/mol at 100 \u00b0C vs +172.13 kJ/mol at 18 \u00b0C (Amend & Shock 1998, *Science*) | Contested: kinetics, stability (Bada & Miller 1995) |\n| Meteoritic/asteroidal delivery | Parent-body aqueous chemistry | Strecker-cyanohydrin, others | Glu racemic in Bennu; L-excesses in Murchison partly terrestrial (Glavin et al. 2021, *MAPS*) | Source of racemic Glu; delivery flux uncertain |\n\n## Details\n\n### 1. Known prebiotic synthesis routes\n\n#### 1a. Atmospheric electric discharge (strongly reducing, volcanic, H\u2082S-rich, neutral)\n\n**Mechanism.** The discharge makes HCN, NH\u2083 and aldehydes in the gas phase. These dissolve, and aqueous Strecker chemistry turns them into \u03b1-aminonitriles and then amino acids (Miller 1957).\\[1\\]\\[2\\] For glutamate, Van Trump & Miller (1972, *Science* 178:859) found acrolein (CH\u2082=CH\u2013CHO) among the spark products of CH\u2084/N\u2082/NH\u2083/H\u2082O/H\u2082S. They proposed it \"as an intermediate in the prebiotic synthesis of methionine and of glutamic acid, homocysteine, homoserine, and \u03b1,\u03b3-diaminobutyric acid.\"\\[3\\]\n- The proposed chemistry is conjugate addition of HCN to acrolein, giving \u03b2-cyanopropionaldehyde (3-cyanopropanal), followed by Strecker reaction and hydrolysis.\n- **For glutamate this remains a proposal.** No Miller-lab yield for acrolein \u2192 Glu has been found. Parker et al. (2011, *OLEB*) restate the idea but give methionine yields only qualitatively.\\[4\\]\n- The underlying organic chemistry is real outside the prebiotic setting. An industrial patent (US 3,010,994, filed 1955\u201356) converts acrylonitrile to \u03b2-cyanopropionaldehyde in up to 70 mol% yield, then by Strecker reaction to glutamic acid at 45.8% of theory based on acrylonitrile.\\[5\\] It runs under non-prebiotic, industrial conditions.\n\n**Evidence.**\n- **Johnson et al. (2008, *Science* 322:404)** reanalysed Miller's archived \"volcanic\" (steam-injected) apparatus extracts.\n  - Conditions: 200 torr CH\u2084, 200 torr NH\u2083, 100 torr H\u2082, 500 mL water, about 1 week of sparking.\\[6\\]\n  - They found 22 amino acids and 5 amines, including D,L-glutamic acid and 2-methylglutamic acid.\\[6\\]\n  - Absolute yields could not be recalculated. Miller's own glycine yields were 2.1%, 1.8% and 0.4% of CH\u2084-carbon for the classic, volcanic and silent-discharge apparatus.\\[6\\]\n  - Glutamate is reported relative to glycine and is a trace component. One secondary reading of Table S1 puts Gly + Ala above 99.8% of protein amino acids; that figure is unverified.\\[7\\]\n- **Parker et al. (2011, *PNAS* 108:5526)** reanalysed Miller's 1958 H\u2082S run (CH\u2084/H\u2082S/NH\u2083/CO\u2082 = 258/100/250/87 mm Hg, 300 mL water, 3 days) and detected glutamic acid.\\[8\\]\n  - They note that aspartic and glutamic acids and serine \"are present in relatively low abundances\" and \"are enriched in the L-enantiomer, indicating that trace amounts of contamination of some amino acids may be present.\"\\[8\\]\n  - A companion *OLEB* paper suggests Asp and Glu may break down to alkyl amino acids via thioimide intermediates when H\u2082S is present.\\[9\\] This is a specific sink for glutamate in sulfur-rich settings.\n- **Ring et al. (1972, *PNAS* 69:765)** found glutamic acid among 19 amino acids from CH\u2084/N\u2082/H\u2082O with trace NH\u2083.\\[10\\]\n\n**Neutral atmospheres.** Cleaves, Chalmers, Lazcano, Miller & Bada (2008, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres* 38:105) showed that earlier low yields from CO\u2082/N\u2082 sparks reflected oxidation by discharge-made nitrite and nitrate during hydrolytic workup. Adding ferrous iron or ascorbate before hydrolysis greatly raised yields,\\[11\\] and a later review summarizes \"significant production of Ala, Ser and Glu.\" By contrast, Plankensteiner et al. (2006) sparked CO\u2082/N\u2082/H\u2082O and did not detect Asp or Glu.\\[12\\]\n\n**Critiques.**\n- The early atmosphere was probably not strongly reducing (Kasting 1993, as summarized by Higgs & Pudritz 2009).\\[1\\]\\[8\\]\n- Neutral-atmosphere yields depend on workup chemistry and on ocean pH/redox buffering.\\[12\\]\n- Critics have argued that the Fe\u00b2\u207a/ascorbate step amounts to experimental intervention; this criticism appears mainly in non-peer-reviewed commentary.\\[13\\]\n- Glutamate is always a minor product, so discharge chemistry supplies Glu only at trace levels relative to Gly/Ala.\n\n#### 1b. Proton/cosmic-ray irradiation, radiolysis, UV and shock\n\n- **Kobayashi et al. (2023, *Life* 13:1103)** irradiated CO\u2082/CH\u2084/N\u2082/H\u2082O mixtures with protons, simulating solar energetic particles.\n  - Amino acids appeared after acid hydrolysis once CH\u2084 reached 0.5% (v/v). Unhydrolysed samples held only precursors.\\[14\\]\n  - No amino acids formed by UV, even at 50% CH\u2084.\\[15\\]\n  - At an initial CH\u2084 ratio of 0.12, proton irradiation made amino acids about 3 orders of magnitude faster than spark discharge.\\[16\\]\n  - Glycine dominated, followed by Asp, Ser, Ala, \u03b1-aminobutyric acid and \u03b2-alanine.\\[17\\] Glutamate is not reported as a major product.\n- Earlier proton work on CO/N\u2082/H\u2082O (Miyakawa et al. 2002, reported as column A3 in Higgs & Pudritz 2009) also gave amino acid precursors.\\[1\\]\n- Gamma radiolysis of HCN solutions, followed by hydrolysis, yields glycine, alanine, valine, serine, threonine, aspartic acid and glutamic acid, with contamination controls (summarized in Ruiz-Bermejo et al. 2013).\\[18\\]\n- Shock synthesis from gas mixtures (Bar-Nun et al. 1970) is among the data sets Higgs & Pudritz used to rank amino acids.\\[1\\]\n\n**Critique.** These routes give macromolecular precursors that need strong acid hydrolysis; that is an analytical step, not necessarily a natural one.\\[15\\] Data on glutamate yields are sparse.\n\n#### 1c. Strecker / nitrile chemistry and the C4 aldehyde problem\n\nGlutamate's Strecker precursor is succinic semialdehyde (4-oxobutanoic acid), or its nitrile analogue 3-cyanopropanal (4-oxobutanenitrile, HCOCH\u2082CH\u2082CN). Strecker chemistry on 3-cyanopropanal gives 2-aminopentanedinitrile, the common precursor of glutamine and glutamate.\\[19\\] Proposed origins of the C4 aldehyde:\n1. Acrolein + HCN (Van Trump & Miller 1972): proposed only.\n2. Cyanoacetylene \u2192 maleonitrile \u2192 succinonitrile \u2192 3-cyanopropanal by UV/H\u2082S photoreduction (Patel et al. 2015; see 1d).\n3. Interstellar/parent-body sources. 4-oxobutanenitrile has been characterized by rotational spectroscopy and searched for in the G+0.693 molecular cloud (arXiv 2512.11500). That work describes the molecule as one \"proposed in prebiotic chemistry\u2026 as a precursor of glutamic acid\" (citing Kitadai & Maruyama 2018).\\[20\\] A search is not a detection.\n\n**Critique.** The C4 aldehyde is much less abundant than formaldehyde or acetaldehyde in every simulated source, which is a main reason Glu trails Gly/Ala. Aldehyde-based Strecker chemistry is also argued to be \"disconnected\" from biology's \u03b1-ketoacid-based amino acid metabolism (Pulletikurti et al. 2022, *Nature Chemistry* 14:1142).\\[21\\]\n\n#### 1d. Cyanosulfidic UV photochemistry (Sutherland)\n\nPatel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy & Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry* 7:301) reported the following sequence:\n1. Photoreduction of maleonitrile (from cyanoacetylene) by H\u2082S \"saturates the double bond giving succinonitrile 51.\"\\[22\\]\n2. \"Further irradiation in the presence of [H\u2082S] selectively reduces one nitrile group of succinonitrile 51 giving the semialdehyde 52.\"\\[22\\] Compound 52 is 3-cyanopropanal.\n3. \"Addition of hydrogen cyanide\u2026 to the semialdehyde 52 gives the cyanohydrin 53 from which 54, the \u03b1-aminonitrile precursor of glutamine and glutamic acid is produced upon addition of ammonia.\"\\[22\\]\n\nThe photoreductions use UV (254 nm), and per the abstract can be \"accelerated by Cu(I)\u2013Cu(II) photoredox cycling.\"\\[23\\]\\[24\\] In this network acrylonitrile feeds the proline/arginine branch, not glutamate.\\[25\\] Per-step yields are in the paper's Supplementary Information and could not be verified here.\n\n**Critiques.**\n- **Sulfide supply.** Sulfidic anions are needed as both photoreductant and feedstock, and Patel et al. invoke impactors as the source. Ranjan, Todd, Sutherland & Sasselov (2018, *Astrobiology*; arXiv 1801.07725) show that this imposes a local geochemical requirement and quantify the sulfide levels attainable in surface waters.\\[24\\]\n- **Copper.** Cu is relatively scarce in the crust.\\[19\\]\n- **Not one-pot.** The chemistry relies on sequential, controlled delivery of reagents (Springsteen 2015; Higgs & Lehman 2015, cited in Ranjan et al. 2018).\\[19\\]\n- **Congruence.** The route does not resemble biology's \u03b1-KG-based glutamate synthesis. A later cyanosulfidic paper (\"A cyanosulfidic origin of the Krebs cycle\", *Science Advances*) tries to link the network to TCA intermediates.\\[26\\]\n\n#### 1e. HCN oligomer/polymer and formamide chemistry\n\n- **Ferris et al. (1978, *J. Mol. Evol.* 11:293)** condensed 0.1 M HCN at pH 9.2. Hydrolysis of the oligomers released pyrimidines, adenine and amino acids.\\[27\\]\n- **Ruiz-Bermejo, Zorzano & Osuna-Esteban (2013, *Life* 3:421)** reviewed the field: HCN polymers yield twelve of the twenty protein amino acids, including glutamic acid, with \"glycine\u2026 always obtained with the greatest yield.\" Acid hydrolysis was usually needed before detection.\\[28\\]\\[29\\] Structural work (TMAH thermochemolysis) finds \u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013 substructures, consistent with Glu side chains on hydrolysis.\\[30\\]\n- **Critiques:**\n  - Steady-state HCN in the open primitive ocean is estimated at only 4 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2076 to 2 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2078 M (pH 8, 0\u201325 \u00b0C; Miyakawa et al. 2002, cited in Ruiz-Bermejo et al. 2013).\\[29\\] Polymerization needs much higher concentrations and is \"very ineffective close to or below room temperature\" at low concentration (Ruiz-Bermejo et al.).\\[31\\]\n  - Release depends on harsh hydrolysis, and the products are unselective.\n  - No formamide-specific glutamate synthesis was found in this review.\n\n#### 1f. \u03b1-Ketoglutarate-based routes (metabolism-first and \"protometabolic\" frameworks)\n\n**Step 1: forming \u03b1-KG.**\n- **Muchowska, Varma & Moran (2019, *Nature* 569:104).** Fe\u00b2\u207a in 70 \u00b0C water turns pyruvate + glyoxylate into 9 of 11 TCA intermediates, including \u03b1-KG. The path is aldol to hydroxyketoglutarate, dehydration to oxopentenedioate, then reduction to \u03b1-KG. Adding hydroxylamine and Fe\u2070 \"produces four biological amino acids\": glycine, alanine, aspartic acid and glutamic acid, by t = 2 h.\\[32\\]\\[33\\]\\[34\\]\n- **Stubbs, Yadav, Krishnamurthy & Springsteen (2020, *Nature Chemistry* 12:1016).** This network needs no metal. Glyoxylate acts as both carbon source and reductant. In 0.5 M phosphate at pH 7 and 50 \u00b0C for 21 h it gives 39% maloyl formate, 6% fumaroyl formate, 5% \u03b1-ketoglutarate, 5% isocitroyl formate and 2% aconitoyl formate. The \u03b1-ketoacids can be transaminated by glycine, \"obviating the need for metal-catalysed abiotic reductive aminations.\"\\[35\\]\\[36\\]\n- **Yadav et al. (2022, *Nature Chemistry* 14:170).** Cyanide acts as a reductant for some TCA steps (oxaloacetate \u2192 malate, fumarate \u2192 succinate), \"whereas pyruvate and \u03b1-ketoglutarate themselves are not reduced.\"\\[37\\] That selectivity spares \u03b1-KG.\n- **Kaur et al. (2024, *Chem* 10).** H\u2082 over Ni or Pd drives the reductive aldol chemistry at 22 \u00b0C. Without a catalyst, aldol condensation still occurs but the products downstream of fumaroyl formate reduction (which include \u03b1-KG) do not.\\[38\\]\n- Guzman & Martin (2009, *Astrobiology*) reported photoelectrochemical production of rTCA \u03b1-ketoacids on ZnS mineral.\n\n**Step 2: \u03b1-KG \u2192 glutamate.**\n- **Fe/FeS reductive amination.** Hafenbradl, Keller, W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user & Stetter (1995, *Tetrahedron Letters* 36:5179) coupled reductive amination of \u03b1-oxo acids to FeS \u2192 pyrite oxidation. Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003, *Tetrahedron Letters* 44:1695) found amino acids \"formed efficiently\u2026 with freshly precipitated FeS or Fe(OH)\u2082\u2026 at pH values near their pKa.\"\\[21\\]\\[39\\]\n  - Yields of up to ~50% for alanine, glutamate, phenylalanine and tyrosine at 50 and 75 \u00b0C are reported in secondary summaries; no exact primary figure for glutamate was located, and Kitadai et al. (2019, *Sci. Adv.* 5:eaav7848) describe as-prepared FeS as promoting keto acid\u2013to\u2013amino acid reductive amination \"albeit only slightly\" at pH 8\u201310.\n  - Fresh sulfides of Zn, Mn, Co, Cu and Ni were inactive, and replacing Fe with Ni simply lowered the yield (as summarized by Barge et al. 2019).\\[40\\]\n- **Electrochemically reduced FeS (alkaline-vent analogue).** Kitadai et al. (2019, *Science Advances* 5:eaav7848) reductively aminated four keto acids (1.25 mM each) in one bottle. All the corresponding amino acids formed in >50% yield, and \"Alanine and glutamate were formed preferentially over the others.\"\\[41\\]\n- **Iron (oxy)hydroxides.** Barge et al. (2019, *PNAS* 116:4828) showed pyruvate \u2192 alanine is greatest at a 1:1 Fe(II):Fe(III) ratio, under alkaline conditions, at about 70 \u00b0C; only lactate forms at pH 7.\\[42\\]\\[43\\] Glutamate was not tested, so this is an analogue result.\n- **Ni or Pd with H\u2082 (Kaur et al. 2024).**\n  - Conditions: 30 mM ketoacid, NH\u2084HCO\u2083 (5 equiv, 150 mM), H\u2082 at 5 bar, pH 8, 22 \u00b0C, 72 h.\\[38\\]\n  - Catalysts: Pd/C (1 mol%), Ni/SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083 (10 mol%) or ground Aletai iron meteorite (18 mol% Ni). All converted \u03b1-KG and five other ketoacids plus glyoxylate into amino acid + hydroxy acid mixtures.\\[38\\]\n  - Combined yields were 6\u201384% for Ni and meteorite, with amino:hydroxy acid ratios near 1:1, and 52\u201388% for Pd, with amino acid favoured >5:1 in almost all cases.\\[38\\]\n  - With no catalyst there was no reduction.\\[38\\]\n  - Ammonia could be cut to 6 mM, which Kaur et al. note is 62\u2013250 times lower than in earlier Fe-based work (375 mM\u20131.5 M).\\[38\\]\n  - **But in the one-pot network starting from oxaloacetate + glyoxylate, \"Glutamate and hydroxy ketoglutarate were not detectable.\"**\\[38\\]\n- **Bucherer\u2013Bergs (cyanide) route.** Pulletikurti, Yadav, Springsteen & Krishnamurthy (2022, *Nature Chemistry* 14:1142) combined \u03b1-KG + diamidophosphate + cyanide to give the glutamate hydantoin in 96% yield. Hydrolysis then gave glutamate in 19%, with 72% hydantoin remaining (preprint data).\\[21\\]\\[44\\] The sequence is hydantoin formation at about 20 \u00b0C, then hydrolysis at about 80 \u00b0C.\\[38\\]\n- **Transamination.** Nonenzymatic transamination with glycine works on \u03b1-ketoacid analogues (Stubbs et al. 2020). Al\u00b3\u207a-catalysed transamination also works, but it is incompatible with phosphate-mediated \u03b1-KG formation (Kaur et al. 2024).\\[38\\] Pyridoxamine and urea reductively aminate \u03b1-KG to glutamate nonenzymatically even in metabolite extracts. That is an analytical artifact study (*J. Chromatogr. A* 2020),\\[45\\] but it shows how easily the reaction happens with B6-type amine donors.\n- **Kinetics.** Mayer & Moran (2022, *Angew. Chem. Int. Ed.*) used BH\u2083CN\u207b as a model hydride donor. Nonenzymatic reductive amination reactivity runs \"\u03b1-ketoglutarate < oxaloacetate \u2248 pyruvate \u226a glyoxylate,\" and \u03b1-KG amination is also \"thermochemically less favored.\"\\[46\\] Biology's choice of \u03b1-KG as the entry point for reductive amination is therefore not explained by intrinsic reactivity. They note that nonenzymatic transamination using glutamate as the donor is thermodynamically favourable for most \u03b1-ketoacids.\\[47\\]\n\n**Critiques of the \u03b1-KG family.**\n- **Reagents.** Hydroxylamine, Fe\u2070, BH\u2083CN\u207b, Pd and DAP are questioned as prebiotic reagents. Kaur et al. note that Fe\u2070/hydroxylamine \"disrupt Fe\u00b2\u207a-promoted ketoacid formation.\" Kaur et al. 2024, in turn, argue that cyanide \"is never directly used in metabolism\u2026 its relevance to metabolic origins is questionable.\"\\[38\\] Proponents of cyanide chemistry answer that their Bucherer\u2013Bergs route makes cyanide chemistry *more* congruent with \u03b1-ketoacid metabolism.\\[21\\]\n- **Coupling.** Every step has been shown, but glutamate itself is not yet produced in an integrated one-pot network.\n- **Feedstock.** A continuous supply of glyoxylate and pyruvate is assumed.\\[48\\]\n- **Cycles.** Orgel (2008, *PLoS Biology*, \"The implausibility of metabolic cycles on the prebiotic Earth\") argued that self-organizing cycles lack the needed catalytic specificity. This objection applies directly to rTCA-like \u03b1-KG supply.\n\n#### 1g. Iron\u2013sulfur world and hydrothermal scenarios; thermodynamic modelling\n\n**Thermodynamics.** Amend & Shock (1998, *Science* 281:1659) wrote net synthesis as 5CO\u2082(aq) + NH\u2084\u207a + 9H\u2082(aq) \u2192 glutamate\u207b + 2H\u207a + 6H\u2082O.\n- In a 100 \u00b0C mixed hydrothermal solution, \u0394Gr = \u22121.43 kJ/mol (glutamate activity 2.7 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2079).\n- In 18 \u00b0C surface seawater, \u0394Gr = +172.13 kJ/mol.\n- 11 of 20 amino acids were exergonic under the hydrothermal conditions.\\[49\\]\n\nLater work extended this to alkaline vents (Kitadai 2015, *OLEB*).\\[50\\]\n\n**Critiques.**\n- Bada, Miller & Zhao (1995, *OLEB* 25:111) showed amino acids \"are irreversibly destroyed by heating at 240 \u00b0C\" and argued that \"quasi-equilibrium calculations give misleading descriptions.\"\\[51\\]\n- Aubrey, Cleaves & Bada (2009, *OLEB* 39:91) assessed submarine systems and found amino acid yields peaking after ~75 h at 175 \u00b0C, with competing degradation to NH\u2083, CO\u2082 and H\u2082.\\[52\\]\n- Higgs & Pudritz (2009) found no correlation between hydrothermal \u0394G and observed prebiotic abundance.\\[1\\]\n- Martin and colleagues (*Frontiers in Microbiology* 2021) note that one-step \u0394G calculations \"do not begin to capture\" the stepwise thermodynamics of metabolism.\\[53\\]\n- Tutolo (2026, *PNAS* 123(36):e2613108123, \"Rethinking the origin of life at seafloor hydrothermal vents\") argues that ancient serpentinizing vents lacked strong pH gradients at hydrothermal temperatures and would have had virtually no sulfide, concluding that \"these considerations challenge currently formulated alkaline vent hypotheses for the origin of life\"; this undercuts the FeS chemistry in alkaline-vent models.\n\nFischer\u2013Tropsch-type syntheses (e.g., Yoshino et al. 1971, CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 over catalysts at high temperature) produced amino acids in some runs.\\[1\\] Glutamate-specific data were not located.\n\n#### 1h. Extraterrestrial sources and delivery\n\n**Meteorites.**\n- Glutamic acid is a common indigenous amino acid in CM chondrites (Kvenvolden et al. 1970).\\[54\\]\n- Engel & Macko (1997, *Nature* 389:265) reported nearly identical \u03b4\u00b9\u2075N for D- and L-Glu (+60\u2030, +58\u2030), argued for an extraterrestrial origin of a large L-excess (~54%), and invoked circularly polarized light.\\[54\\]\\[55\\]\n- Pizzarello & Cooper (2001, *MAPS*) found L-excesses of 16\u201347.2% in pyroglutamic acid. \u03b4\u00b9\u00b3C values were D-Glu +32.2\u2030 and L-Glu +14.6\u2030, which implies terrestrial dilution of the L-enantiomer.\\[56\\]\n- Glavin et al. (2021, *MAPS*) calculated that \"between 27% and 80% of the measured L-glutamic acid excess in the Murchison meteorite could be terrestrial.\" That puts the corrected indigenous L-excess at 16\u201340%. In Aguas Zarcas, L-Glu excesses of ~56\u201373% are \"mostly terrestrial.\"\\[57\\]\n- In Antarctic CR2s, Glu is the only amino acid with L-excess (D/L 0.58\u20130.83), attributed to contamination.\\[58\\]\n\n**Returned samples.**\n- **Bennu (OSIRIS-REx):** 33 amino acids, including 14 protein amino acids, with total abundance of 70 \u00b1 2 nmol/g (Parker, McLain, Glavin, Dworkin et al., NASA NTRS 20250002782), described by Glavin, Dworkin et al. (2025, *Nature Astronomy* 9:199) as \"lower by a factor of 3.6 than that in Murchison but 4.7 times higher than in Ryugu extracts.\" All chiral amino acids are racemic (Glavin, Dworkin et al. 2025, *Nature Astronomy* 9:199). Glu is among the dominant species.\n- **Bennu isotopes (Baczynski, McIntosh, Simkus\u2026 Glavin, Elsila\u2026 Lauretta 2026, *PNAS* 123(8):e2517723123):**\n  - L-Glu \u03b4\u00b9\u00b3C = +20 \u00b1 5\u2030.\n  - \"The \u03b415N value of D-glutamic acid (+277 \u00b1 7\u2030) is 87\u2030 higher than L-glutamic acid (+190 \u00b1 32\u2030), even though the enantiomers are racemic,\" which \"challenge[s] the assumption of isotopic uniformity between amino acid chiral pairs.\"\n  - The authors suggest Murchison glycine formed mainly by Strecker-like aqueous chemistry, whereas Bennu glycine may come mainly from radical chemistry in cold ices.\\[59\\]\n- **Ryugu:** about 15 nmol/g total amino acids, dominated by \u03b2-Ala and \u03b3-aminobutyric acid rather than protein amino acids.\\[60\\]\n\n**Ice photolysis.** UV irradiation of interstellar-ice analogues (H\u2082O/CO/NH\u2083/CH\u2083OH/HCN) gives several amino acids after hydrolysis (Bernstein et al. 2002; Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. 2002, both *Nature*). Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. 2002 is one of the Higgs & Pudritz data sets.\\[1\\] Glu-specific yields from ice photolysis were not confirmed here.\n\n**Delivery critiques.**\n- Exogenous delivery matters only if it rivals endogenous synthesis. Pierazzo & Chyba (1999) estimated organic delivery on the order of 10\u2078 kg/yr, but survival on impact is uncertain (Higgs & Pudritz 2009).\\[1\\]\n- Pristine samples show Glu is racemic, so meteorites do not explain biological homochirality.\\[61\\]\n\n### 2. Key intermediates and precursors (by route)\n- **Strecker/nitrile:** HCN, NH\u2083, acrolein (proposed), cyanoacetylene \u2192 maleonitrile/fumaronitrile \u2192 succinonitrile \u2192 3-cyanopropanal (52) \u2192 cyanohydrin (53) \u2192 2-aminopentanedinitrile (54) \u2192 glutamine/glutamate.\\[22\\] Succinic semialdehyde is the carboxylic-acid analogue.\n- **HCN polymer:** HCN oligomers (aminomalononitrile, DAMN-derived units), which release Glu on hydrolysis.\n- **\u03b1-KG:** CO\u2082 \u2192 formate/acetate/pyruvate; glyoxylate; oxaloacetate; aldol products (hydroxyketoglutarate/maloyl formate) \u2192 oxopentenedioate/fumaroyl formate \u2192 \u03b1-KG \u2192 imine \u2192 glutamate.\\[38\\] Branches go through the \u03b1-KG hydantoin (Bucherer\u2013Bergs) or through transamination with glycine or a PLP/pyridoxamine analogue.\n- **Downstream sinks:** pyroglutamate (5-oxoproline), succinate, formate, CO\u2082, NH\u2083 (thermal), and alkyl amino acids (H\u2082S-mediated).\n\n### 3. Conditions and environments\n\n| Route | T | pH | Energy/reductant | Environment invoked |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Discharge/Strecker | Ambient aqueous after gas-phase synthesis | ~8\u20139 for Strecker | Lightning, volcanic lightning | Volcanic plumes, surface ocean, tidal pools |\n| Proton irradiation | Ambient | \u2014 | Solar energetic particles, GCR | Upper atmosphere of a weakly reducing CO\u2082/N\u2082 atmosphere |\n| Cyanosulfidic | Ambient, UV 254 nm | Near-neutral | UV + HS\u207b/H\u2082S, Cu photoredox | Surface streams/pools with impact-derived sulfide and cyanide salts |\n| HCN polymer | Up to 80\u201390 \u00b0C | ~9.2 | HCN self-condensation | Evaporative/concentrated basins |\n| \u03b1-KG formation | 22 \u00b0C (Ni/H\u2082) to 50\u201370 \u00b0C (phosphate, Fe\u00b2\u207a) | 7\u20138 | Glyoxylate, H\u2082 | Generic aqueous; vents (H\u2082 1\u201315 mmol/kg in modern fluids) |\n| Reductive amination | 22 \u00b0C (Ni/H\u2082), 50\u201375 \u00b0C (FeS), ~70 \u00b0C (Fe oxyhydroxide) | Alkaline, near the NH\u2084\u207a pKa of 9.25 | H\u2082, Fe\u00b2\u207a/FeS, electro-reduced FeS | Alkaline vents, Fe-rich sediments |\n| Hydrothermal one-step | ~100 \u00b0C, 250 bar | \u2014 | H\u2082 | Mixing zones |\n| Parent bodies | <50 \u00b0C (Bennu/Ryugu alteration) | Alkaline, NH\u2083-rich | Radiogenic heat | Carbonaceous asteroids |\n\n**Which environments are best supported?** None is uniquely established. On the evidence:\n- Parent-body aqueous alteration is the only setting where indigenous Glu is *observed*, from Murchison isotopes and racemic Glu in Bennu.\n- Iron-rich, H\u2082-bearing, alkaline aqueous settings have the strongest *experimental* support for the biology-congruent \u03b1-KG \u2192 Glu step. Glutamate's selective formation over FeS (Kitadai et al. 2019) is notable.\\[41\\]\n- UV-lit surface pools with sulfide and cyanide have the only explicit *de novo* nitrile route to the Glu skeleton.\n\n### 4. Catalysts and surfaces\n\n**Shown to promote glutamate-relevant steps:**\n- Freshly precipitated FeS and Fe(OH)\u2082 (Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user 2003).\\[39\\]\n- FeS with pyrite formation (Hafenbradl et al. 1995).\\[40\\]\n- Electrochemically reduced FeS (Kitadai et al. 2019).\\[41\\]\n- Mixed-valence iron oxyhydroxides, shown for pyruvate (Barge et al. 2019).\\[43\\]\n- Ni/SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083, Pd/C and iron meteorites (Aletai) with H\u2082 (Kaur et al. 2024).\\[38\\]\n- Fe\u00b2\u207a, which promotes \u03b1-KG formation (Muchowska et al. 2019).\\[33\\]\n- Phosphate as a general catalyst for \u03b1-KG formation (Stubbs et al. 2020).\\[35\\]\n- Al\u00b3\u207a for transamination.\n- Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox for cyanosulfidic photoreduction (Patel et al. 2015).\\[23\\]\n- ZnS photoelectrochemistry for \u03b1-ketoacids (Guzman & Martin 2009).\n\n**Shown inactive:** fresh sulfides of Zn, Mn, Co, Cu and Ni for reductive amination (Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user 2003).\\[40\\]\n\n**No catalyst required:**\n- Strecker/aminonitrile formation and hydrolysis.\n- Bucherer\u2013Bergs hydantoin formation from \u03b1-KG.\n- The aldol condensation of oxaloacetate/pyruvate with glyoxylate at 22 \u00b0C.\\[38\\]\n- Glyoxylate-driven reduction of fumaroyl formate in phosphate. Phosphate acts as a general catalyst here, with no metal (Stubbs et al. 2020).\\[36\\]\n- Glycine-mediated transamination of \u03b1-ketoacid analogues.\\[36\\]\n\n**Explicitly required:** H\u2082-driven reductive amination did not proceed without a metal (Kaur et al. 2024).\\[38\\]\n\n### 5. Stability and degradation constraints specific to glutamate\n\n- **Cyclization to pyroglutamate.** This is the dominant intramolecular sink.\n  - For N-terminal residues in peptides, spontaneous Glu cyclization has a limiting rate constant of 1.0 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2079 s\u207b\u00b9 (Glu-\u03b2NA at pH 6.0, where no pyroglutamate formed over 4 weeks) versus 1.2 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2077 s\u207b\u00b9 for Gln-\u03b2NA (glutaminyl/glutamate cyclase patent US 8,470,781, the same data as Seifert et al. 2009, *Biochemistry*); the Glu value is an upper bound, not a measured rate. It speeds up at low pH, where the Glu and Gln rates converge.\n  - Free glutamate converts to pyroglutamate in aqueous solution at \u226560 \u00b0C, especially at pH 2\u20133.5. Gayte-Sorbier et al. (\"Stability of Glutamic Acid and Monosodium Glutamate Under Model System Conditions\") found conversion to pyrrolidonecarboxylic acid \"favored by pH values ranging from 2\u20133, by temperature increase and by oxygen\"; a rate constant k \u2248 0.0066 h\u207b\u00b9 at pH 3 and 75 \u00b0C (half-life of about 4\u20135 days) is attributed to this work only in a secondary citation.\n  - In the solid state, glutamic acid cyclizes above about 140 \u00b0C, with polymer (polyglutamic acid) formation on further heating (Wu et al. 2010, *Crystal Growth & Design*).\\[62\\]\n  - DSC/TGA shows decomposition at T_peak = 200 \u00b0C, \u0394H = 88 kJ/mol (endothermic), mainly by water loss to pyroglutamic acid (Weiss et al. 2018, *BMC Biophysics*).\\[63\\]\n  - Pyroglutamic acid racemizes when heated above its melting point (162 \u00b0C).\\[64\\]\n- **Hydrothermal decomposition.** Lee et al. (2014, *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta*) ran flow-through experiments at 150\u2013250 \u00b0C and initial pH 6 and 10, with 3\u201336 min residence. Glutamate cyclized and decomposed to succinate, formate, CO\u2082 and NH\u2083. Adding 13 mM H\u2082(aq) suppressed decomposition products at all temperatures and pH values tested, so reducing vent fluids partly mitigate losses.\\[65\\]\n- **Implications for wet\u2013dry and evaporitic settings.** Drying and heating favour pyroglutamate and polyglutamate formation over free Glu. Pyroglutamate rehydrolyses only under fairly harsh conditions. Meteoritic glutamate is partly stored as pyroglutamate: Pizzarello & Cooper treat pyroglutamic acid as an acid-labile precursor that gives Glu on hydrolysis.\\[66\\] Network designs should treat pyroglutamate as a reservoir, not purely a loss.\n- **Racemization.** Every abiotic route gives racemic Glu, as seen in Bennu and in controlled syntheses.\\[8\\] Apparent L-excesses in meteorites are at least partly contamination (Glavin et al. 2021).\\[57\\]\n\n### 6. Glutamate as an \"early\" amino acid, and congruence with biochemistry\n\n- **Thermodynamic and abundance argument.** Higgs & Pudritz (2009, *Astrobiology* 9:483) ranked amino acids across meteorites, discharge, hydrothermal, ice, shock and cyanide data sets. Glu falls in the \"early ten\" (G, A, D, E, V, S, I, L, P, T). Rank correlates with \u0394G_surf (r = 0.96); early amino acids average 169.3 \u00b1 42.0 kJ/mol versus 285.0 \u00b1 94.1 kJ/mol for late ones. Relative concentration falls off as C_rel = 15.8 exp(\u2212\u0394G_surf/31.3).\\[1\\]\n- **Critiques of that argument.** The authors note that the exponential may reflect kinetics rather than equilibrium, and that differential stability could bias meteorite ratios. In their own data, hydrothermal \u0394G shows no correlation with abundance.\\[1\\] Glu's early status rests heavily on discharge and meteorite data, where it is consistently minor and sometimes contaminated.\n- **Genetic-code coevolution.** In Wong's coevolution theory (Wong 1975, 2005), Glu is a precursor-amino acid from which Gln, Pro and Arg biosynthetically derive.\\[1\\] That fits with Glu's precursor role, and conveniently, the cyanosulfidic network delivers Gln/Glu and Pro/Arg precursors from related nitriles.\\[25\\]\n- **Congruence with extant biosynthesis.** In biology, nitrogen enters through glutamate dehydrogenase (\u03b1-KG + NH\u2084\u207a + NAD(P)H) or GS/GOGAT, then spreads by PLP-dependent transamination using Glu as donor.\\[38\\]\\[47\\]\n  - \u03b1-KG reductive-amination and transamination routes are directly congruent.\n  - Strecker/cyanosulfidic routes are not, and need a later \"switch.\" Bucherer\u2013Bergs on \u03b1-ketoacids is proposed as a bridge (Pulletikurti et al. 2022).\\[21\\]\n  - Mayer & Moran (2022) caution that \u03b1-KG is intrinsically the *slowest* ketoacid to aminate, so biology's \u03b1-KG-centred choice is not simply inherited from nonenzymatic preference.\\[46\\]\n  - Kitadai et al. (2019) observed preferential Glu formation over FeS, which partly runs against that.\\[41\\]\n\n## Recommendations (for synthesis-network design and pathway evaluation)\n1. **Model two parallel feeder modules** rather than one route:\n   - (i) a nitrile module (HCN + C4 aldehyde/nitrile \u2192 aminonitrile \u2192 Glu/Gln);\n   - (ii) an \u03b1-ketoacid module (glyoxylate + pyruvate \u2192 \u03b1-KG \u2192 Glu).\n\n   Treat their interconversion as hydantoin (Bucherer\u2013Bergs) chemistry or transamination.\n2. **Make C4 precursor supply an explicit, rate-limiting node.** Track 3-cyanopropanal/succinic semialdehyde or \u03b1-KG flux. Benchmark \u03b1-KG at \u22645% from glyoxylate/pyruvate (Stubbs et al. 2020). Treat the acrolein \u2192 Glu step as hypothetical until yields are measured.\n3. **Use measured amination parameters:**\n   - Ni/Pd/meteorite + H\u2082 at 22 \u00b0C, pH 8, 6\u2013150 mM NH\u2083, 72 h (6\u201388% combined; roughly 1:1 amino:hydroxy acid on Ni);\n   - FeS at 50\u201375 \u00b0C (up to ~50%);\n   - electro-reduced FeS (>50%, Glu favoured).\n\n   Include the competing \u03b1-hydroxyglutarate channel.\n4. **Include pyroglutamate as a reversible sink.** Use the limiting rate constant k \u2264 1.0 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2079 s\u207b\u00b9 near neutral and ambient temperature (pH 6.0; patent US 8,470,781 / Seifert et al.), and much faster at acidic pH and above 60 \u00b0C. Include irreversible thermal loss above ~150\u2013200 \u00b0C, mitigated by H\u2082 (Lee et al. 2014).\n5. **Flag reagent-plausibility issues on every edge:** hydroxylamine, Fe\u2070, Pd, BH\u2083CN\u207b, DAP, 254 nm UV, high HCN, and impact-derived sulfide.\n6. **Prioritize experiments that close the gap.** The single most diagnostic missing result is glutamate formation *in situ* in a one-pot \u03b1-KG-forming network. Kaur et al. 2024 did not detect it.\n\n## Caveats\n- Several quantitative values could not be checked against primary full text:\n  - per-step yields for the Patel et al. 2015 glutamate branch (in the SI);\n  - Glu/Gly ratios in Johnson et al. 2008 and Parker et al. 2011 (figures/tables only);\n  - the exact Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user 2003 Glu yield (reported secondarily as \"up to ~50%\"; Kitadai et al. 2019 characterize as-prepared FeS as promoting this amination only \"slightly\");\n  - the 19% glutamate figure for the Bucherer\u2013Bergs route (from the preprint version).\n- The N-terminal cyclization rate (a limiting value of 1.0 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2079 s\u207b\u00b9) is an upper bound for peptide-bound Glu, not free glutamate. The pH 3, 75 \u00b0C rate constant comes from food-chemistry model systems and is attributed to Gayte-Sorbier et al. only via a secondary citation.\n- The proposed origin of 4-oxobutanenitrile in interstellar sources is based on a reported search, not a confirmed detection.\n- The atmospheric-composition, hydrothermal-plausibility and cyanide-vs-metabolism debates are unresolved.