ropriate pH adjustment.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Attach reaction-specific citations and quantitative recipes, including buffer, temperature, catalyst loading, H2 pressure and residence time.\",\n        \"Demonstrate sequential transfer into the amination conditions and measure glutamate versus 2-hydroxyglutarate without assuming quantitative alpha-ketoglutarate production.\",\n        \"Specify final buffering near pH 6-8 to make the glutamate monoanion predominant.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.9,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_003\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.73,\n        \"rxn_021\": 0.98\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.78,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.84,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.6,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.78,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.75,\n      \"feedback\": \"The carbon-skeleton sequence is supported, and rxn_006 has a balanced redox closure through FeS/H2S conversion to pyrite. Hafenbradl et al. (1995) and Huber and Wachtershauser (2003) provide relevant precedents, but activity depends strongly on mineral preparation and solution conditions. Results for electrochemically reduced FeS cannot establish the performance of the fresh FeS specified here. Pyrite formation and glutamate production must be demonstrated together under the chosen receiving conditions.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_006\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify FeS phase, preparation, aging, sulfide concentration, ammonia concentration, pH and temperature.\",\n        \"Measure glutamate formation alongside pyrite formation and competing products to establish the proposed redox coupling.\",\n        \"Test the compatibility of th