science/article/pii/S2667325826004255)\n  57. NASA probe brings back samples from asteroid Bennu, discovers diverse amino acids: International research group confirmed the discovery of \"Materials responsible for life\" (https://sj.jst.go.jp/news/202503/n0324-01k.html)\n  58. Phase Transformations of Glutamic Acid and Its Decomposition Products (https://pubs.acs.org/doi/abs/10.1021/cg901303a)\n  59. Thermal decomposition of the amino acids glycine, cysteine, aspartic acid, asparagine, glutamic acid, glutamine, arginine and histidine - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5807855/)\n  60. Phase Transformations of Glutamic Acid and Its Decomposition Products (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/231233025_Phase_Transformations_of_Glutamic_Acid_and_Its_Decomposition_Products)\n  61. The effects of temperature, pH and redox state on the stability of glutamic acid in hydrothermal fluids - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0016703714001537)\n  62. Enantiomeric Excesses of Acid Labile Amino Acid Precursors of the Murchison Meteorite - NASA Technical Reports Server (NTRS) (https://ntrs.nasa.gov/search.jsp?R=19990021044&hterms=pyroglutamic+acid&qs=N%3D0%26Ntk%3DAll%26Ntx%3Dmode%2Bmatchall%26Ntt%3Dpyroglutamic%2Bacid)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.79\n- Feedback: The network is formally well constructed: its 33 molecule nodes and 23 reaction nodes form a connected graph, and target-reaching schedules can be reconstructed from the stated inventories. The supplied deterministic check establishes that all reactions conserve atoms and charge with appropriate coefficients; there are no UNBALANCED reactions. Encode the inferred coefficients for rxn_008, rxn_012, rxn_013, rxn_016 and rxn_023. The factors of eight associated with S8 are ordinary net-reaction bookkeeping, not implausible elementary-event requirements. Repair the reported mol_018 formula/InChI inconsistency without treating it as an additional conservation failure. Literature alignment is good at the module level, and the strategy appropriately acknowledges staged operation, competing hydroxyglutarate production, racemic products and uncertain closures. However, the supplied reaction entries contain titles and broad environment labels rather than the claimed reaction-specific reasoning, citations, numerical conditions and physical transfer descriptions. Consequently, environmental consistency is incompletely established, not demonstrated to be universally incompatible. 'Biochemical' does not identify a physical compartment or establish enzyme involvement; active catalysts must be specified for rxn_004, rxn_010 and rxn_018, although catalysts need not be stoichiometric molecule nodes. The off-path chemistry also matters: rxn_018 is a supported competing reduction when an appropriate catalyst is present; rxn_019 is a plausible cyclization sink; rxn_020 requires substantial hydrolytic treatment and should not imply rapid ambient reservoir recovery. Reactions rxn_021 and rxn_022 are valid acid-base bookkeeping, but actual speciation depends on receiving pH. Reaction rxn_023 is balanced and appropriately qualified as lumped, yet the exact succinate/CO2/NH3/H2 product allocation is not established by evidence for thermal degradation generally. Initial feedstocks are accepted as stipulated; neither their identities nor the number of alternative schedules determines the score. Failure of a related one-pot experiment does not invalidate staged synthesis, but successful transfer and net glutamate recovery still require evidence. The network is a credible synthesis proposal, not sufficiently specified and validated for approval at the 0.90 network threshold.\n- Issues: ['The carbon-skeleton sequence is supported, and rxn_006 has a balanced redox closure through FeS/H2S conversion to pyrite. Hafenbradl et al. (1995) and Huber and Wachtershauser (2003) provide relevant precedents, but activity depends strongly on mineral preparation and solution conditions. Results for electrochemically reduced FeS cannot establish the performance of the fresh FeS specified here. Pyrite formation and glutamate production must be demonstrated together under the chosen receiving conditions.', 'Oxime formation followed by iron reduction is chemically credible, with relevant glutamate-producing hydroxylamine/Fe0 chemistry reported by Muchowska et al. (2019). Coupling that chemistry to the particular glyoxylate-driven upstream sequence remains a staged adaptation rather than a demonstrated schedule. Conditions must allow oxime formation before competing hydroxylamine reduction or iron corrosion dominates. The mol_018 formula/InChI discrepancy requires correction, but the supplied balance check does not identify rxn_008 as a conservation violation.', 'The glycine donor is internally producible, so this schedule does not depend on an unidentified nitrogen-bearing intermediate. Combining rxn_010 and rxn_009 also gives a coherent net reductive amination rather than a source of unaccounted reducing power. Stubbs et al. (2020) provide relevant transamination precedent, but alpha-ketoglutarate-specific rates, yields and equilibrium control are not supplied. Glyoxylate accumulation can oppose forward nitrogen transfer, and rxn_010 does not identify its required hydrogenation catalyst.', 'The sequence is stoichiometrically coherent and joins two experimentally motivated modules. Its main uncertainties are the unspecified catalyst for rxn_004 and the condition-dependent performance of the pyrite-coupled amination in rxn_006. Transfer between a metal/H2 reduction stage and a sulfide-rich mineral stage is possible, but the supplied environment labels do not establish how it occurs or how catalyst carryover is controlled.', 'This combines H2-dependent alpha-ketoglutarate supply with the hydroxylamine/iron chemistry supported by Muchowska et al. (2019). The proposed transformations are credible, but no quantitative schedule establishes selective alpha-ketoglutarate production, oxime accumulation and subsequent iron reduction in the transferred mixture. The required catalyst for rxn_004 and the acidity and iron consumption of rxn_008 remain unspecified. The Fe2+ metadata issue is distinct from atom or charge conservation.', 'The separately prepared glycine co-reactant makes this an executable reaction schedule in principle. However, neither H2-dependent preparation step identifies its active catalyst, and the nitrogen-transfer step lacks substrate-specific operating parameters. Literature showing rapid glyoxylate amination does not by itself establish efficient subsequent nitrogen transfer to alpha-ketoglutarate. The relay therefore remains less secure than direct, experimentally demonstrated alpha-ketoglutarate reductive amination.', \"Patel et al. (2015) directly support the central sequence through succinonitrile, 3-cyanopropanal, its cyanohydrin and the glutamate/glutamine aminonitrile precursor. This is strong support for precursor construction, not a quantified demonstration of final glutamate recovery under the network's unspecified conditions. Exhaustive hydrolysis of both nitriles is chemically reasonable but requires a defined hydrolysis regime and control of partial hydrolysis and cyclization. UV exposure, sulfide handling and any copper-mediated acceleration also need explicit environmental descriptions.\", 'The network correctly identifies the acrolein entry as proposed rather than experimentally established. Van Trump and Miller (1972) motivate rxn_017, but do not demonstrate useful selective production of 3-cyanopropanal followed by glutamate under the stated setting. Conjugate cyanide addition must compete with carbonyl cyanohydrin formation and acrolein side reactions. The downstream Strecker-type sequence is credible once the correct C4 aldehyde is supplied, while exhaustive hydrolysis retains the same uncertainties as P9.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Glutamate, racemic\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Construct a connected network containing 31 chemical-species nodes, 21 reaction nodes and 10 distinct target-reaching reaction schedules. Eight schedules combine two chemically different routes for reducing the C5 unsaturated ketoacid precursor with four nitrogen-incorporation routes. Two additional schedules construct glutamate through a C4 aldehyde and a dinitrile. These are conditional synthesis proposals, not ten independently demonstrated end-to-end prebiotic syntheses. Initial pyruvate, glyoxylate, cyanoacetylene, acrolein and inorganic reagents are stipulated feedstocks; their geochemical availability remains a boundary condition. Reaction conditions distinguish published benchmarks from proposed operating windows. Physical staging is required: irradiated sulfide chemistry, phosphate-rich carbon assembly, metal hydrogenation and hydrolysis are not assumed compatible in one vessel. Biochemical denotes nonenzymatic aqueous chemistry in specified compartments, including cool H2-pressurized rock pores, not biological catalysis. All compartments are anoxic; nonphotochemical reactions occur beneath sediment, in shaded pools or inside rock pores despite strong surface UV. Organic acids are represented by neutral parent structures for net-reaction bookkeeping, with explicit final equilibration to the glutamate monoanion. Actual protonation depends on receiving pH. Each environment_formed tag identifies a representative formation environment, not an exclusive one. Because the schema lacks a byproduct or sink role, mineral products, oxalate and hydroxyglutarate are labeled intermediate. Pyroglutamate formation and difficult recovery are included but are not counted as additional synthesis pathways. Exposures above approximately 423 K are excluded from synthesis: thermal destruction is experimentally supported, but an unsupported fixed decomposition-product allocation is not invented. Neither useful integrated yields nor selective L-glutamate production are claimed.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"water\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"H+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydron\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Proton\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Pyruvic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glyoxylic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6O6\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxy-4-oxopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydroxyketoglutaric acid; maloylformic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H4O5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2E)-4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Fumaroylformic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Oxalic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2O4/c3-1(4)2(5)6/h(H,3,4)(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6O5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Alpha-ketoglutaric acid; 2-oxoglutaric acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H6O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glutamic acid, racemic\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO4/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h3H,1-2,6H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8NO4-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-azaniumylpentanedioate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glutamate monoanion, racemic\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO4/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h3H,1-2,6H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)/p-1\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8O5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"2-Hydroxyglutaric acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeS\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(II) sulfide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Mackinawite\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"FeS2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(II) disulfide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Pyrite\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H7NO5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(hydroxyimino)pentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Alpha-ketoglutaric acid oxime\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Zero-valent iron\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe+2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iron(2+)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ferrous ion\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe/q+2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3HN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"prop-2-ynenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Cyanoacetylene\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3HN/c1-2-3-4/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_024\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H2N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2Z)-but-2-enedinitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Maleonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_025\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H4N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"butanedinitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Succinonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H4N2/c5-3-1-2-4-6/h1-2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_026\",\n      \"formula\": \"S8\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"cyclooctasulfur\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Elemental sulfur\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_027\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H5NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-oxobutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"3-Cyanopropanal; beta-cyanopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_028\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypentanedinitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"3-Cyanopropanal cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_029\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H7N3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopentanedinitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glutamate/glutamine aminodinitrile precursor\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_030\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"prop-2-enal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Acrolein\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O/c1-2-3-4/h2-3H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_031\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H7NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-oxopyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Pyroglutamic acid; 5-oxoproline\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"C5 skeleton construction by pyruvate\u2013glyoxylate aldol addition\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013323 K; proposed surface operating window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shaded pool or shallow sediment pore; pH 7\u20138. Start with concentrated C2/C3 substrates and monitor the C5 aldol-product pool. No Fe0, hydroxylamine, sulfide or nickel is introduced during this entry step. Concentrations and residence time require optimization; no isolated-step yield is assigned.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pyruvate enol/enolate attacks the aldehyde carbon of glyoxylate, forming the C\u2013C bond of hydroxyketoglutarate. The two feedstock carbon skeletons are retained without carbon loss.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pyruvate\u2013glyoxylate C5 assembly is supported by Muchowska et al. (2019) and Stubbs et al. (2020). Kaur et al. (2024) report that aldol condensation still occurs without a hydrogenation catalyst, supporting an entry that does not require an invented mineral catalyst. The proposed temperature window is an environmental adaptation, not a verbatim experimental recipe. Competing aldol products and inadequate feedstock concentration can limit flux; the network does not assume a selective self-organizing cycle.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Dehydration of hydroxyketoglutarate to fumaroylformate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Continue the shaded, anoxic aldol compartment at pH 7\u20138. Maintain water rather than drying the organic-acid mixture. Permit equilibration over hours to days as a proposed screening interval; measure the unsaturated C5 pool before transferring it to either reduction compartment.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"General acid\u2013base elimination of water from the beta-hydroxy ketoacid produces the conjugated unsaturated ketoacid. The E isomer is represented; geometric-isomer mixtures and reversible hydration may occur.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydroxyketoglutarate and fumaroylformate are intermediates in the C5 assembly sequence described by Muchowska et al. (2019), Stubbs et al. (2020) and Kaur et al. (2024). Resolving dehydration as its own node exposes a potentially low-flux intermediate rather than treating alpha-ketoglutarate formation as instantaneous. The supplied report does not establish an isolated uncatalyzed dehydration rate or E-isomer yield under this precise window; those parameters remain unmeasured.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Phosphate-assisted, glyoxylate-driven reduction to alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved orthophosphate buffer\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Orthophosphate, general acid\u2013base catalyst\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic phosphate-rich pool; pH 7. The reported whole-network benchmark uses 0.5 M phosphate and 21 h. Additional glyoxylate supplies reducing capacity. Retain a separate aliquot for carbon-product and oxalate analysis. Before metal-dependent downstream steps, cool and dilute into low-phosphate receiving water; this necessarily also dilutes alpha-ketoglutarate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formal coupled redox conversion: fumaroylformic acid + glyoxylic acid + water -> alpha-ketoglutaric acid + oxalic acid. Glyoxylate oxidation supplies the two reducing equivalents needed to saturate the C=C bond while retaining the keto group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Stubbs et al. (2020) support metal-free, glyoxylate-driven alpha-ketoglutarate formation and report approximately 5% alpha-ketoglutarate in the complete phosphate-buffered mixture, not a 5% isolated yield for this edge. The explicit oxalate-only redox closure is a chemically balanced modeling assumption that should be checked against primary product analysis; the supplied report does not establish the complete reductant-product distribution. High phosphate availability is a major geochemical limitation, especially with abundant Fe2+. Dilution is a physically specified transfer operation, not a claim of selective phosphate removal or preserved substrate concentration.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Nickel-catalyzed hydrogenation of fumaroylformate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni(0) supported on silica\u2013alumina\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"H2 partial pressure approximately 5 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni(0)/SiO2\u2013Al2O3\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cool the phosphate-free output of rxn_002 into a shaded, gas-retaining rock pore or sealed experimental analogue; pH approximately 8. A 10 mol% Ni loading and a 72 h observation window are experimental-network benchmarks, not established natural values. Do not add ammonia until the carbon-assembly stage has been assessed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface dissociation of H2 followed by conjugate hydrogen addition saturates the C=C bond of fumaroylformate. The desired channel preserves the alpha-keto group for subsequent nitrogen incorporation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) demonstrate H2/metal-dependent progression beyond the unsaturated aldol products and formation of downstream ketoacids. This node is a resolved mechanistic channel within that network, not a claim of a separately quantified pure-fumaroylformate experiment. Supported Ni is explicitly identified because H2 alone is ineffective. Natural Ni-bearing grains are possible analogues, but their active area and behavior cannot be equated automatically with a prepared supported catalyst. Carbonyl over-reduction and residual glyoxylate consumption remain competing processes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Nickel/H2 reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni(0) supported on silica\u2013alumina\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"H2 partial pressure approximately 5 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni(0)/SiO2\u2013Al2O3\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shaded, gas-retaining aqueous pore; pH 8. Published isolated-substrate benchmark: 30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM NH4HCO3, approximately 10 mol% Ni and 72 h. Lower ammoniacal-nitrogen concentrations were also examined. For rxn_004 material, introduce ammonia after carbon assembly. For rxn_003 material, cool and dilute into low-phosphate water first; do not assume the resulting solution still reaches the benchmark ketoacid concentration.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible condensation of the keto group with ammonia generates imine/iminium species, followed by Ni-mediated hydrogenation. Net: alpha-ketoglutaric acid + ammonia + H2 -> glutamic acid + water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Direct conversion of supplied alpha-ketoglutarate is supported by Kaur et al. (2024). Their broad yield ranges refer to multiple substrates and combined amino-acid/hydroxy-acid products, so no glutamate-specific yield is assigned here. Their integrated network did not detect glutamate: delayed ammonia addition and compartment transfer are proposals to test, not a demonstrated solution. Mayer and Moran (2022) identify alpha-ketoglutarate as intrinsically slow to reductively aminate. Catalyst poisoning, dilution and the explicitly represented hydroxyglutarate branch, rxn_018, constrain recovery.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional pyrite-coupled reductive amination on fresh FeS\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Freshly precipitated mackinawite, consumed as a reactive mineral reductant\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323\u2013348 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm in a sulfide-retaining sediment compartment\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, UV-shielded alkaline sediment pore; proposed pH 9\u201310 and total ammoniacal nitrogen approximately 0.375 M. Use freshly precipitated rather than aged FeS, with accessible dissolved sulfide. Proposed observation window: 24\u201372 h. Transfer cooled carbon-stage liquid by pore flow; dilute phosphate-rich feed before contact. If the preceding stage used supported Ni, retain those particles upstream by sediment filtration rather than assuming removal of dissolved species.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Idealized coupled net reaction: alpha-ketoglutaric acid + ammonia + FeS + H2S -> glutamic acid + water + FeS2. Oxidation of sulfide sulfur into disulfide sulfur supplies two reducing equivalents for imine reduction. FeS/H2S are consumable reagents, not an inexhaustible catalyst.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hafenbradl et al. (1995) motivate coupling reductive amination to pyrite formation, and Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003) support activity of fresh iron sulfide preparations. This particular receiving-compartment protocol and its quantitative electron closure remain conditional: pyrite formation and glutamate production must be measured together. The disputed approximately 50% fresh-FeS glutamate figure is not adopted. The greater-than-50% results with electrochemically reduced FeS in Kitadai et al. (2019) do not establish the performance of the fresh FeS used here. Concentrated ammonia, mineral aging, phosphate passivation and sulfide-mediated product loss are explicit limitations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Formation of the alpha-ketoglutarate oxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013323 K; proposed staged-formation window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shaded receiving pool; proposed pH 5.5\u20137. Add approximately stoichiometric hydroxylamine only after alpha-ketoglutarate formation. Do not introduce Fe0 yet. Assess oxime accumulation over an initial 1\u20136 h screening interval. For phosphate-derived material, dilution reduces phosphate carryover but also lowers substrate concentration.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine condenses with the keto carbonyl, eliminating water to form the C=N\u2013OH oxime. E/Z oxime forms are not distinguished because neither selective formation nor separate reactivity is established here.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska et al. (2019) report glutamate formation after hydroxylamine/Fe0 treatment of a ketoacid-producing network. Resolving oxime formation before iron addition is a chemically motivated staged adaptation, not a separately demonstrated protocol from that paper. Hydroxylamine availability is uncertain, and premature contact with Fe0 can consume it without productive carbonyl capture. Kaur et al. (2024) identify incompatibility of hydroxylamine/Fe0 with ketoacid-forming chemistry, which motivates temporal separation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Stoichiometric iron reduction of the alpha-ketoglutarate oxime\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_019\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fresh exposed zero-valent iron, consumed as reductant\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323\u2013343 K; proposed reduction window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shaded iron-bearing sediment pore; proposed pH 5\u20136. Introduce Fe0 only after confirming an oxime-containing feed. The formal minimum is two Fe atoms per oxime; additional iron may be lost to corrosion. Screen over approximately 2\u201324 h. Maintain acidity with the receiving fluid rather than assuming an unlimited proton supply.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reduction of the oxime includes C=N reduction and N\u2013O bond cleavage. Balanced net: oxime + 2 Fe0 + 4 H+ -> glutamic acid + 2 Fe2+ + water. The iron and proton coefficients are encoded in this net equation without repeating molecule IDs.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The hydroxylamine/Fe0 glutamate-producing chemistry of Muchowska et al. (2019) supports this reduction module. The separately accumulated-oxime schedule and selected pH window are proposed adaptations, not verified substrate-specific optima. Iron corrosion, residual hydroxylamine reduction and phosphate passivation can dominate electron consumption. Fe0 supply is more restrictive than Fe2+ abundance. The ferrous product is consistently represented by formula Fe+2 and InChI=1S/Fe/q+2.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Independent glycine production by nickel/H2 reductive amination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni(0) supported on silica\u2013alumina\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"H2 partial pressure approximately 5 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni(0)/SiO2\u2013Al2O3\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"A separate shaded, gas-retaining pore supplies the nitrogen-donor branch; pH 8. Use the Kaur et al. experimental family as a benchmark: ketoacid/glyoxylate concentrations of order 30 mM, ammoniacal nitrogen up to 150 mM and observation over 72 h. Retain supported Ni particles before transferring the glycine-containing liquid. Residual glyoxylate is not assumed absent.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glyoxylate forms an imine with ammonia, which is hydrogenated on nickel. Net: glyoxylic acid + ammonia + H2 -> glycine + water. Glycine is a consumed nitrogen donor in rxn_010, not an unexplained catalytic amine source.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) include glyoxylate in their metal/H2 amination chemistry, and Mayer and Moran (2022) find glyoxylate especially reactive toward reductive amination. An active hydrogenation catalyst is therefore specified rather than attributing reduction to H2 alone. Glycolate formation and incomplete conversion can contaminate this donor stream. No quantitative selectivity is assumed for the transferred mixture.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional glycine-to-alpha-ketoglutarate transamination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_021\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323\u2013343 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mix the independently prepared carbon and glycine streams in a shaded anoxic pool; proposed pH 7\u20138.5. Screen glycine in at least tenfold molar excess over alpha-ketoglutarate. Low glyoxylate activity is required; upstream carryover must be measured. No selective glyoxylate removal, Al3+ catalysis or efficient donor regeneration is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible imine-mediated amino-group transfer converts glycine into glyoxylate and alpha-ketoglutarate into glutamic acid. Net: alpha-ketoglutaric acid + glycine -> glutamic acid + glyoxylic acid. Together with rxn_009, the nitrogen relay has the same net reducing-equivalent requirement as reductive amination.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Stubbs et al. (2020) provide precedent for glycine-mediated transamination in alpha-ketoacid networks, but the supplied research does not establish an alpha-ketoglutarate-specific rate or yield under these conditions. This edge is therefore a speculative, substrate-specific extension rather than an asserted efficient glutamate synthesis. Mayer and Moran (2022) emphasize thermodynamic constraints on glutamate-centered nitrogen transfer. Excess glyoxylate from rxn_003 or rxn_009 can oppose the desired direction; failing to control that activity would invalidate this schedule. Al3+ is deliberately not invoked because compatibility with phosphate is unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrocyanation of cyanoacetylene to maleonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_023\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_024\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved Cu(I)-cyanide complexes\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013310 K; proposed environmental window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)-cyanide complexes\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic cyanide-bearing shallow pool; proposed pH 7\u20139. Supply cyanoacetylene and HCN before the sulfide-rich photoreduction stage. Control volatilization through short residence times and local replenishment. The supplied report does not provide a verified catalyst loading or step yield.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Copper-assisted addition of HCN across the activated carbon\u2013carbon triple bond constructs the C4 unsaturated dinitrile. Maleonitrile is the tracked product; alternative geometric isomers and side additions are not assumed absent.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) support the cyanoacetylene-to-maleonitrile entry into the glutamate-relevant nitrile branch. This reaction creates the required C4 precursor rather than assuming an abundant C4 aldehyde. Copper availability, cyanide complexation and controlled reagent delivery remain geochemical requirements. The chosen environmental pH and temperature window is a proposal informed by the chemistry, not a verified reproduction of supplementary experimental conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide-driven photoreduction of maleonitrile to succinonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_024\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_026\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II) cyanide-complex photoredox system\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) cyanide-complex photoredox system\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, optically shallow pool; near-neutral pH with controlled sulfide addition. The laboratory irradiation benchmark is 254 nm; a natural setting supplies a spectrum rather than monochromatic light. Monitor conversion and stop the first irradiation stage before extensive nitrile reduction. Retain soluble copper only where cyanide complexation can compete with copper-sulfide precipitation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photochemical electron/proton delivery from sulfide saturates the alkene. With S8 as the oxidized-sulfur end-member, the balanced aggregate is: 8 maleonitrile + 8 H2S -> 8 succinonitrile + S8. This is net bookkeeping, not an eight-substrate elementary event.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) explicitly describe reduction of maleonitrile to succinonitrile and photoredox acceleration involving copper. Ranjan et al. (2018) constrain achievable sulfide concentrations in surface waters. Sulfur may initially occur as polysulfides or colloidal material; S8 represents an anoxic terminal sulfur bookkeeping state, not a claim that it is the sole observed immediate coproduct. No unverified step yield is quoted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Selective photoreduction and hydrolysis of one succinonitrile nitrile group\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_027\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_026\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II) cyanide-complex photoredox system\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) cyanide-complex photoredox system\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Continue in a separately timed UV exposure stage with sulfide replenishment; near-neutral pH and a 254 nm experimental benchmark. Monitor aldehyde accumulation and stop irradiation before substantial reduction of the remaining nitrile. Transfer promptly into a shaded HCN-trapping compartment; no indefinite aldehyde storage is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"One nitrile undergoes two-electron reduction to an imine, followed by hydrolysis to the aldehyde and ammonia. Balanced aggregate with S8 bookkeeping: 8 succinonitrile + 8 H2S + 8 water -> 8 3-cyanopropanal + 8 ammonia + S8.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) explicitly identify succinonitrile-to-semialdehyde conversion, making this the strongest supplied evidence for constructing the needed C4 aldehyde. This is a major selectivity bottleneck: continued irradiation can consume the second nitrile or damage aldehyde product. The supplied report does not verify the per-step yield or oxidized-sulfur distribution. Sulfide supply and temporally controlled irradiation remain unresolved environmental requirements, as discussed by Ranjan et al. (2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin trapping of 3-cyanopropanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_027\", \"mol_023\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_028\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shaded receiving pool; proposed pH 7\u20139. Add or encounter HCN after the aldehyde-producing irradiation stage. Stop further UV exposure and avoid adding additional sulfide. Transfer by local water flow is proposed; copper and dissolved sulfide carryover must be assessed rather than assumed removed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide addition to the aldehyde carbonyl followed by proton transfer gives the cyanohydrin. The added cyanide carbon becomes the future alpha-carboxyl carbon of glutamate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) explicitly report HCN addition to the semialdehyde to form the cyanohydrin. No mineral catalyst is required by this reaction class. Cyanohydrin formation can stabilize the otherwise reactive aldehyde pool, but equilibrium, HCN dilution and reactions with residual sulfide may reduce the amount available for nitrogen incorporation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-dependent conversion of the cyanohydrin to the aminodinitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_028\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_029\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shaded ammonia-bearing pool; proposed pH 8\u20139. Use an exploratory total ammoniacal-nitrogen range of 0.05\u20130.5 M, explicitly not a verified Patel et al. recipe. Supply ammonia after cyanohydrin formation. Avoid continued irradiation and unnecessary heating.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanohydrin\u2013aldehyde equilibration permits imine formation with ammonia and cyanide trapping, giving 2-aminopentanedinitrile. The net substitution does not imply a simple direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) explicitly describe ammonia addition producing the aminonitrile precursor of glutamine and glutamic acid. This is evidence for precursor formation, not proof of quantitative final glutamate recovery. Concentrated ammonia and sequential additions are significant environmental assumptions. Formation without a chiral influence is expected to yield a racemic alpha center.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Exhaustive hydrolysis of both aminodinitrile groups\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_029\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide supplied by a concentrated alkaline brine\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"363\u2013370 K; proposed hydrolysis window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide, a pH-maintained hydrolysis promoter\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, UV-shielded alkaline geothermal or evaporitic pool; proposed pH 11.5\u201312.5 with continuous liquid water. Screen residence times of days to weeks. This is a proposed natural analogue of exhaustive hydrolysis, not a measured glutamate-yield protocol. After treatment, cool and mix into near-neutral water for rxn_021. Detect remaining nitriles, amides and pyroglutamate before claiming complete conversion.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis converts both nitrile carbons into carboxyl groups. Balanced neutral-parent net: aminodinitrile + 4 water -> glutamic acid + 2 ammonia. Partially hydrolyzed aminoamides, including glutamine-related products, can accumulate; this node is explicitly a multistep net hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) support the identity of the glutamate/glutamine precursor, but the supplied research does not establish exhaustive free-glutamate recovery under this proposed alkaline window. The edge is therefore a chemically justified but experimentally unclosed downstream module. Harsh hydrolysis requirements in precursor studies are discussed by Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013). Strong alkalinity is a local geochemical requirement, and hot treatment may cause side reactions. A precursor detection must not be reported as equivalent to a measured free-glutamate yield.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Proposed conjugate hydrocyanation of acrolein\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_030\", \"mol_023\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_027\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013310 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, shaded aqueous pool receiving acrolein and HCN; proposed pH 8\u20139. Use short, monitored residence times to assess competing acrolein polymerization and carbonyl addition. Do not infer productive conjugate addition from disappearance of acrolein alone.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The desired 1,4-addition of cyanide to the acrolein beta carbon, followed by proton transfer, produces 3-cyanopropanal. Competing 1,2-carbonyl addition produces a different cyanohydrin and does not establish the desired C4 aldehyde branch.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Van Trump and Miller (1972) proposed acrolein as a precursor relevant to glutamic acid, but did not demonstrate a selective acrolein-to-glutamate sequence. This edge is explicitly speculative in the prebiotic setting. Industrial aldehyde/Strecker chemistry establishes chemical possibility under other conditions, not the performance of this uncatalyzed environmental window. A useful yield and selectivity for 3-cyanopropanal must be measured before pathway P10 can be regarded as established.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing nickel-catalyzed reduction to 2-hydroxyglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni(0) supported on silica\u2013alumina\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"H2 partial pressure approximately 5 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni(0)/SiO2\u2013Al2O3\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Same shaded aqueous pore and pH approximately 8 as the nickel reductive-amination stage. Ammonia may be present but is not consumed in this competing reaction. Track hydroxyglutarate alongside glutamate over the 72 h experimental benchmark interval.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nickel-activated hydrogen reduces the keto carbonyl directly to a secondary alcohol instead of reducing an ammonia-derived imine. Net: alpha-ketoglutaric acid + H2 -> 2-hydroxyglutaric acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) report amino-acid/hydroxy-acid mixtures during metal/H2 treatment of ketoacids. This is a supported competing reduction, not an uncatalyzed sink. An active Ni catalyst is explicitly specified. The broad approximately 1:1 amino/hydroxy product tendency reported for Ni-based systems is not assigned as an exact alpha-ketoglutarate-specific ratio. No unsupported efficient recycling of hydroxyglutarate is included.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Glutamate cyclization to the pyroglutamate reservoir\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_031\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium, promoting acid-catalyzed cyclization\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333\u2013370 K; proposed warm-pool sink window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic acidic pool or acidified margin; pH approximately 2\u20133.5. Prolonged heating and concentration increase concern, so productive streams should normally be cooled and neutralized rather than routed here. This node evaluates loss during accidental or environmental acidification; it is not a required synthesis step.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Intramolecular attack of the amino group on the side-chain carboxyl group forms a five-membered lactam with loss of water. Net: glutamic acid -> pyroglutamic acid + water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glutamate cyclization and thermal loss are supported by Lee et al. (2014), with solid-state behavior also documented by Wu et al. (2010) and Weiss et al. (2018). Those results do not establish a numerical free-glutamate rate throughout this lower-temperature aqueous window. In particular, the peptide-bound upper-bound rate discussed in Seifert et al. (2009) is not applied to free glutamate. The sink requires no molecular oxygen. More severe hydrothermal decomposition is avoided rather than represented by an unverified fixed product allocation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_020\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional harsh hydrolysis of the pyroglutamate reservoir\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_031\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide supplied by a strongly alkaline brine\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"363\u2013370 K; proposed recovery-screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide, a pH-maintained lactam-hydrolysis promoter\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Transfer into a separate anoxic, UV-shielded caustic pool; proposed pH 12\u201313 with liquid water maintained for days to weeks. Such strongly alkaline warm compartments are a restrictive boundary condition. Cool and neutralize the recovered material afterward. Neither rapid ambient recovery nor quantitative recycling is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide-promoted lactam opening regenerates the open-chain amino dicarboxylate pool. Neutral-parent bookkeeping: pyroglutamic acid + water -> glutamic acid. Strongly basic conditions favor the ring-opened carboxylate form, but side reactions and incomplete conversion must be measured.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pizzarello and Cooper (2001) support treating pyroglutamate as an acid-labile glutamate reservoir, not exclusively an irreversible loss. They do not validate this proposed alkaline environmental protocol. The alkaline edge is therefore explicitly speculative, justified by standard lactam-hydrolysis chemistry, and included to expose the substantial treatment required for recovery. It is not counted as an independent synthesis pathway and cannot be used to assume that cyclization losses are automatically reversible under ordinary pool conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_021\",\n      \"name\": \"Final acid\u2013base equilibration to the glutamate monoanion\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cool the product stream and mix into an anoxic, shaded receiving pool buffered at pH 6.5\u20138.5. Let entrained mineral particles settle where present. This operation changes protonation but does not separate glutamate from other dissolved organics. Acidic or highly alkaline upstream solutions must actually be buffered to the receiving pH.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formal neutral-parent dissociation: glutamic acid -> glutamate monoanion + H+. Internal proton redistribution gives the dominant near-neutral form with a protonated amino group and two carboxylate groups. The formal equation does not imply that neutral glutamic acid predominates in the upstream reaction mixtures.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is standard acid\u2013base bookkeeping rather than a new carbon- or nitrogen-forming reaction. Near-neutral collection is consistent with the pH-sensitive reaction and stability constraints discussed by Kaur et al. (2024) and Lee et al. (2014). Protonation must be determined from the receiving pH; a broad environment label alone is insufficient. Cooling and avoiding strongly acidic residence limit, but do not eliminate, subsequent pyroglutamate formation.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate-driven C5 reduction followed by nickel/H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Build the C5 skeleton, reduce its alkene in the phosphate/glyoxylate compartment, then cool and dilute into a separate Ni/H2/ammonia compartment before near-neutral collection. The component chemistries have literature support, but phosphate carryover, dilution and integrated glutamate recovery remain unvalidated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate-driven C5 reduction followed by pyrite-coupled amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Transfer the phosphate-generated alpha-ketoglutarate pool, after dilution, into warm fresh-FeS/sulfide/ammonia sediment. This is conditional on observing coupled pyrite and glutamate formation; results obtained using electrochemically reduced FeS are not substituted for evidence about fresh FeS.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate-driven C5 reduction followed by staged oxime reduction\",\n      \"description\": \"Generate alpha-ketoglutarate before adding hydroxylamine. Accumulate an oxime-containing fraction, then introduce Fe0 under separately controlled acidity. Hydroxylamine availability, phosphate passivation and competing iron corrosion are central limitations; simultaneous upstream addition is not assumed workable.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxylate-driven C5 reduction with an independently prepared glycine donor\",\n      \"description\": \"Prepare the alpha-ketoglutarate branch and a separate Ni/H2-derived glycine stream, then combine them for nonenzymatic transamination. Both branches are included in the executable schedule. This is a lower-confidence route because alpha-ketoglutarate-specific transamination performance is unverified and glyoxylate carryover can oppose net glutamate formation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_021\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction followed by delayed nickel/H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Use supported Ni/H2 to generate the alpha-ketoglutarate pool without introducing the high-phosphate reduction stage, then introduce ammonia after evaluating carbon assembly. Delayed dosing distinguishes the proposed schedule from an assumed successful one-pot synthesis; Kaur et al. did not detect glutamate in their integrated network.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction followed by fresh-FeS amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Generate alpha-ketoglutarate in a cool H2/Ni pore, retain supported catalyst particles upstream, and transfer the liquid into a warmer sulfide-bearing sediment compartment. This separates the metal-hydrogenation and sulfide stages physically, but dissolved carryover and fresh-FeS electron coupling still require experimental validation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction followed by hydroxylamine/iron chemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"Supply alpha-ketoglutarate through the Ni/H2 carbon branch, retain supported Ni solids, and form the oxime before adding stoichiometric Fe0. The route avoids exposing the skeleton-forming mixture directly to hydroxylamine/Fe0, but selective oxime accumulation and net glutamate recovery after transfer are proposed rather than demonstrated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction with a glycine nitrogen relay\",\n      \"description\": \"Prepare alpha-ketoglutarate and glycine in separate H2/Ni compartments, retain catalyst particles, and mix the donor and acceptor streams for transamination. Glycine formation supplies the nitrogen donor and reducing-equivalent accounting explicitly. The final transamination remains conditional on favorable concentrations and sufficiently low glyoxylate activity.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_021\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanoacetylene\u2013cyanosulfidic construction followed by Strecker-type chemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"Construct maleonitrile from cyanoacetylene and HCN, photoreduce sequentially to succinonitrile and the C4 aldehyde, then trap the aldehyde as a cyanohydrin and aminodinitrile. Finish with a separately staged exhaustive-hydrolysis treatment and near-neutral collection. The precursor sequence is strongly supported; useful free-glutamate recovery under the proposed hydrolysis window is not established.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_024\", \"mol_025\", \"mol_027\", \"mol_028\", \"mol_029\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Acrolein hydrocyanation followed by the aminodinitrile route\",\n      \"description\": \"Use proposed conjugate HCN addition to acrolein as an alternative C4-aldehyde source, then follow cyanohydrin formation, ammonia incorporation, exhaustive hydrolysis and near-neutral collection. This pathway is explicitly speculative at its entry step: competing carbonyl addition and polymerization may prevent useful 3-cyanopropanal production.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_017\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_027\", \"mol_028\", \"mol_029\", \"mol_011\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_010\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_015\",\n    \"mol_023\",\n    \"mol_027\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_018\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_027\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_017\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_020\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_019\", \"rxn_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_015\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Glutamate, racemic", "overall_strategy": "Construct a connected network containing 31 chemical-species nodes, 21 reaction nodes and 10 distinct target-reaching reaction schedules. Eight schedules combine two chemically different routes for reducing the C5 unsaturated ketoacid precursor with four nitrogen-incorporation routes. Two additional schedules construct glutamate through a C4 aldehyde and a dinitrile. These are conditional synthesis proposals, not ten independently demonstrated end-to-end prebiotic syntheses. Initial pyruvate, glyoxylate, cyanoacetylene, acrolein and inorganic reagents are stipulated feedstocks; their geochemical availability remains a boundary condition. Reaction conditions distinguish published benchmarks from proposed operating windows. Physical staging is required: irradiated sulfide chemistry, phosphate-rich carbon assembly, metal hydrogenation and hydrolysis are not assumed compatible in one vessel. Biochemical denotes nonenzymatic aqueous chemistry in specified compartments, including cool H2-pressurized rock pores, not biological catalysis. All compartments are anoxic; nonphotochemical reactions occur beneath sediment, in shaded pools or inside rock pores despite strong surface UV. Organic acids are represented by neutral parent structures for net-reaction bookkeeping, with explicit final equilibration to the glutamate monoanion. Actual protonation depends on receiving pH. Each environment_formed tag identifies a representative formation environment, not an exclusive one. Because the schema lacks a byproduct or sink role, mineral products, oxalate and hydroxyglutarate are labeled intermediate. Pyroglutamate formation and difficult recovery are included but are not counted as additional synthesis pathways. Exposures above approximately 423 K are excluded from synthesis: thermal destruction is experimentally supported, but an unsupported fixed decomposition-product allocation is not invented. Neither useful integrated yields nor selective L-glutamate production are claimed.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "water", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "Hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "hydron", "common_name": "Proton", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "Pyruvic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C2H2O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxoacetic acid", "common_name": "Glyoxylic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C5H6O6", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxy-4-oxopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "Hydroxyketoglutaric acid; maloylformic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C5H4O5", "iupac_name": "(2E)-4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid", "common_name": "Fumaroylformic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C2H2O4", "iupac_name": "ethanedioic acid", "common_name": "Oxalic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O4/c3-1(4)2(5)6/h(H,3,4)(H,5,6)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C5H6O5", "iupac_name": "2-oxopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "Alpha-ketoglutaric acid; 2-oxoglutaric acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H6O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C5H9NO4", "iupac_name": "2-aminopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "Glutamic acid, racemic", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO4/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h3H,1-2,6H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C5H8NO4-", "iupac_name": "2-azaniumylpentanedioate", "common_name": "Glutamate monoanion, racemic", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO4/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h3H,1-2,6H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)/p-1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C5H8O5", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypentanedioic acid", "common_name": "2-Hydroxyglutaric acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "FeS", "iupac_name": "iron(II) sulfide", "common_name": "Mackinawite", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "FeS2", "iupac_name": "iron(II) disulfide", "common_name": "Pyrite", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "H3NO", "iupac_name": "hydroxylamine", "common_name": "Hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C5H7NO5", "iupac_name": "2-(hydroxyimino)pentanedioic acid", "common_name": "Alpha-ketoglutaric acid oxime", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "Fe", "iupac_name": "iron", "common_name": "Zero-valent iron", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "Fe+2", "iupac_name": "iron(2+)", "common_name": "Ferrous ion", "inchi": "InChI=1S/Fe/q+2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetic acid", "common_name": "Glycine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "C3HN", "iupac_name": "prop-2-ynenitrile", "common_name": "Cyanoacetylene", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3HN/c1-2-3-4/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_024", "formula": "C4H2N2", "iupac_name": "(2Z)-but-2-enedinitrile", "common_name": "Maleonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_025", "formula": "C4H4N2", "iupac_name": "butanedinitrile", "common_name": "Succinonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H4N2/c5-3-1-2-4-6/h1-2H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_026", "formula": "S8", "iupac_name": "cyclooctasulfur", "common_name": "Elemental sulfur", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_027", "formula": "C4H5NO", "iupac_name": "4-oxobutanenitrile", "common_name": "3-Cyanopropanal; beta-cyanopropionaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_028", "formula": "C5H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypentanedinitrile", "common_name": "3-Cyanopropanal cyanohydrin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_029", "formula": "C5H7N3", "iupac_name": "2-aminopentanedinitrile", "common_name": "Glutamate/glutamine aminodinitrile precursor", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_030", "formula": "C3H4O", "iupac_name": "prop-2-enal", "common_name": "Acrolein", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O/c1-2-3-4/h2-3H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_031", "formula": "C5H7NO3", "iupac_name": "5-oxopyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "Pyroglutamic acid; 5-oxoproline", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "C5 skeleton construction by pyruvate\u2013glyoxylate aldol addition", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300\u2013323 K; proposed surface operating window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shaded pool or shallow sediment pore; pH 7\u20138. Start with concentrated C2/C3 substrates and monitor the C5 aldol-product pool. No Fe0, hydroxylamine, sulfide or nickel is introduced during this entry step. Concentrations and residence time require optimization; no isolated-step yield is assigned."}, "mechanism": "Pyruvate enol/enolate attacks the aldehyde carbon of glyoxylate, forming the C\u2013C bond of hydroxyketoglutarate. The two feedstock carbon skeletons are retained without carbon loss.", "reasoning": "Pyruvate\u2013glyoxylate C5 assembly is supported by Muchowska et al. (2019) and Stubbs et al. (2020). Kaur et al. (2024) report that aldol condensation still occurs without a hydrogenation catalyst, supporting an entry that does not require an invented mineral catalyst. The proposed temperature window is an environmental adaptation, not a verbatim experimental recipe. Competing aldol products and inadequate feedstock concentration can limit flux; the network does not assume a selective self-organizing cycle."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Dehydration of hydroxyketoglutarate to fumaroylformate", "inputs": ["mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300\u2013323 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Continue the shaded, anoxic aldol compartment at pH 7\u20138. Maintain water rather than drying the organic-acid mixture. Permit equilibration over hours to days as a proposed screening interval; measure the unsaturated C5 pool before transferring it to either reduction compartment."}, "mechanism": "General acid\u2013base elimination of water from the beta-hydroxy ketoacid produces the conjugated unsaturated ketoacid. The E isomer is represented; geometric-isomer mixtures and reversible hydration may occur.", "reasoning": "Hydroxyketoglutarate and fumaroylformate are intermediates in the C5 assembly sequence described by Muchowska et al. (2019), Stubbs et al. (2020) and Kaur et al. (2024). Resolving dehydration as its own node exposes a potentially low-flux intermediate rather than treating alpha-ketoglutarate formation as instantaneous. The supplied report does not establish an isolated uncatalyzed dehydration rate or E-isomer yield under this precise window; those parameters remain unmeasured."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Phosphate-assisted, glyoxylate-driven reduction to alpha-ketoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_006", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved orthophosphate buffer"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Orthophosphate, general acid\u2013base catalyst", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic phosphate-rich pool; pH 7. The reported whole-network benchmark uses 0.5 M phosphate and 21 h. Additional glyoxylate supplies reducing capacity. Retain a separate aliquot for carbon-product and oxalate analysis. Before metal-dependent downstream steps, cool and dilute into low-phosphate receiving water; this necessarily also dilutes alpha-ketoglutarate."}, "mechanism": "Formal coupled redox conversion: fumaroylformic acid + glyoxylic acid + water -> alpha-ketoglutaric acid + oxalic acid. Glyoxylate oxidation supplies the two reducing equivalents needed to saturate the C=C bond while retaining the keto group.", "reasoning": "Stubbs et al. (2020) support metal-free, glyoxylate-driven alpha-ketoglutarate formation and report approximately 5% alpha-ketoglutarate in the complete phosphate-buffered mixture, not a 5% isolated yield for this edge. The explicit oxalate-only redox closure is a chemically balanced modeling assumption that should be checked against primary product analysis; the supplied report does not establish the complete reductant-product distribution. High phosphate availability is a major geochemical limitation, especially with abundant Fe2+. Dilution is a physically specified transfer operation, not a claim of selective phosphate removal or preserved substrate concentration."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Nickel-catalyzed hydrogenation of fumaroylformate", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni(0) supported on silica\u2013alumina"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "H2 partial pressure approximately 5 bar", "catalyst": "Ni(0)/SiO2\u2013Al2O3", "aqueous": true, "other": "Cool the phosphate-free output of rxn_002 into a shaded, gas-retaining rock pore or sealed experimental analogue; pH approximately 8. A 10 mol% Ni loading and a 72 h observation window are experimental-network benchmarks, not established natural values. Do not add ammonia until the carbon-assembly stage has been assessed."}, "mechanism": "Surface dissociation of H2 followed by conjugate hydrogen addition saturates the C=C bond of fumaroylformate. The desired channel preserves the alpha-keto group for subsequent nitrogen incorporation.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) demonstrate H2/metal-dependent progression beyond the unsaturated aldol products and formation of downstream ketoacids. This node is a resolved mechanistic channel within that network, not a claim of a separately quantified pure-fumaroylformate experiment. Supported Ni is explicitly identified because H2 alone is ineffective. Natural Ni-bearing grains are possible analogues, but their active area and behavior cannot be equated automatically with a prepared supported catalyst. Carbonyl over-reduction and residual glyoxylate consumption remain competing processes."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Nickel/H2 reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni(0) supported on silica\u2013alumina"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "H2 partial pressure approximately 5 bar", "catalyst": "Ni(0)/SiO2\u2013Al2O3", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shaded, gas-retaining aqueous pore; pH 8. Published isolated-substrate benchmark: 30 mM ketoacid, 150 mM NH4HCO3, approximately 10 mol% Ni and 72 h. Lower ammoniacal-nitrogen concentrations were also examined. For rxn_004 material, introduce ammonia after carbon assembly. For rxn_003 material, cool and dilute into low-phosphate water first; do not assume the resulting solution still reaches the benchmark ketoacid concentration."}, "mechanism": "Reversible condensation of the keto group with ammonia generates imine/iminium species, followed by Ni-mediated hydrogenation. Net: alpha-ketoglutaric acid + ammonia + H2 -> glutamic acid + water.", "reasoning": "Direct conversion of supplied alpha-ketoglutarate is supported by Kaur et al. (2024). Their broad yield ranges refer to multiple substrates and combined amino-acid/hydroxy-acid products, so no glutamate-specific yield is assigned here. Their integrated network did not detect glutamate: delayed ammonia addition and compartment transfer are proposals to test, not a demonstrated solution. Mayer and Moran (2022) identify alpha-ketoglutarate as intrinsically slow to reductively aminate. Catalyst poisoning, dilution and the explicitly represented hydroxyglutarate branch, rxn_018, constrain recovery."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Conditional pyrite-coupled reductive amination on fresh FeS", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003", "mol_014", "mol_015"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001", "mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Freshly precipitated mackinawite, consumed as a reactive mineral reductant"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323\u2013348 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm in a sulfide-retaining sediment compartment", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, UV-shielded alkaline sediment pore; proposed pH 9\u201310 and total ammoniacal nitrogen approximately 0.375 M. Use freshly precipitated rather than aged FeS, with accessible dissolved sulfide. Proposed observation window: 24\u201372 h. Transfer cooled carbon-stage liquid by pore flow; dilute phosphate-rich feed before contact. If the preceding stage used supported Ni, retain those particles upstream by sediment filtration rather than assuming removal of dissolved species."}, "mechanism": "Idealized coupled net reaction: alpha-ketoglutaric acid + ammonia + FeS + H2S -> glutamic acid + water + FeS2. Oxidation of sulfide sulfur into disulfide sulfur supplies two reducing equivalents for imine reduction. FeS/H2S are consumable reagents, not an inexhaustible catalyst.", "reasoning": "Hafenbradl et al. (1995) motivate coupling reductive amination to pyrite formation, and Huber and W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003) support activity of fresh iron sulfide preparations. This particular receiving-compartment protocol and its quantitative electron closure remain conditional: pyrite formation and glutamate production must be measured together. The disputed approximately 50% fresh-FeS glutamate figure is not adopted. The greater-than-50% results with electrochemically reduced FeS in Kitadai et al. (2019) do not establish the performance of the fresh FeS used here. Concentrated ammonia, mineral aging, phosphate passivation and sulfide-mediated product loss are explicit limitations."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Formation of the alpha-ketoglutarate oxime", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_017"], "outputs": ["mol_018", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300\u2013323 K; proposed staged-formation window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shaded receiving pool; proposed pH 5.5\u20137. Add approximately stoichiometric hydroxylamine only after alpha-ketoglutarate formation. Do not introduce Fe0 yet. Assess oxime accumulation over an initial 1\u20136 h screening interval. For phosphate-derived material, dilution reduces phosphate carryover but also lowers substrate concentration."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxylamine condenses with the keto carbonyl, eliminating water to form the C=N\u2013OH oxime. E/Z oxime forms are not distinguished because neither selective formation nor separate reactivity is established here.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019) report glutamate formation after hydroxylamine/Fe0 treatment of a ketoacid-producing network. Resolving oxime formation before iron addition is a chemically motivated staged adaptation, not a separately demonstrated protocol from that paper. Hydroxylamine availability is uncertain, and premature contact with Fe0 can consume it without productive carbonyl capture. Kaur et al. (2024) identify incompatibility of hydroxylamine/Fe0 with ketoacid-forming chemistry, which motivates temporal separation."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Stoichiometric iron reduction of the alpha-ketoglutarate oxime", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_019", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_020", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Fresh exposed zero-valent iron, consumed as reductant"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323\u2013343 K; proposed reduction window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shaded iron-bearing sediment pore; proposed pH 5\u20136. Introduce Fe0 only after confirming an oxime-containing feed. The formal minimum is two Fe atoms per oxime; additional iron may be lost to corrosion. Screen over approximately 2\u201324 h. Maintain acidity with the receiving fluid rather than assuming an unlimited proton supply."}, "mechanism": "Reduction of the oxime includes C=N reduction and N\u2013O bond cleavage. Balanced net: oxime + 2 Fe0 + 4 H+ -> glutamic acid + 2 Fe2+ + water. The iron and proton coefficients are encoded in this net equation without repeating molecule IDs.", "reasoning": "The hydroxylamine/Fe0 glutamate-producing chemistry of Muchowska et al. (2019) supports this reduction module. The separately accumulated-oxime schedule and selected pH window are proposed adaptations, not verified substrate-specific optima. Iron corrosion, residual hydroxylamine reduction and phosphate passivation can dominate electron consumption. Fe0 supply is more restrictive than Fe2+ abundance. The ferrous product is consistently represented by formula Fe+2 and InChI=1S/Fe/q+2."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Independent glycine production by nickel/H2 reductive amination", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_003", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni(0) supported on silica\u2013alumina"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "H2 partial pressure approximately 5 bar", "catalyst": "Ni(0)/SiO2\u2013Al2O3", "aqueous": true, "other": "A separate shaded, gas-retaining pore supplies the nitrogen-donor branch; pH 8. Use the Kaur et al. experimental family as a benchmark: ketoacid/glyoxylate concentrations of order 30 mM, ammoniacal nitrogen up to 150 mM and observation over 72 h. Retain supported Ni particles before transferring the glycine-containing liquid. Residual glyoxylate is not assumed absent."}, "mechanism": "Glyoxylate forms an imine with ammonia, which is hydrogenated on nickel. Net: glyoxylic acid + ammonia + H2 -> glycine + water. Glycine is a consumed nitrogen donor in rxn_010, not an unexplained catalytic amine source.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) include glyoxylate in their metal/H2 amination chemistry, and Mayer and Moran (2022) find glyoxylate especially reactive toward reductive amination. An active hydrogenation catalyst is therefore specified rather than attributing reduction to H2 alone. Glycolate formation and incomplete conversion can contaminate this donor stream. No quantitative selectivity is assumed for the transferred mixture."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Conditional glycine-to-alpha-ketoglutarate transamination", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_021"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "323\u2013343 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Mix the independently prepared carbon and glycine streams in a shaded anoxic pool; proposed pH 7\u20138.5. Screen glycine in at least tenfold molar excess over alpha-ketoglutarate. Low glyoxylate activity is required; upstream carryover must be measured. No selective glyoxylate removal, Al3+ catalysis or efficient donor regeneration is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Reversible imine-mediated amino-group transfer converts glycine into glyoxylate and alpha-ketoglutarate into glutamic acid. Net: alpha-ketoglutaric acid + glycine -> glutamic acid + glyoxylic acid. Together with rxn_009, the nitrogen relay has the same net reducing-equivalent requirement as reductive amination.", "reasoning": "Stubbs et al. (2020) provide precedent for glycine-mediated transamination in alpha-ketoacid networks, but the supplied research does not establish an alpha-ketoglutarate-specific rate or yield under these conditions. This edge is therefore a speculative, substrate-specific extension rather than an asserted efficient glutamate synthesis. Mayer and Moran (2022) emphasize thermodynamic constraints on glutamate-centered nitrogen transfer. Excess glyoxylate from rxn_003 or rxn_009 can oppose the desired direction; failing to control that activity would invalidate this schedule. Al3+ is deliberately not invoked because compatibility with phosphate is unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Hydrocyanation of cyanoacetylene to maleonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_022", "mol_023"], "outputs": ["mol_024"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved Cu(I)-cyanide complexes"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300\u2013310 K; proposed environmental window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)-cyanide complexes", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic cyanide-bearing shallow pool; proposed pH 7\u20139. Supply cyanoacetylene and HCN before the sulfide-rich photoreduction stage. Control volatilization through short residence times and local replenishment. The supplied report does not provide a verified catalyst loading or step yield."}, "mechanism": "Copper-assisted addition of HCN across the activated carbon\u2013carbon triple bond constructs the C4 unsaturated dinitrile. Maleonitrile is the tracked product; alternative geometric isomers and side additions are not assumed absent.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) support the cyanoacetylene-to-maleonitrile entry into the glutamate-relevant nitrile branch. This reaction creates the required C4 precursor rather than assuming an abundant C4 aldehyde. Copper availability, cyanide complexation and controlled reagent delivery remain geochemical requirements. The chosen environmental pH and temperature window is a proposal informed by the chemistry, not a verified reproduction of supplementary experimental conditions."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Sulfide-driven photoreduction of maleonitrile to succinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_024", "mol_015"], "outputs": ["mol_025", "mol_026"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)/Cu(II) cyanide-complex photoredox system"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300\u2013310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)/Cu(II) cyanide-complex photoredox system", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, optically shallow pool; near-neutral pH with controlled sulfide addition. The laboratory irradiation benchmark is 254 nm; a natural setting supplies a spectrum rather than monochromatic light. Monitor conversion and stop the first irradiation stage before extensive nitrile reduction. Retain soluble copper only where cyanide complexation can compete with copper-sulfide precipitation."}, "mechanism": "Photochemical electron/proton delivery from sulfide saturates the alkene. With S8 as the oxidized-sulfur end-member, the balanced aggregate is: 8 maleonitrile + 8 H2S -> 8 succinonitrile + S8. This is net bookkeeping, not an eight-substrate elementary event.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) explicitly describe reduction of maleonitrile to succinonitrile and photoredox acceleration involving copper. Ranjan et al. (2018) constrain achievable sulfide concentrations in surface waters. Sulfur may initially occur as polysulfides or colloidal material; S8 represents an anoxic terminal sulfur bookkeeping state, not a claim that it is the sole observed immediate coproduct. No unverified step yield is quoted."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Selective photoreduction and hydrolysis of one succinonitrile nitrile group", "inputs": ["mol_025", "mol_015", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_027", "mol_003", "mol_026"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)/Cu(II) cyanide-complex photoredox system"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300\u2013310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)/Cu(II) cyanide-complex photoredox system", "aqueous": true, "other": "Continue in a separately timed UV exposure stage with sulfide replenishment; near-neutral pH and a 254 nm experimental benchmark. Monitor aldehyde accumulation and stop irradiation before substantial reduction of the remaining nitrile. Transfer promptly into a shaded HCN-trapping compartment; no indefinite aldehyde storage is assumed."}, "mechanism": "One nitrile undergoes two-electron reduction to an imine, followed by hydrolysis to the aldehyde and ammonia. Balanced aggregate with S8 bookkeeping: 8 succinonitrile + 8 H2S + 8 water -> 8 3-cyanopropanal + 8 ammonia + S8.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) explicitly identify succinonitrile-to-semialdehyde conversion, making this the strongest supplied evidence for constructing the needed C4 aldehyde. This is a major selectivity bottleneck: continued irradiation can consume the second nitrile or damage aldehyde product. The supplied report does not verify the per-step yield or oxidized-sulfur distribution. Sulfide supply and temporally controlled irradiation remain unresolved environmental requirements, as discussed by Ranjan et al. (2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Cyanohydrin trapping of 3-cyanopropanal", "inputs": ["mol_027", "mol_023"], "outputs": ["mol_028"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300\u2013310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shaded receiving pool; proposed pH 7\u20139. Add or encounter HCN after the aldehyde-producing irradiation stage. Stop further UV exposure and avoid adding additional sulfide. Transfer by local water flow is proposed; copper and dissolved sulfide carryover must be assessed rather than assumed removed."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide addition to the aldehyde carbonyl followed by proton transfer gives the cyanohydrin. The added cyanide carbon becomes the future alpha-carboxyl carbon of glutamate.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) explicitly report HCN addition to the semialdehyde to form the cyanohydrin. No mineral catalyst is required by this reaction class. Cyanohydrin formation can stabilize the otherwise reactive aldehyde pool, but equilibrium, HCN dilution and reactions with residual sulfide may reduce the amount available for nitrogen incorporation."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Ammonia-dependent conversion of the cyanohydrin to the aminodinitrile", "inputs": ["mol_028", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_029", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300\u2013320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shaded ammonia-bearing pool; proposed pH 8\u20139. Use an exploratory total ammoniacal-nitrogen range of 0.05\u20130.5 M, explicitly not a verified Patel et al. recipe. Supply ammonia after cyanohydrin formation. Avoid continued irradiation and unnecessary heating."}, "mechanism": "Cyanohydrin\u2013aldehyde equilibration permits imine formation with ammonia and cyanide trapping, giving 2-aminopentanedinitrile. The net substitution does not imply a simple direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) explicitly describe ammonia addition producing the aminonitrile precursor of glutamine and glutamic acid. This is evidence for precursor formation, not proof of quantitative final glutamate recovery. Concentrated ammonia and sequential additions are significant environmental assumptions. Formation without a chiral influence is expected to yield a racemic alpha center."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Exhaustive hydrolysis of both aminodinitrile groups", "inputs": ["mol_029", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide supplied by a concentrated alkaline brine"], "conditions": {"temperature": "363\u2013370 K; proposed hydrolysis window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydroxide, a pH-maintained hydrolysis promoter", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, UV-shielded alkaline geothermal or evaporitic pool; proposed pH 11.5\u201312.5 with continuous liquid water. Screen residence times of days to weeks. This is a proposed natural analogue of exhaustive hydrolysis, not a measured glutamate-yield protocol. After treatment, cool and mix into near-neutral water for rxn_021. Detect remaining nitriles, amides and pyroglutamate before claiming complete conversion."}, "mechanism": "Sequential nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis converts both nitrile carbons into carboxyl groups. Balanced neutral-parent net: aminodinitrile + 4 water -> glutamic acid + 2 ammonia. Partially hydrolyzed aminoamides, including glutamine-related products, can accumulate; this node is explicitly a multistep net hydrolysis.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) support the identity of the glutamate/glutamine precursor, but the supplied research does not establish exhaustive free-glutamate recovery under this proposed alkaline window. The edge is therefore a chemically justified but experimentally unclosed downstream module. Harsh hydrolysis requirements in precursor studies are discussed by Ruiz-Bermejo et al. (2013). Strong alkalinity is a local geochemical requirement, and hot treatment may cause side reactions. A precursor detection must not be reported as equivalent to a measured free-glutamate yield."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Proposed conjugate hydrocyanation of acrolein", "inputs": ["mol_030", "mol_023"], "outputs": ["mol_027"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300\u2013310 K; proposed screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, shaded aqueous pool receiving acrolein and HCN; proposed pH 8\u20139. Use short, monitored residence times to assess competing acrolein polymerization and carbonyl addition. Do not infer productive conjugate addition from disappearance of acrolein alone."}, "mechanism": "The desired 1,4-addition of cyanide to the acrolein beta carbon, followed by proton transfer, produces 3-cyanopropanal. Competing 1,2-carbonyl addition produces a different cyanohydrin and does not establish the desired C4 aldehyde branch.", "reasoning": "Van Trump and Miller (1972) proposed acrolein as a precursor relevant to glutamic acid, but did not demonstrate a selective acrolein-to-glutamate sequence. This edge is explicitly speculative in the prebiotic setting. Industrial aldehyde/Strecker chemistry establishes chemical possibility under other conditions, not the performance of this uncatalyzed environmental window. A useful yield and selectivity for 3-cyanopropanal must be measured before pathway P10 can be regarded as established."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Competing nickel-catalyzed reduction to 2-hydroxyglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni(0) supported on silica\u2013alumina"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "H2 partial pressure approximately 5 bar", "catalyst": "Ni(0)/SiO2\u2013Al2O3", "aqueous": true, "other": "Same shaded aqueous pore and pH approximately 8 as the nickel reductive-amination stage. Ammonia may be present but is not consumed in this competing reaction. Track hydroxyglutarate alongside glutamate over the 72 h experimental benchmark interval."}, "mechanism": "Nickel-activated hydrogen reduces the keto carbonyl directly to a secondary alcohol instead of reducing an ammonia-derived imine. Net: alpha-ketoglutaric acid + H2 -> 2-hydroxyglutaric acid.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) report amino-acid/hydroxy-acid mixtures during metal/H2 treatment of ketoacids. This is a supported competing reduction, not an uncatalyzed sink. An active Ni catalyst is explicitly specified. The broad approximately 1:1 amino/hydroxy product tendency reported for Ni-based systems is not assigned as an exact alpha-ketoglutarate-specific ratio. No unsupported efficient recycling of hydroxyglutarate is included."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "Glutamate cyclization to the pyroglutamate reservoir", "inputs": ["mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_031", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium, promoting acid-catalyzed cyclization"], "conditions": {"temperature": "333\u2013370 K; proposed warm-pool sink window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic acidic pool or acidified margin; pH approximately 2\u20133.5. Prolonged heating and concentration increase concern, so productive streams should normally be cooled and neutralized rather than routed here. This node evaluates loss during accidental or environmental acidification; it is not a required synthesis step."}, "mechanism": "Intramolecular attack of the amino group on the side-chain carboxyl group forms a five-membered lactam with loss of water. Net: glutamic acid -> pyroglutamic acid + water.", "reasoning": "Glutamate cyclization and thermal loss are supported by Lee et al. (2014), with solid-state behavior also documented by Wu et al. (2010) and Weiss et al. (2018). Those results do not establish a numerical free-glutamate rate throughout this lower-temperature aqueous window. In particular, the peptide-bound upper-bound rate discussed in Seifert et al. (2009) is not applied to free glutamate. The sink requires no molecular oxygen. More severe hydrothermal decomposition is avoided rather than represented by an unverified fixed product allocation."}, {"id": "rxn_020", "name": "Conditional harsh hydrolysis of the pyroglutamate reservoir", "inputs": ["mol_031", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide supplied by a strongly alkaline brine"], "conditions": {"temperature": "363\u2013370 K; proposed recovery-screening window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydroxide, a pH-maintained lactam-hydrolysis promoter", "aqueous": true, "other": "Transfer into a separate anoxic, UV-shielded caustic pool; proposed pH 12\u201313 with liquid water maintained for days to weeks. Such strongly alkaline warm compartments are a restrictive boundary condition. Cool and neutralize the recovered material afterward. Neither rapid ambient recovery nor quantitative recycling is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide-promoted lactam opening regenerates the open-chain amino dicarboxylate pool. Neutral-parent bookkeeping: pyroglutamic acid + water -> glutamic acid. Strongly basic conditions favor the ring-opened carboxylate form, but side reactions and incomplete conversion must be measured.", "reasoning": "Pizzarello and Cooper (2001) support treating pyroglutamate as an acid-labile glutamate reservoir, not exclusively an irreversible loss. They do not validate this proposed alkaline environmental protocol. The alkaline edge is therefore explicitly speculative, justified by standard lactam-hydrolysis chemistry, and included to expose the substantial treatment required for recovery. It is not counted as an independent synthesis pathway and cannot be used to assume that cyclization losses are automatically reversible under ordinary pool conditions."}, {"id": "rxn_021", "name": "Final acid\u2013base equilibration to the glutamate monoanion", "inputs": ["mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300\u2013320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Cool the product stream and mix into an anoxic, shaded receiving pool buffered at pH 6.5\u20138.5. Let entrained mineral particles settle where present. This operation changes protonation but does not separate glutamate from other dissolved organics. Acidic or highly alkaline upstream solutions must actually be buffered to the receiving pH."}, "mechanism": "Formal neutral-parent dissociation: glutamic acid -> glutamate monoanion + H+. Internal proton redistribution gives the dominant near-neutral form with a protonated amino group and two carboxylate groups. The formal equation does not imply that neutral glutamic acid predominates in the upstream reaction mixtures.", "reasoning": "This is standard acid\u2013base bookkeeping rather than a new carbon- or nitrogen-forming reaction. Near-neutral collection is consistent with the pH-sensitive reaction and stability constraints discussed by Kaur et al. (2024) and Lee et al. (2014). Protonation must be determined from the receiving pH; a broad environment label alone is insufficient. Cooling and avoiding strongly acidic residence limit, but do not eliminate, subsequent pyroglutamate formation."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Glyoxylate-driven C5 reduction followed by nickel/H2 amination", "description": "Build the C5 skeleton, reduce its alkene in the phosphate/glyoxylate compartment, then cool and dilute into a separate Ni/H2/ammonia compartment before near-neutral collection. The component chemistries have literature support, but phosphate carryover, dilution and integrated glutamate recovery remain unvalidated.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_010", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Glyoxylate-driven C5 reduction followed by pyrite-coupled amination", "description": "Transfer the phosphate-generated alpha-ketoglutarate pool, after dilution, into warm fresh-FeS/sulfide/ammonia sediment. This is conditional on observing coupled pyrite and glutamate formation; results obtained using electrochemically reduced FeS are not substituted for evidence about fresh FeS.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_006", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_010", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Glyoxylate-driven C5 reduction followed by staged oxime reduction", "description": "Generate alpha-ketoglutarate before adding hydroxylamine. Accumulate an oxime-containing fraction, then introduce Fe0 under separately controlled acidity. Hydroxylamine availability, phosphate passivation and competing iron corrosion are central limitations; simultaneous upstream addition is not assumed workable.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010", "mol_018", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Glyoxylate-driven C5 reduction with an independently prepared glycine donor", "description": "Prepare the alpha-ketoglutarate branch and a separate Ni/H2-derived glycine stream, then combine them for nonenzymatic transamination. Both branches are included in the executable schedule. This is a lower-confidence route because alpha-ketoglutarate-specific transamination performance is unverified and glyoxylate carryover can oppose net glutamate formation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010", "mol_021", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction followed by delayed nickel/H2 amination", "description": "Use supported Ni/H2 to generate the alpha-ketoglutarate pool without introducing the high-phosphate reduction stage, then introduce ammonia after evaluating carbon assembly. Delayed dosing distinguishes the proposed schedule from an assumed successful one-pot synthesis; Kaur et al. did not detect glutamate in their integrated network.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_010", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction followed by fresh-FeS amination", "description": "Generate alpha-ketoglutarate in a cool H2/Ni pore, retain supported catalyst particles upstream, and transfer the liquid into a warmer sulfide-bearing sediment compartment. This separates the metal-hydrogenation and sulfide stages physically, but dissolved carryover and fresh-FeS electron coupling still require experimental validation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_010", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction followed by hydroxylamine/iron chemistry", "description": "Supply alpha-ketoglutarate through the Ni/H2 carbon branch, retain supported Ni solids, and form the oxime before adding stoichiometric Fe0. The route avoids exposing the skeleton-forming mixture directly to hydroxylamine/Fe0, but selective oxime accumulation and net glutamate recovery after transfer are proposed rather than demonstrated.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010", "mol_018", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction with a glycine nitrogen relay", "description": "Prepare alpha-ketoglutarate and glycine in separate H2/Ni compartments, retain catalyst particles, and mix the donor and acceptor streams for transamination. Glycine formation supplies the nitrogen donor and reducing-equivalent accounting explicitly. The final transamination remains conditional on favorable concentrations and sufficiently low glyoxylate activity.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010", "mol_021", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Cyanoacetylene\u2013cyanosulfidic construction followed by Strecker-type chemistry", "description": "Construct maleonitrile from cyanoacetylene and HCN, photoreduce sequentially to succinonitrile and the C4 aldehyde, then trap the aldehyde as a cyanohydrin and aminodinitrile. Finish with a separately staged exhaustive-hydrolysis treatment and near-neutral collection. The precursor sequence is strongly supported; useful free-glutamate recovery under the proposed hydrolysis window is not established.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_013", "rxn_014", "rxn_015", "rxn_016", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_024", "mol_025", "mol_027", "mol_028", "mol_029", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Acrolein hydrocyanation followed by the aminodinitrile route", "description": "Use proposed conjugate HCN addition to acrolein as an alternative C4-aldehyde source, then follow cyanohydrin formation, ammonia incorporation, exhaustive hydrolysis and near-neutral collection. This pathway is explicitly speculative at its entry step: competing carbonyl addition and polymerization may prevent useful 3-cyanopropanal production.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_017", "rxn_014", "rxn_015", "rxn_016", "rxn_021"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_027", "mol_028", "mol_029", "mol_011"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003", "mol_006", "mol_008", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_015", "mol_023", "mol_027"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_010", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_010", "rxn_018"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_027", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_013", "rxn_017"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_014"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_011", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_008", "rxn_010", "rxn_016", "rxn_020"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_019", "rxn_021"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_003", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_013", "rxn_016"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_009", "rxn_015"]}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 537083.5199356079, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-18T20:29:42.354875", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Glutamate, racemic\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct a connected network containing 31 chemical-species nodes, 21 reaction nodes and 10 distinct target-reaching reaction schedules. Eight schedules combine two chemically different routes for reducing the C5 unsaturated ketoacid precursor with four nitrogen-incorporation routes. Two additional schedules construct glutamate through a C4 aldehyde and a dinitrile. These are conditional synthesis proposals, not ten independently demonstrated end-to-end prebiotic syntheses. Initial pyruvate, glyoxylate, cyanoacetylene, acrolein and inorganic reagents are stipulated feedstocks; their geochemical availability remains a boundary condition. Reaction conditions distinguish published benchmarks from proposed operating windows. Physical staging is required: irradiated sulfide chemistry, phosphate-rich carbon assembly, metal hydrogenation and hydrolysis are not assumed compatible in one vessel. Biochemical denotes nonenzymatic aqueous chemistry in specified compartments, including cool H2-pressurized rock pores, not biological catalysis. All compartments are anoxic; nonphotochemical reactions occur beneath sediment, in shaded pools or inside rock pores despite strong surface UV. Organic acids are represented by neutral parent structures for net-reaction bookkeeping, with explicit final equilibration to the glutamate monoanion. Actual protonation depends on receiving pH. Each environment_formed tag identifies a representative formation environment, not an exclusive one. Because the schema lacks a byproduct or sink role, mineral products, oxalate and hydroxyglutarate are labeled intermediate. Pyroglutamate formation and difficult recovery are included but are not counted as additional synthesis pathways. Exposures above approximately 423 K are excluded from synthesis: thermal destruction is experimentally supported, but an unsupported fixed decomposition-product allocation is not invented. Neither useful integrated yields nor selective L-glutamate production are claimed.\n\nMOLECULES (31 nodes):\n  - mol_001: H2O (Water) [starting]\n  - mol_002: H2 (Hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_003: NH3 (Ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_004: H+ (Proton) [starting]\n  - mol_005: C3H4O3 (Pyruvic acid) [starting]\n  - mol_006: C2H2O3 (Glyoxylic acid) [starting]\n  - mol_007: C5H6O6 (Hydroxyketoglutaric acid; maloylformic acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_008: C5H4O5 (Fumaroylformic acid) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_009: C2H2O4 (Oxalic acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_010: C5H6O5 (Alpha-ketoglutaric acid; 2-oxoglutaric acid) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_011: C5H9NO4 (Glutamic acid, racemic) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C5H8NO4- (Glutamate monoanion, racemic) [target]\n  - mol_013: C5H8O5 (2-Hydroxyglutaric acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: FeS (Mackinawite) [starting]\n  - mol_015: H2S (Hydrogen sulfide) [starting]\n  - mol_016: FeS2 (Pyrite) [intermediate]\n  - mol_017: H3NO (Hydroxylamine) [starting]\n  - mol_018: C5H7NO5 (Alpha-ketoglutaric acid oxime) [intermediate]\n  - mol_019: Fe (Zero-valent iron) [starting]\n  - mol_020: Fe+2 (Ferrous ion) [intermediate]\n  - mol_021: C2H5NO2 (Glycine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_022: C3HN (Cyanoacetylene) [starting]\n  - mol_023: CHN (Hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_024: C4H2N2 (Maleonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_025: C4H4N2 (Succinonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_026: S8 (Elemental sulfur) [intermediate]\n  - mol_027: C4H5NO (3-Cyanopropanal; beta-cyanopropionaldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_028: C5H6N2O (3-Cyanopropanal cyanohydrin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_029: C5H7N3 (Glutamate/glutamine aminodinitrile precursor) [intermediate]\n  - mol_030: C3H4O (Acrolein) [starting]\n  - mol_031: C5H7NO3 (Pyroglutamic acid; 5-oxoproline) [intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (21 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_005, mol_006 -> mol_007 (C5 skeleton construction by pyruvate\u2013glyoxylate aldol addition) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_007 -> mol_008, mol_001 (Dehydration of hydroxyketoglutarate to fumaroylformate) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_008, mol_006, mol_001 -> mol_010, mol_009 (Phosphate-assisted, glyoxylate-driven reduction to alpha-ketoglutarate) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_008, mol_002 -> mol_010 (Nickel-catalyzed hydrogenation of fumaroylformate) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_005: mol_010, mol_003, mol_002 -> mol_011, mol_001 (Nickel/H2 reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_006: mol_010, mol_003, mol_014, mol_015 -> mol_011, mol_001, mol_016 (Conditional pyrite-coupled reductive amination on fresh FeS) [Surface]\n  - rxn_007: mol_010, mol_017 -> mol_018, mol_001 (Formation of the alpha-ketoglutarate oxime) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol_018, mol_019, mol_004 -> mol_011, mol_020, mol_001 (Stoichiometric iron reduction of the alpha-ketoglutarate oxime) [Surface]\n  - rxn_009: mol_006, mol_003, mol_002 -> mol_021, mol_001 (Independent glycine production by nickel/H2 reductive amination) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_010: mol_010, mol_021 -> mol_011, mol_006 (Conditional glycine-to-alpha-ketoglutarate transamination) [Surface]\n  - rxn_011: mol_022, mol_023 -> mol_024 (Hydrocyanation of cyanoacetylene to maleonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_012: mol_024, mol_015 -> mol_025, mol_026 (Sulfide-driven photoreduction of maleonitrile to succinonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_013: mol_025, mol_015, mol_001 -> mol_027, mol_003, mol_026 (Selective photoreduction and hydrolysis of one succinonitrile nitrile group) [Surface]\n  - rxn_014: mol_027, mol_023 -> mol_028 (Cyanohydrin trapping of 3-cyanopropanal) [Surface]\n  - rxn_015: mol_028, mol_003 -> mol_029, mol_001 (Ammonia-dependent conversion of the cyanohydrin to the aminodinitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_016: mol_029, mol_001 -> mol_011, mol_003 (Exhaustive hydrolysis of both aminodinitrile groups) [Surface]\n  - rxn_017: mol_030, mol_023 -> mol_027 (Proposed conjugate hydrocyanation of acrolein) [Surface]\n  - rxn_018: mol_010, mol_002 -> mol_013 (Competing nickel-catalyzed reduction to 2-hydroxyglutarate) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_019: mol_011 -> mol_031, mol_001 (Glutamate cyclization to the pyroglutamate reservoir) [Surface]\n  - rxn_020: mol_031, mol_001 -> mol_011 (Conditional harsh hydrolysis of the pyroglutamate reservoir) [Surface]\n  - rxn_021: mol_011 -> mol_012, mol_004 (Final acid\u2013base equilibration to the glutamate monoanion) [Surface]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Glyoxylate-driven C5 reduction followed by nickel/H2 amination (5 steps)\n  - P2: Glyoxylate-driven C5 reduction followed by pyrite-coupled amination (5 steps)\n  - P3: Glyoxylate-driven C5 reduction followed by staged oxime reduction (6 steps)\n  - P4: Glyoxylate-driven C5 reduction with an independently prepared glycine donor (6 steps)\n  - P5: Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction followed by delayed nickel/H2 amination (5 steps)\n  - P6: Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction followed by fresh-FeS amination (5 steps)\n  - P7: Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction followed by hydroxylamine/iron chemistry (6 steps)\n  - P8: Hydrogen-driven C5 reduction with a glycine nitrogen relay (6 steps)\n  - P9: Cyanoacetylene\u2013cyanosulfidic construction followed by Strecker-type chemistry (7 steps)\n  - P10: Acrolein hydrocyanation followed by the aminodinitrile route (5 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 21 reactions | 17 balanced as written | 4 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_008 balanceable: C5H7NO5 + 2 Fe + 4 H+ -> C5H9NO4 + 2 Fe+2 + H2O\n  - rxn_012 balanceable: 8 C4H2N2 + 8 H2S -> 8 C4H4N2 + S8\n  - rxn_013 balanceable: 8 C4H4N2 + 8 H2S + 8 H2O -> 8 C4H5NO + 8 NH3 + S8\n  - rxn_016 balanceable: C5H7N3 + 4 H2O -> C5H9NO4 + 2 NH3\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH ON TARGET MOLECULE ===\nThe following research was conducted on the target molecule before network generation. Use it to assess whether the network aligns with known prebiotic chemistry literature.\n\n=== DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of Glutamate (Glutamic Acid): Routes, Evidence, Constraints and Open Problems\n\nNo single prebiotic route to glutamate is both experimentally complete and uncontested. The best-documented chemistry splits into two disconnected families. One is aldehyde/nitrile (Strecker-type) chemistry, which makes glutamate as a minor product of atmospheric, cyanide and meteoritic chemistry. The other is \u03b1-ketoglutarate (\u03b1-KG) chemistry, which mirrors biology: abiotic \u03b1-KG formation and nonenzymatic reductive amination or transamination have each been shown. Coupling the two in one pot has so far failed for glutamate specifically. Glutamate is consistently a low-abundance \"early\" amino acid, and it is unusually prone to cyclizing to pyroglutamate. Both points should be built into any network design.\n\n## TL;DR\n- **Two families of routes, neither closed end-to-end.** Strecker/nitrile chemistry needs a C4 aldehyde, 3-cyanopropanal (\u03b2-cyanopropionaldehyde). This has been reached by an explicit UV/H\u2082S (and Cu-photoredox) sequence from cyanoacetylene (Patel et al. 2015, *Nature Chemistry*), and it is proposed (not demonstrated) from spark-derived acrolein (Van Trump & Miller 1972, *Science*). \u03b1-KG routes have shown each half separately:\n  - \u03b1-KG from glyoxylate + pyruvate (Fe\u00b2\u207a: Muchowska et al. 2019, *Nature*; metal-free: Stubbs et al. 2020, *Nature Chemistry*).\n  - \u03b1-KG \u2192 glutamate with Fe/FeS, Ni/H\u2082, cyanide/Bucherer\u2013Bergs or transamination.\n  - But in the best one-pot H\u2082/Ni network, glutamate itself was not detectable (Kaur et al. 2024, *Chem*).\n- **Glutamate is real but minor in abiotic inventories.** It shows up in reanalysed Miller discharge extracts (Johnson et al. 2008, *Science*), in neutral-atmosphere sparks only when nitrite oxidation is suppressed (Cleaves et al. 2008, *OLEB*), and in HCN-polymer hydrolysates. It is present in carbonaceous chondrites and racemic in pristine Bennu samples (Glavin et al. 2025, *Nature Astronomy*). It falls in Higgs & Pudritz's (2009, *Astrobiology*) thermodynamically ranked \"early ten\", but always well below Gly/Ala.\n- **The main bottlenecks are:**\n  - supply of the C4 precursor (3-cyanopropanal or \u03b1-KG) at useful concentration;\n  - incompatibility between \u03b1-KG-forming and aminating conditions;\n  - \u03b1-KG's intrinsically slow reductive amination (Mayer & Moran 2022, *Angew. Chem.*);\n  - cyclization of glutamate to pyroglutamate and thermal loss above ~150\u2013200 \u00b0C.\n\n  The Strecker routes are contested mainly on precursor availability and on the cyanide-vs-metabolism \"congruence\" argument. The \u03b1-KG routes are contested on reagent choice (hydroxylamine, stoichiometric Fe\u2070, noble metals), on ammonia concentration and on Orgel-type objections to self-organizing cycles.\n\n## Key Findings\n\n| Route / framework | Feedstocks | Key glutamate-relevant intermediate | Best quantitative evidence | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Electric discharge, strongly reducing (CH\u2084/NH\u2083/H\u2082/H\u2082O) | CH\u2084, NH\u2083, H\u2082, H\u2082O | HCN + aldehydes \u2192 aminonitriles (Strecker); acrolein proposed for Glu | Glu identified in Miller's 1950s extracts (Johnson et al. 2008); glycine yields 2.1% / 1.8% / 0.4% of CH\u2084-carbon in classic / volcanic / silent apparatus; Glu is a trace product | Contested: early-atmosphere redox |\n| Discharge, H\u2082S-rich | CH\u2084, H\u2082S, NH\u2083, CO\u2082 | Same, plus thio-chemistry | Glu detected (Parker et al. 2011, *PNAS*); L-enrichment of Asp/Glu/Ser flagged as possible trace contamination | Contested: atmosphere; Glu losses via H\u2082S |\n| Discharge, neutral (CO\u2082/N\u2082) | CO\u2082, N\u2082, H\u2082O | Strecker | Glu among products only when Fe\u00b2\u207a/ascorbate suppress nitrite/nitrate oxidation during workup (Cleaves et al. 2008); Plankensteiner et al. 2006 did not detect Asp/Glu | Contested: yields, workup |\n| Proton/cosmic-ray irradiation | CO/CO\u2082, N\u2082, H\u2082O \u00b1 CH\u2084 | Macromolecular \"amino acid precursors\" (hydrolysis needed) | Amino acids appear at \u22650.5% CH\u2084 (Kobayashi et al. 2023, *Life*); Gly dominant; Glu not reported as a major product | Plausible energy source; Glu-specific data thin |\n| HCN oligomer/polymer hydrolysis | HCN, NH\u2083 | Polymer-bound nitrile/amide units | Glu among 12 protein amino acids released on acid hydrolysis (Ruiz-Bermejo et al. 2013, *Life*); Gly always dominant | Contested: needs \u22650.1 M HCN and acid hydrolysis |\n| Cyanosulfidic photochemistry | HCN, cyanoacetylene, H\u2082S, Cu, UV | Maleonitrile \u2192 succinonitrile \u2192 3-cyanopropanal \u2192 cyanohydrin \u2192 \u03b1-aminonitrile 54 | Explicit Glu/Gln aminonitrile route (Patel et al. 2015); step yields only in SI | Contested: sulfide, Cu, UV, sequential additions |\n| \u03b1-KG via glyoxylate + pyruvate | Glyoxylate, pyruvate (ultimately CO\u2082) | Hydroxyketoglutarate \u2192 oxopentenedioate \u2192 \u03b1-KG | 5% \u03b1-KG (0.5 M phosphate, pH 7, 50 \u00b0C, 21 h; Stubbs et al. 2020); Fe\u00b2\u207a 70 \u00b0C network (Muchowska et al. 2019) | Contested: glyoxylate supply, cycle plausibility |\n| \u03b1-KG reductive amination (Fe, FeS, Ni/H\u2082, electrochemical) | \u03b1-KG, NH\u2083/NH\u2084\u207a, reductant | Imine of \u03b1-KG | Up to ~50% Glu with fresh FeS at 50\u201375 \u00b0C (Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user 2003); >50% in electrochemically reduced FeS (Kitadai et al. 2019); Ni/Pd/meteorite + H\u2082 at 22 \u00b0C, pH 8 (Kaur et al. 2024) | Strongest experimentally; contested on reagents, NH\u2083 levels and coupling |\n| \u03b1-KG via Bucherer\u2013Bergs (cyanide) | \u03b1-KG, HCN, NH\u2083 source (DAP) | 5-substituted hydantoin | Hydantoin 96%; glutamate 19% after hydrolysis with 72% hydantoin left (Pulletikurti et al. 2022, *Nat. Chem.*) | Contested: cyanide \"incongruent\" with metabolism |\n| Hydrothermal one-step thermodynamics | CO\u2082, NH\u2084\u207a, H\u2082 | n/a | \u0394Gr(Glu\u207b) = \u22121.43 kJ/mol at 100 \u00b0C vs +172.13 kJ/mol at 18 \u00b0C (Amend & Shock 1998, *Science*) | Contested: kinetics, stability (Bada & Miller 1995) |\n| Meteoritic/asteroidal delivery | Parent-body aqueous chemistry | Strecker-cyanohydrin, others | Glu racemic in Bennu; L-excesses in Murchison partly terrestrial (Glavin et al. 2021, *MAPS*) | Source of racemic Glu; delivery flux uncertain |\n\n## Details\n\n### 1. Known prebiotic synthesis routes\n\n#### 1a. Atmospheric electric discharge (strongly reducing, volcanic, H\u2082S-rich, neutral)\n\n**Mechanism.** The discharge makes HCN, NH\u2083 and aldehydes in the gas phase. These dissolve, and aqueous Strecker chemistry turns them into \u03b1-aminonitriles and then amino acids (Miller 1957).\\[1\\]\\[2\\] For glutamate, Van Trump & Miller (1972, *Science* 178:859) found acrolein (CH\u2082=CH\u2013CHO) among the spark products of CH\u2084/N\u2082/NH\u2083/H\u2082O/H\u2082S. They proposed it \"as an intermediate in the prebiotic synthesis of methionine and of glutamic acid, homocysteine, homoserine, and \u03b1,\u03b3-diaminobutyric acid.\"\\[3\\]\n- The proposed chemistry is conjugate addition of HCN to acrolein, giving \u03b2-cyanopropionaldehyde (3-cyanopropanal), followed by Strecker reaction and hydrolysis.\n- **For glutamate this remains a proposal.** No Miller-lab yield for acrolein \u2192 Glu has been found. Parker et al. (2011, *OLEB*) restate the idea but give methionine yields only qualitatively.\\[4\\]\n- The underlying organic chemistry is real outside the prebiotic setting. An industrial patent (US 3,010,994, filed 1955\u201356) converts acrylonitrile to \u03b2-cyanopropionaldehyde in up to 70 mol% yield, then by Strecker reaction to glutamic acid at 45.8% of theory based on acrylonitrile.\\[5\\] It runs under non-prebiotic, industrial conditions.\n\n**Evidence.**\n- **Johnson et al. (2008, *Science* 322:404)** reanalysed Miller's archived \"volcanic\" (steam-injected) apparatus extracts.\n  - Conditions: 200 torr CH\u2084, 200 torr NH\u2083, 100 torr H\u2082, 500 mL water, about 1 week of sparking.\\[6\\]\n  - They found 22 amino acids and 5 amines, including D,L-glutamic acid and 2-methylglutamic acid.\\[6\\]\n  - Absolute yields could not be recalculated. Miller's own glycine yields were 2.1%, 1.8% and 0.4% of CH\u2084-carbon for the classic, volcanic and silent-discharge apparatus.\\[6\\]\n  - Glutamate is reported relative to glycine and is a trace component. One secondary reading of Table S1 puts Gly + Ala above 99.8% of protein amino acids; that figure is unverified.\\[7\\]\n- **Parker et al. (2011, *PNAS* 108:5526)** reanalysed Miller's 1958 H\u2082S run (CH\u2084/H\u2082S/NH\u2083/CO\u2082 = 258/100/250/87 mm Hg, 300 mL water, 3 days) and detected glutamic acid.\\[8\\]\n  - They note that aspartic and glutamic acids and serine \"are present in relatively low abundances\" and \"are enriched in the L-enantiomer, indicating that trace amounts of contamination of some amino acids may be present.\"\\[8\\]\n  - A companion *OLEB* paper suggests Asp and Glu may break down to alkyl amino acids via thioimide intermediates when H\u2082S is present.\\[9\\] This is a specific sink for glutamate in sulfur-rich settings.\n- **Ring et al. (1972, *PNAS* 69:765)** found glutamic acid among 19 amino acids from CH\u2084/N\u2082/H\u2082O with trace NH\u2083.\\[10\\]\n\n**Neutral atmospheres.** Cleaves, Chalmers, Lazcano, Miller & Bada (2008, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres* 38:105) showed that earlier low yields from CO\u2082/N\u2082 sparks reflected oxidation by discharge-made nitrite and nitrate during hydrolytic workup. Adding ferrous iron or ascorbate before hydrolysis greatly raised yields,\\[11\\] and a later review summarizes \"significant production of Ala, Ser and Glu.\" By contrast, Plankensteiner et al. (2006) sparked CO\u2082/N\u2082/H\u2082O and did not detect Asp or Glu.\\[12\\]\n\n**Critiques.**\n- The early atmosphere was probably not strongly reducing (Kasting 1993, as summarized by Higgs & Pudritz 2009).\\[1\\]\\[8\\]\n- Neutral-atmosphere yields depend on workup chemistry and on ocean pH/redox buffering.\\[12\\]\n- Critics have argued that the Fe\u00b2\u207a/ascorbate step amounts to experimental intervention; this criticism appears mainly in non-peer-reviewed commentary.\\[13\\]\n- Glutamate is always a minor product, so discharge chemistry supplies Glu only at trace levels relative to Gly/Ala.\n\n#### 1b. Proton/cosmic-ray irradiation, radiolysis, UV and shock\n\n- **Kobayashi et al. (2023, *Life* 13:1103)** irradiated CO\u2082/CH\u2084/N\u2082/H\u2082O mixtures with protons, simulating solar energetic particles.\n  - Amino acids appeared after acid hydrolysis once CH\u2084 reached 0.5% (v/v). Unhydrolysed samples held only precursors.\\[14\\]\n  - No amino acids formed by UV, even at 50% CH\u2084.\\[15\\]\n  - At an initial CH\u2084 ratio of 0.12, proton irradiation made amino acids about 3 orders of magnitude faster than spark discharge.\\[16\\]\n  - Glycine dominated, followed by Asp, Ser, Ala, \u03b1-aminobutyric acid and \u03b2-alanine.\\[17\\] Glutamate is not reported as a major product.\n- Earlier proton work on CO/N\u2082/H\u2082O (Miyakawa et al. 2002, reported as column A3 in Higgs & Pudritz 2009) also gave amino acid precursors.\\[1\\]\n- Gamma radiolysis of HCN solutions, followed by hydrolysis, yields glycine, alanine, valine, serine, threonine, aspartic acid and glutamic acid, with contamination controls (summarized in Ruiz-Bermejo et al. 2013).\\[18\\]\n- Shock synthesis from gas mixtures (Bar-Nun et al. 1970) is among the data sets Higgs & Pudritz used to rank amino acids.\\[1\\]\n\n**Critique.** These routes give macromolecular precursors that need strong acid hydrolysis; that is an analytical step, not necessarily a natural one.\\[15\\] Data on glutamate yields are sparse.\n\n#### 1c. Strecker / nitrile chemistry and the C4 aldehyde problem\n\nGlutamate's Strecker precursor is succinic semialdehyde (4-oxobutanoic acid), or its nitrile analogue 3-cyanopropanal (4-oxobutanenitrile, HCOCH\u2082CH\u2082CN). Strecker chemistry on 3-cyanopropanal gives 2-aminopentanedinitrile, the common precursor of glutamine and glutamate.\\[19\\] Proposed origins of the C4 aldehyde:\n1. Acrolein + HCN (Van Trump & Miller 1972): proposed only.\n2. Cyanoacetylene \u2192 maleonitrile \u2192 succinonitrile \u2192 3-cyanopropanal by UV/H\u2082S photoreduction (Patel et al. 2015; see 1d).\n3. Interstellar/parent-body sources. 4-oxobutanenitrile has been characterized by rotational spectroscopy and searched for in the G+0.693 molecular cloud (arXiv 2512.11500). That work describes the molecule as one \"proposed in prebiotic chemistry\u2026 as a precursor of glutamic acid\" (citing Kitadai & Maruyama 2018).\\[20\\] A search is not a detection.\n\n**Critique.** The C4 aldehyde is much less abundant than formaldehyde or acetaldehyde in every simulated source, which is a main reason Glu trails Gly/Ala. Aldehyde-based Strecker chemistry is also argued to be \"disconnected\" from biology's \u03b1-ketoacid-based amino acid metabolism (Pulletikurti et al. 2022, *Nature Chemistry* 14:1142).\\[21\\]\n\n#### 1d. Cyanosulfidic UV photochemistry (Sutherland)\n\nPatel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy & Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry* 7:301) reported the following sequence:\n1. Photoreduction of maleonitrile (from cyanoacetylene) by H\u2082S \"saturates the double bond giving succinonitrile 51.\"\\[22\\]\n2. \"Further irradiation in the presence of [H\u2082S] selectively reduces one nitrile group of succinonitrile 51 giving the semialdehyde 52.\"\\[22\\] Compound 52 is 3-cyanopropanal.\n3. \"Addition of hydrogen cyanide\u2026 to the semialdehyde 52 gives the cyanohydrin 53 from which 54, the \u03b1-aminonitrile precursor of glutamine and glutamic acid is produced upon addition of ammonia.\"\\[22\\]\n\nThe photoreductions use UV (254 nm), and per the abstract can be \"accelerated by Cu(I)\u2013Cu(II) photoredox cycling.\"\\[23\\]\\[24\\] In this network acrylonitrile feeds the proline/arginine branch, not glutamate.\\[25\\] Per-step yields are in the paper's Supplementary Information and could not be verified here.\n\n**Critiques.**\n- **Sulfide supply.** Sulfidic anions are needed as both photoreductant and feedstock, and Patel et al. invoke impactors as the source. Ranjan, Todd, Sutherland & Sasselov (2018, *Astrobiology*; arXiv 1801.07725) show that this imposes a local geochemical requirement and quantify the sulfide levels attainable in surface waters.\\[24\\]\n- **Copper.** Cu is relatively scarce in the crust.\\[19\\]\n- **Not one-pot.** The chemistry relies on sequential, controlled delivery of reagents (Springsteen 2015; Higgs & Lehman 2015, cited in Ranjan et al. 2018).\\[19\\]\n- **Congruence.** The route does not resemble biology's \u03b1-KG-based glutamate synthesis. A later cyanosulfidic paper (\"A cyanosulfidic origin of the Krebs cycle\", *Science Advances*) tries to link the network to TCA intermediates.\\[26\\]\n\n#### 1e. HCN oligomer/polymer and formamide chemistry\n\n- **Ferris et al. (1978, *J. Mol. Evol.* 11:293)** condensed 0.1 M HCN at pH 9.2. Hydrolysis of the oligomers released pyrimidines, adenine and amino acids.\\[27\\]\n- **Ruiz-Bermejo, Zorzano & Osuna-Esteban (2013, *Life* 3:421)** reviewed the field: HCN polymers yield twelve of the twenty protein amino acids, including glutamic acid, with \"glycine\u2026 always obtained with the greatest yield.\" Acid hydrolysis was usually needed before detection.\\[28\\]\\[29\\] Structural work (TMAH thermochemolysis) finds \u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013 substructures, consistent with Glu side chains on hydrolysis.\\[30\\]\n- **Critiques:**\n  - Steady-state HCN in the open primitive ocean is estimated at only 4 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2076 to 2 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2078 M (pH 8, 0\u201325 \u00b0C; Miyakawa et al. 2002, cited in Ruiz-Bermejo et al. 2013).\\[29\\] Polymerization needs much higher concentrations and is \"very ineffective close to or below room temperature\" at low concentration (Ruiz-Bermejo et al.).\\[31\\]\n  - Release depends on harsh hydrolysis, and the products are unselective.\n  - No formamide-specific glutamate synthesis was found in this review.\n\n#### 1f. \u03b1-Ketoglutarate-based routes (metabolism-first and \"protometabolic\" frameworks)\n\n**Step 1: forming \u03b1-KG.**\n- **Muchowska, Varma & Moran (2019, *Nature* 569:104).** Fe\u00b2\u207a in 70 \u00b0C water turns pyruvate + glyoxylate into 9 of 11 TCA intermediates, including \u03b1-KG. The path is aldol to hydroxyketoglutarate, dehydration to oxopentenedioate, then reduction to \u03b1-KG. Adding hydroxylamine and Fe\u2070 \"produces four biological amino acids\": glycine, alanine, aspartic acid and glutamic acid, by t = 2 h.\\[32\\]\\[33\\]\\[34\\]\n- **Stubbs, Yadav, Krishnamurthy & Springsteen (2020, *Nature Chemistry* 12:1016).** This network needs no metal. Glyoxylate acts as both carbon source and reductant. In 0.5 M phosphate at pH 7 and 50 \u00b0C for 21 h it gives 39% maloyl formate, 6% fumaroyl formate, 5% \u03b1-ketoglutarate, 5% isocitroyl formate and 2% aconitoyl formate. The \u03b1-ketoacids can be transaminated by glycine, \"obviating the need for metal-catalysed abiotic reductive aminations.\"\\[35\\]\\[36\\]\n- **Yadav et al. (2022, *Nature Chemistry* 14:170).** Cyanide acts as a reductant for some TCA steps (oxaloacetate \u2192 malate, fumarate \u2192 succinate), \"whereas pyruvate and \u03b1-ketoglutarate themselves are not reduced.\"\\[37\\] That selectivity spares \u03b1-KG.\n- **Kaur et al. (2024, *Chem* 10).** H\u2082 over Ni or Pd drives the reductive aldol chemistry at 22 \u00b0C. Without a catalyst, aldol condensation still occurs but the products downstream of fumaroyl formate reduction (which include \u03b1-KG) do not.\\[38\\]\n- Guzman & Martin (2009, *Astrobiology*) reported photoelectrochemical production of rTCA \u03b1-ketoacids on ZnS mineral.\n\n**Step 2: \u03b1-KG \u2192 glutamate.**\n- **Fe/FeS reductive amination.** Hafenbradl, Keller, W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user & Stetter (1995, *Tetrahedron Letters* 36:5179) coupled reductive amination of \u03b1-oxo acids to FeS \u2192 pyrite oxidation. Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003, *Tetrahedron Letters* 44:1695) found amino acids \"formed efficiently\u2026 with freshly precipitated FeS or Fe(OH)\u2082\u2026 at pH values near their pKa.\"\\[21\\]\\[39\\]\n  - Yields of up to ~50% for alanine, glutamate, phenylalanine and tyrosine at 50 and 75 \u00b0C are reported in secondary summaries; no exact primary figure for glutamate was located, and Kitadai et al. (2019, *Sci. Adv.* 5:eaav7848) describe as-prepared FeS as promoting keto acid\u2013to\u2013amino acid reductive amination \"albeit only slightly\" at pH 8\u201310.\n  - Fresh sulfides of Zn, Mn, Co, Cu and Ni were inactive, and replacing Fe with Ni simply lowered the yield (as summarized by Barge et al. 2019).\\[40\\]\n- **Electrochemically reduced FeS (alkaline-vent analogue).** Kitadai et al. (2019, *Science Advances* 5:eaav7848) reductively aminated four keto acids (1.25 mM each) in one bottle. All the corresponding amino acids formed in >50% yield, and \"Alanine and glutamate were formed preferentially over the others.\"\\[41\\]\n- **Iron (oxy)hydroxides.** Barge et al. (2019, *PNAS* 116:4828) showed pyruvate \u2192 alanine is greatest at a 1:1 Fe(II):Fe(III) ratio, under alkaline conditions, at about 70 \u00b0C; only lactate forms at pH 7.\\[42\\]\\[43\\] Glutamate was not tested, so this is an analogue result.\n- **Ni or Pd with H\u2082 (Kaur et al. 2024).**\n  - Conditions: 30 mM ketoacid, NH\u2084HCO\u2083 (5 equiv, 150 mM), H\u2082 at 5 bar, pH 8, 22 \u00b0C, 72 h.\\[38\\]\n  - Catalysts: Pd/C (1 mol%), Ni/SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083 (10 mol%) or ground Aletai iron meteorite (18 mol% Ni). All converted \u03b1-KG and five other ketoacids plus glyoxylate into amino acid + hydroxy acid mixtures.\\[38\\]\n  - Combined yields were 6\u201384% for Ni and meteorite, with amino:hydroxy acid ratios near 1:1, and 52\u201388% for Pd, with amino acid favoured >5:1 in almost all cases.\\[38\\]\n  - With no catalyst there was no reduction.\\[38\\]\n  - Ammonia could be cut to 6 mM, which Kaur et al. note is 62\u2013250 times lower than in earlier Fe-based work (375 mM\u20131.5 M).\\[38\\]\n  - **But in the one-pot network starting from oxaloacetate + glyoxylate, \"Glutamate and hydroxy ketoglutarate were not detectable.\"**\\[38\\]\n- **Bucherer\u2013Bergs (cyanide) route.** Pulletikurti, Yadav, Springsteen & Krishnamurthy (2022, *Nature Chemistry* 14:1142) combined \u03b1-KG + diamidophosphate + cyanide to give the glutamate hydantoin in 96% yield. Hydrolysis then gave glutamate in 19%, with 72% hydantoin remaining (preprint data).\\[21\\]\\[44\\] The sequence is hydantoin formation at about 20 \u00b0C, then hydrolysis at about 80 \u00b0C.\\[38\\]\n- **Transamination.** Nonenzymatic transamination with glycine works on \u03b1-ketoacid analogues (Stubbs et al. 2020). Al\u00b3\u207a-catalysed transamination also works, but it is incompatible with phosphate-mediated \u03b1-KG formation (Kaur et al. 2024).\\[38\\] Pyridoxamine and urea reductively aminate \u03b1-KG to glutamate nonenzymatically even in metabolite extracts. That is an analytical artifact study (*J. Chromatogr. A* 2020),\\[45\\] but it shows how easily the reaction happens with B6-type amine donors.\n- **Kinetics.** Mayer & Moran (2022, *Angew. Chem. Int. Ed.*) used BH\u2083CN\u207b as a model hydride donor. Nonenzymatic reductive amination reactivity runs \"\u03b1-ketoglutarate < oxaloacetate \u2248 pyruvate \u226a glyoxylate,\" and \u03b1-KG amination is also \"thermochemically less favored.\"\\[46\\] Biology's choice of \u03b1-KG as the entry point for reductive amination is therefore not explained by intrinsic reactivity. They note that nonenzymatic transamination using glutamate as the donor is thermodynamically favourable for most \u03b1-ketoacids.\\[47\\]\n\n**Critiques of the \u03b1-KG family.**\n- **Reagents.** Hydroxylamine, Fe\u2070, BH\u2083CN\u207b, Pd and DAP are questioned as prebiotic reagents. Kaur et al. note that Fe\u2070/hydroxylamine \"disrupt Fe\u00b2\u207a-promoted ketoacid formation.\" Kaur et al. 2024, in turn, argue that cyanide \"is never directly used in metabolism\u2026 its relevance to metabolic origins is questionable.\"\\[38\\] Proponents of cyanide chemistry answer that their Bucherer\u2013Bergs route makes cyanide chemistry *more* congruent with \u03b1-ketoacid metabolism.\\[21\\]\n- **Coupling.** Every step has been shown, but glutamate itself is not yet produced in an integrated one-pot network.\n- **Feedstock.** A continuous supply of glyoxylate and pyruvate is assumed.\\[48\\]\n- **Cycles.** Orgel (2008, *PLoS Biology*, \"The implausibility of metabolic cycles on the prebiotic Earth\") argued that self-organizing cycles lack the needed catalytic specificity. This objection applies directly to rTCA-like \u03b1-KG supply.\n\n#### 1g. Iron\u2013sulfur world and hydrothermal scenarios; thermodynamic modelling\n\n**Thermodynamics.** Amend & Shock (1998, *Science* 281:1659) wrote net synthesis as 5CO\u2082(aq) + NH\u2084\u207a + 9H\u2082(aq) \u2192 glutamate\u207b + 2H\u207a + 6H\u2082O.\n- In a 100 \u00b0C mixed hydrothermal solution, \u0394Gr = \u22121.43 kJ/mol (glutamate activity 2.7 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2079).\n- In 18 \u00b0C surface seawater, \u0394Gr = +172.13 kJ/mol.\n- 11 of 20 amino acids were exergonic under the hydrothermal conditions.\\[49\\]\n\nLater work extended this to alkaline vents (Kitadai 2015, *OLEB*).\\[50\\]\n\n**Critiques.**\n- Bada, Miller & Zhao (1995, *OLEB* 25:111) showed amino acids \"are irreversibly destroyed by heating at 240 \u00b0C\" and argued that \"quasi-equilibrium calculations give misleading descriptions.\"\\[51\\]\n- Aubrey, Cleaves & Bada (2009, *OLEB* 39:91) assessed submarine systems and found amino acid yields peaking after ~75 h at 175 \u00b0C, with competing degradation to NH\u2083, CO\u2082 and H\u2082.\\[52\\]\n- Higgs & Pudritz (2009) found no correlation between hydrothermal \u0394G and observed prebiotic abundance.\\[1\\]\n- Martin and colleagues (*Frontiers in Microbiology* 2021) note that one-step \u0394G calculations \"do not begin to capture\" the stepwise thermodynamics of metabolism.\\[53\\]\n- Tutolo (2026, *PNAS* 123(36):e2613108123, \"Rethinking the origin of life at seafloor hydrothermal vents\") argues that ancient serpentinizing vents lacked strong pH gradients at hydrothermal temperatures and would have had virtually no sulfide, concluding that \"these considerations challenge currently formulated alkaline vent hypotheses for the origin of life\"; this undercuts the FeS chemistry in alkaline-vent models.\n\nFischer\u2013Tropsch-type syntheses (e.g., Yoshino et al. 1971, CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 over catalysts at high temperature) produced amino acids in some runs.\\[1\\] Glutamate-specific data were not located.\n\n#### 1h. Extraterrestrial sources and delivery\n\n**Meteorites.**\n- Glutamic acid is a common indigenous amino acid in CM chondrites (Kvenvolden et al. 1970).\\[54\\]\n- Engel & Macko (1997, *Nature* 389:265) reported nearly identical \u03b4\u00b9\u2075N for D- and L-Glu (+60\u2030, +58\u2030), argued for an extraterrestrial origin of a large L-excess (~54%), and invoked circularly polarized light.\\[54\\]\\[55\\]\n- Pizzarello & Cooper (2001, *MAPS*) found L-excesses of 16\u201347.2% in pyroglutamic acid. \u03b4\u00b9\u00b3C values were D-Glu +32.2\u2030 and L-Glu +14.6\u2030, which implies terrestrial dilution of the L-enantiomer.\\[56\\]\n- Glavin et al. (2021, *MAPS*) calculated that \"between 27% and 80% of the measured L-glutamic acid excess in the Murchison meteorite could be terrestrial.\" That puts the corrected indigenous L-excess at 16\u201340%. In Aguas Zarcas, L-Glu excesses of ~56\u201373% are \"mostly terrestrial.\"\\[57\\]\n- In Antarctic CR2s, Glu is the only amino acid with L-excess (D/L 0.58\u20130.83), attributed to contamination.\\[58\\]\n\n**Returned samples.**\n- **Bennu (OSIRIS-REx):** 33 amino acids, including 14 protein amino acids, with total abundance of 70 \u00b1 2 nmol/g (Parker, McLain, Glavin, Dworkin et al., NASA NTRS 20250002782), described by Glavin, Dworkin et al. (2025, *Nature Astronomy* 9:199) as \"lower by a factor of 3.6 than that in Murchison but 4.7 times higher than in Ryugu extracts.\" All chiral amino acids are racemic (Glavin, Dworkin et al. 2025, *Nature Astronomy* 9:199). Glu is among the dominant species.\n- **Bennu isotopes (Baczynski, McIntosh, Simkus\u2026 Glavin, Elsila\u2026 Lauretta 2026, *PNAS* 123(8):e2517723123):**\n  - L-Glu \u03b4\u00b9\u00b3C = +20 \u00b1 5\u2030.\n  - \"The \u03b415N value of D-glutamic acid (+277 \u00b1 7\u2030) is 87\u2030 higher than L-glutamic acid (+190 \u00b1 32\u2030), even though the enantiomers are racemic,\" which \"challenge[s] the assumption of isotopic uniformity between amino acid chiral pairs.\"\n  - The authors suggest Murchison glycine formed mainly by Strecker-like aqueous chemistry, whereas Bennu glycine may come mainly from radical chemistry in cold ices.\\[59\\]\n- **Ryugu:** about 15 nmol/g total amino acids, dominated by \u03b2-Ala and \u03b3-aminobutyric acid rather than protein amino acids.\\[60\\]\n\n**Ice photolysis.** UV irradiation of interstellar-ice analogues (H\u2082O/CO/NH\u2083/CH\u2083OH/HCN) gives several amino acids after hydrolysis (Bernstein et al. 2002; Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. 2002, both *Nature*). Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. 2002 is one of the Higgs & Pudritz data sets.\\[1\\] Glu-specific yields from ice photolysis were not confirmed here.\n\n**Delivery critiques.**\n- Exogenous delivery matters only if it rivals endogenous synthesis. Pierazzo & Chyba (1999) estimated organic delivery on the order of 10\u2078 kg/yr, but survival on impact is uncertain (Higgs & Pudritz 2009).\\[1\\]\n- Pristine samples show Glu is racemic, so meteorites do not explain biological homochirality.\\[61\\]\n\n### 2. Key intermediates and precursors (by route)\n- **Strecker/nitrile:** HCN, NH\u2083, acrolein (proposed), cyanoacetylene \u2192 maleonitrile/fumaronitrile \u2192 succinonitrile \u2192 3-cyanopropanal (52) \u2192 cyanohydrin (53) \u2192 2-aminopentanedinitrile (54) \u2192 glutamine/glutamate.\\[22\\] Succinic semialdehyde is the carboxylic-acid analogue.\n- **HCN polymer:** HCN oligomers (aminomalononitrile, DAMN-derived units), which release Glu on hydrolysis.\n- **\u03b1-KG:** CO\u2082 \u2192 formate/acetate/pyruvate; glyoxylate; oxaloacetate; aldol products (hydroxyketoglutarate/maloyl formate) \u2192 oxopentenedioate/fumaroyl formate \u2192 \u03b1-KG \u2192 imine \u2192 glutamate.\\[38\\] Branches go through the \u03b1-KG hydantoin (Bucherer\u2013Bergs) or through transamination with glycine or a PLP/pyridoxamine analogue.\n- **Downstream sinks:** pyroglutamate (5-oxoproline), succinate, formate, CO\u2082, NH\u2083 (thermal), and alkyl amino acids (H\u2082S-mediated).\n\n### 3. Conditions and environments\n\n| Route | T | pH | Energy/reductant | Environment invoked |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Discharge/Strecker | Ambient aqueous after gas-phase synthesis | ~8\u20139 for Strecker | Lightning, volcanic lightning | Volcanic plumes, surface ocean, tidal pools |\n| Proton irradiation | Ambient | \u2014 | Solar energetic particles, GCR | Upper atmosphere of a weakly reducing CO\u2082/N\u2082 atmosphere |\n| Cyanosulfidic | Ambient, UV 254 nm | Near-neutral | UV + HS\u207b/H\u2082S, Cu photoredox | Surface streams/pools with impact-derived sulfide and cyanide salts |\n| HCN polymer | Up to 80\u201390 \u00b0C | ~9.2 | HCN self-condensation | Evaporative/concentrated basins |\n| \u03b1-KG formation | 22 \u00b0C (Ni/H\u2082) to 50\u201370 \u00b0C (phosphate, Fe\u00b2\u207a) | 7\u20138 | Glyoxylate, H\u2082 | Generic aqueous; vents (H\u2082 1\u201315 mmol/kg in modern fluids) |\n| Reductive amination | 22 \u00b0C (Ni/H\u2082), 50\u201375 \u00b0C (FeS), ~70 \u00b0C (Fe oxyhydroxide) | Alkaline, near the NH\u2084\u207a pKa of 9.25 | H\u2082, Fe\u00b2\u207a/FeS, electro-reduced FeS | Alkaline vents, Fe-rich sediments |\n| Hydrothermal one-step | ~100 \u00b0C, 250 bar | \u2014 | H\u2082 | Mixing zones |\n| Parent bodies | <50 \u00b0C (Bennu/Ryugu alteration) | Alkaline, NH\u2083-rich | Radiogenic heat | Carbonaceous asteroids |\n\n**Which environments are best supported?** None is uniquely established. On the evidence:\n- Parent-body aqueous alteration is the only setting where indigenous Glu is *observed*, from Murchison isotopes and racemic Glu in Bennu.\n- Iron-rich, H\u2082-bearing, alkaline aqueous settings have the strongest *experimental* support for the biology-congruent \u03b1-KG \u2192 Glu step. Glutamate's selective formation over FeS (Kitadai et al. 2019) is notable.\\[41\\]\n- UV-lit surface pools with sulfide and cyanide have the only explicit *de novo* nitrile route to the Glu skeleton.\n\n### 4. Catalysts and surfaces\n\n**Shown to promote glutamate-relevant steps:**\n- Freshly precipitated FeS and Fe(OH)\u2082 (Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user 2003).\\[39\\]\n- FeS with pyrite formation (Hafenbradl et al. 1995).\\[40\\]\n- Electrochemically reduced FeS (Kitadai et al. 2019).\\[41\\]\n- Mixed-valence iron oxyhydroxides, shown for pyruvate (Barge et al. 2019).\\[43\\]\n- Ni/SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083, Pd/C and iron meteorites (Aletai) with H\u2082 (Kaur et al. 2024).\\[38\\]\n- Fe\u00b2\u207a, which promotes \u03b1-KG formation (Muchowska et al. 2019).\\[33\\]\n- Phosphate as a general catalyst for \u03b1-KG formation (Stubbs et al. 2020).\\[35\\]\n- Al\u00b3\u207a for transamination.\n- Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox for cyanosulfidic photoreduction (Patel et al. 2015).\\[23\\]\n- ZnS photoelectrochemistry for \u03b1-ketoacids (Guzman & Martin 2009).\n\n**Shown inactive:** fresh sulfides of Zn, Mn, Co, Cu and Ni for reductive amination (Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user 2003).\\[40\\]\n\n**No catalyst required:**\n- Strecker/aminonitrile formation and hydrolysis.\n- Bucherer\u2013Bergs hydantoin formation from \u03b1-KG.\n- The aldol condensation of oxaloacetate/pyruvate with glyoxylate at 22 \u00b0C.\\[38\\]\n- Glyoxylate-driven reduction of fumaroyl formate in phosphate. Phosphate acts as a general catalyst here, with no metal (Stubbs et al. 2020).\\[36\\]\n- Glycine-mediated transamination of \u03b1-ketoacid analogues.\\[36\\]\n\n**Explicitly required:** H\u2082-driven reductive amination did not proceed without a metal (Kaur et al. 2024).\\[38\\]\n\n### 5. Stability and degradation constraints specific to glutamate\n\n- **Cyclization to pyroglutamate.** This is the dominant intramolecular sink.\n  - For N-terminal residues in peptides, spontaneous Glu cyclization has a limiting rate constant of 1.0 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2079 s\u207b\u00b9 (Glu-\u03b2NA at pH 6.0, where no pyroglutamate formed over 4 weeks) versus 1.2 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2077 s\u207b\u00b9 for Gln-\u03b2NA (glutaminyl/glutamate cyclase patent US 8,470,781, the same data as Seifert et al. 2009, *Biochemistry*); the Glu value is an upper bound, not a measured rate. It speeds up at low pH, where the Glu and Gln rates converge.\n  - Free glutamate converts to pyroglutamate in aqueous solution at \u226560 \u00b0C, especially at pH 2\u20133.5. Gayte-Sorbier et al. (\"Stability of Glutamic Acid and Monosodium Glutamate Under Model System Conditions\") found conversion to pyrrolidonecarboxylic acid \"favored by pH values ranging from 2\u20133, by temperature increase and by oxygen\"; a rate constant k \u2248 0.0066 h\u207b\u00b9 at pH 3 and 75 \u00b0C (half-life of about 4\u20135 days) is attributed to this work only in a secondary citation.\n  - In the solid state, glutamic acid cyclizes above about 140 \u00b0C, with polymer (polyglutamic acid) formation on further heating (Wu et al. 2010, *Crystal Growth & Design*).\\[62\\]\n  - DSC/TGA shows decomposition at T_peak = 200 \u00b0C, \u0394H = 88 kJ/mol (endothermic), mainly by water loss to pyroglutamic acid (Weiss et al. 2018, *BMC Biophysics*).\\[63\\]\n  - Pyroglutamic acid racemizes when heated above its melting point (162 \u00b0C).\\[64\\]\n- **Hydrothermal decomposition.** Lee et al. (2014, *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta*) ran flow-through experiments at 150\u2013250 \u00b0C and initial pH 6 and 10, with 3\u201336 min residence. Glutamate cyclized and decomposed to succinate, formate, CO\u2082 and NH\u2083. Adding 13 mM H\u2082(aq) suppressed decomposition products at all temperatures and pH values tested, so reducing vent fluids partly mitigate losses.\\[65\\]\n- **Implications for wet\u2013dry and evaporitic settings.** Drying and heating favour pyroglutamate and polyglutamate formation over free Glu. Pyroglutamate rehydrolyses only under fairly harsh conditions. Meteoritic glutamate is partly stored as pyroglutamate: Pizzarello & Cooper treat pyroglutamic acid as an acid-labile precursor that gives Glu on hydrolysis.\\[66\\] Network designs should treat pyroglutamate as a reservoir, not purely a loss.\n- **Racemization.** Every abiotic route gives racemic Glu, as seen in Bennu and in controlled syntheses.\\[8\\] Apparent L-excesses in meteorites are at least partly contamination (Glavin et al. 2021).\\[57\\]\n\n### 6. Glutamate as an \"early\" amino acid, and congruence with biochemistry\n\n- **Thermodynamic and abundance argument.** Higgs & Pudritz (2009, *Astrobiology* 9:483) ranked amino acids across meteorites, discharge, hydrothermal, ice, shock and cyanide data sets. Glu falls in the \"early ten\" (G, A, D, E, V, S, I, L, P, T). Rank correlates with \u0394G_surf (r = 0.96); early amino acids average 169.3 \u00b1 42.0 kJ/mol versus 285.0 \u00b1 94.1 kJ/mol for late ones. Relative concentration falls off as C_rel = 15.8 exp(\u2212\u0394G_surf/31.3).\\[1\\]\n- **Critiques of that argument.** The authors note that the exponential may reflect kinetics rather than equilibrium, and that differential stability could bias meteorite ratios. In their own data, hydrothermal \u0394G shows no correlation with abundance.\\[1\\] Glu's early status rests heavily on discharge and meteorite data, where it is consistently minor and sometimes contaminated.\n- **Genetic-code coevolution.** In Wong's coevolution theory (Wong 1975, 2005), Glu is a precursor-amino acid from which Gln, Pro and Arg biosynthetically derive.\\[1\\] That fits with Glu's precursor role, and conveniently, the cyanosulfidic network delivers Gln/Glu and Pro/Arg precursors from related nitriles.\\[25\\]\n- **Congruence with extant biosynthesis.** In biology, nitrogen enters through glutamate dehydrogenase (\u03b1-KG + NH\u2084\u207a + NAD(P)H) or GS/GOGAT, then spreads by PLP-dependent transamination using Glu as donor.\\[38\\]\\[47\\]\n  - \u03b1-KG reductive-amination and transamination routes are directly congruent.\n  - Strecker/cyanosulfidic routes are not, and need a later \"switch.\" Bucherer\u2013Bergs on \u03b1-ketoacids is proposed as a bridge (Pulletikurti et al. 2022).\\[21\\]\n  - Mayer & Moran (2022) caution that \u03b1-KG is intrinsically the *slowest* ketoacid to aminate, so biology's \u03b1-KG-centred choice is not simply inherited from nonenzymatic preference.\\[46\\]\n  - Kitadai et al. (2019) observed preferential Glu formation over FeS, which partly runs against that.\\[41\\]\n\n## Recommendations (for synthesis-network design and pathway evaluation)\n1. **Model two parallel feeder modules** rather than one route:\n   - (i) a nitrile module (HCN + C4 aldehyde/nitrile \u2192 aminonitrile \u2192 Glu/Gln);\n   - (ii) an \u03b1-ketoacid module (glyoxylate + pyruvate \u2192 \u03b1-KG \u2192 Glu).\n\n   Treat their interconversion as hydantoin (Bucherer\u2013Bergs) chemistry or transamination.\n2. **Make C4 precursor supply an explicit, rate-limiting node.** Track 3-cyanopropanal/succinic semialdehyde or \u03b1-KG flux. Benchmark \u03b1-KG at \u22645% from glyoxylate/pyruvate (Stubbs et al. 2020). Treat the acrolein \u2192 Glu step as hypothetical until yields are measured.\n3. **Use measured amination parameters:**\n   - Ni/Pd/meteorite + H\u2082 at 22 \u00b0C, pH 8, 6\u2013150 mM NH\u2083, 72 h (6\u201388% combined; roughly 1:1 amino:hydroxy acid on Ni);\n   - FeS at 50\u201375 \u00b0C (up to ~50%);\n   - electro-reduced FeS (>50%, Glu favoured).\n\n   Include the competing \u03b1-hydroxyglutarate channel.\n4. **Include pyroglutamate as a reversible sink.** Use the limiting rate constant k \u2264 1.0 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2079 s\u207b\u00b9 near neutral and ambient temperature (pH 6.0; patent US 8,470,781 / Seifert et al.), and much faster at acidic pH and above 60 \u00b0C. Include irreversible thermal loss above ~150\u2013200 \u00b0C, mitigated by H\u2082 (Lee et al. 2014).\n5. **Flag reagent-plausibility issues on every edge:** hydroxylamine, Fe\u2070, Pd, BH\u2083CN\u207b, DAP, 254 nm UV, high HCN, and impact-derived sulfide.\n6. **Prioritize experiments that close the gap.** The single most diagnostic missing result is glutamate formation *in situ* in a one-pot \u03b1-KG-forming network. Kaur et al. 2024 did not detect it.\n\n## Caveats\n- Several quantitative values could not be checked against primary full text:\n  - per-step yields for the Patel et al. 2015 glutamate branch (in the SI);\n  - Glu/Gly ratios in Johnson et al. 2008 and Parker et al. 2011 (figures/tables only);\n  - the exact Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user 2003 Glu yield (reported secondarily as \"up to ~50%\"; Kitadai et al. 2019 characterize as-prepared FeS as promoting this amination only \"slightly\");\n  - the 19% glutamate figure for the Bucherer\u2013Bergs route (from the preprint version).\n- The N-terminal cyclization rate (a limiting value of 1.0 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2079 s\u207b\u00b9) is an upper bound for peptide-bound Glu, not free glutamate. The pH 3, 75 \u00b0C rate constant comes from food-chemistry model systems and is attributed to Gayte-Sorbier et al. only via a secondary citation.\n- The proposed origin of 4-oxobutanenitrile in interstellar sources is based on a reported search, not a confirmed detection.\n- The atmospheric-composition, hydrothermal-plausibility and cyanide-vs-metabolism debates are unresolved. This report presents the evidence as published and does not adjudicate a winner among frameworks.\n\n## Sources\n\n1. [A thermodynamic basis for prebiotic amino acid synthesis and the nature